WN.lb(5,{"mt_ukLvUD8DFA":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is an inference?","choices":["Working something out from clues in the text","A wild guess with no reason","Copying a sentence from the text"],"answer":0,"hint":"How did you know Mia was angry?","explain":"An inference uses clues, like slammed and threw, to work out what is not written."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"\"Nina's eyes filled with tears as the bus drove away with her best friend on it.\" How does Nina feel?","choices":["Sad","Bored","Proud"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do tears and a friend leaving tell you?","explain":"Tears as her best friend leaves are the clue that Nina is sad."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You say Max is scared. Which is the best way to back it up?","choices":["Quote: \"Max froze and gripped the rail.\"","Say: \"I just think he is scared.\"","Say: \"Max is nine years old.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Evidence points to the text, not just your opinion.","explain":"Quoting froze and gripped the rail proves the inference. An opinion or an unrelated fact does not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"First you think Oscar is rude for not saying hi. Then you read he had headphones on. What now?","choices":["Change the idea: he did not hear","Keep thinking he is rude","Ignore the headphones"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does your first idea still fit every clue?","explain":"The headphones clue does not fit rude, so a good reader changes the inference."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"\"'Get out!' she shouted.\" Which feeling fits best?","choices":["Angry","Calm","Sleepy"],"answer":0,"hint":"How does the speech verb say the words are spoken?","explain":"Get out spoken with shouted shows anger."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"\"Jay gave his lunch to the new boy, who had none.\" What is Jay's motive?","choices":["He wants to be kind and help","He hates his lunch","He wants to get in trouble"],"answer":0,"hint":"A motive is the reason. Why give food to someone with none?","explain":"Giving lunch to a boy who had none shows a kind motive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"\"Priya practised her lines {n} times and couldn't eat breakfast.\" What can you infer?","choices":["She is nervous about a performance","She does not like breakfast","She has forgotten her lines"],"answer":0,"hint":"Why practise {n} times and skip breakfast?","explain":"Practising {n} times and not eating are clues that she is nervous about performing.","params":{"n":[5,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"\"Dan grinned when he saw {n} presents with his name on.\" Which part is inferred?","choices":["Dan is happy","Dan saw the presents","There were {n} presents"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is not written but worked out from grinned?","explain":"The text says he grinned and saw {n} presents. That he is happy is worked out from the grin.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"\"Kim kicked a stone and stared at the ground.\" How might Kim feel?","choices":["Upset or fed up","Excited","We can not tell at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do kicking a stone and staring at the ground show?","explain":"Kicking a stone and staring down are clues to being upset. The text shows it without saying it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"\"Zara checked the clock {n} times before the bell.\" What can you infer?","choices":["She is eager for the bell to ring","She has a new clock","She does not care about time"],"answer":0,"hint":"Why would someone keep checking the time?","explain":"Checking the clock {n} times is the clue: she is waiting eagerly, maybe impatient, for the bell.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"\"Ali's face went red. He hid the broken vase behind his back.\" Which is stated, not inferred?","choices":["Ali hid the vase","Ali feels guilty","Ali broke the vase"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is written in the words?","explain":"He hid the vase is in the text. Guilty and broke it are worked out from the red face and hiding."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ruby waited {n} minutes in the rain for her friend, who never came. She sighed and walked home. Best evidence she is disappointed?","choices":["She sighed and walked home","It was raining","She waited {n} minutes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Evidence of a feeling is something Ruby does that shows it.","explain":"Sighing and walking home shows how Ruby feels. Rain and the {n} minutes set the scene but do not show her feeling on their own.","params":{"n":[10,30]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A story might never say Mia is angry. It might say she slammed the door and threw her bag. Those actions are clues. When you use clues to work something out, you are making an inference.","Some things the text says. His hands shook is right there in the words. Other things you work out. Sam is nervous is not written, but the shaking hands show it.","An inference needs proof. Point to what the character does or says. Leo is Ben's brother is true, but it does not show jealousy. He muttered, mine is better anyway, does.","A motive is the reason someone acts. Read on and test your idea. If a new clue does not fit, change your idea. Words like snapped, gasped and sighed show feelings too."],"adult":["Inference means working out what the text does not state, using clues it does give. For characters, the clues are actions, dialogue, how lines are spoken, and how others react. Readers aged 7 to 10 are expected to infer feelings, thoughts and motives and to justify each inference with evidence from the text.","The lab separates the skills. Learners tap action words that reveal a feeling, sort statements into what the text says and what we work out, build a because with two real pieces of evidence, revise a motive one clue at a time, and match speech verbs such as snapped and sighed to feelings.","Common mistakes: refusing to infer because it is not written; treating a wild guess as an inference; citing a true but irrelevant fact as evidence; restating the claim (I think she is nervous) instead of quoting; fixing on a first idea and ignoring later clues; and mixing up what is stated with what is inferred.","When reading together, ask how a character feels, then ask what in the text makes you think so. Expect a quote or an action, not just an opinion."]},"familyTip":"Pause a story or a TV scene and ask, \"How is that character feeling? What did they do or say that tells you?\" Then ask, \"Did anything later change your mind?\"","keyIdea":"Writers often show a feeling instead of naming it. You work it out from what a character does and says, and how they say it. That is an inference. A good inference fits every clue, and you can point to the exact words that prove it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"\"Ella's voice wobbled. She held her notes tight.\" Is each card in the text, or worked out? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["The text says it","We work it out"],"items":[["Her voice wobbled",0],["She held her notes tight",0],["Ella is nervous",1],["Ella is about to speak to a group",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaginfer","caption":"Flagship lab: tap the actions that give a feeling away, sort said from worked out, prove it with evidence, revise a motive clue by clue, read how lines are said","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_mLPEMpYb_R":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Story: The cat sat on the roof and could not get down. Where was the cat?","choices":["On the roof","In a basket","Up my tree"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen for the place word in the story.","explain":"The story says the cat sat on the roof. A basket or your tree are not in the story."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Story: Leo and his mum went to the zoo. They saw {n} lions asleep in the sun. How many lions did they see?","choices":["{n}","{=n+2}","Lots"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen for the number the story gave, not a guess.","explain":"The story said {n} lions, so the answer is {n}. Lots is too fuzzy, and {=n+2} was never said.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The reader says a word you have never heard. What should you do?","choices":["Ask what the word means","Pretend you know it","Stop listening"],"answer":0,"hint":"How can you find out what a new word means?","explain":"Asking what a word means helps the rest of the story make sense. Pretending leaves you lost."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Story: Pip's boat had a hole, so it began to sink. What went wrong?","choices":["The boat had a hole","Pip had a boat","Boats are fun"],"answer":0,"hint":"A problem is something that goes wrong. What was bad?","explain":"The hole is the problem, because it made the boat sink. Having a boat is just a detail."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Story: Sita baked {n} buns, and then she ate one. How many buns did she bake?","choices":["{n}","{=n-1}","One"],"answer":0,"hint":"The question asks how many she baked, before she ate any.","explain":"She baked {n} buns. {=n-1} is how many were left after she ate one, which is a different question.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Fact: Bees make honey in a hive. Your friend says bees live in shoes. Where do bees make honey?","choices":["In a hive","In shoes","In a shop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use what the fact said, not what your friend said.","explain":"The fact says bees make honey in a hive. Your friend's idea is not in the text."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Story: First the egg cracked. Next a chick came out. Last it said cheep. What happened first?","choices":["The egg cracked","It said cheep","A chick came out"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part did the story say first?","explain":"The egg cracked first. The chick came out next, and it said cheep last."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Story: Nell wore her boots because it was snowing. Why did Nell wear boots?","choices":["It was snowing","Her boots are pink","She likes dogs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen for the word because. What comes after it?","explain":"The story says because it was snowing. The word because tells you the reason."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Story: Tom's dog ran away in the rain. Tom looked everywhere and found it under a bench. What went wrong?","choices":["The dog ran away","It was a dog","Tom found it"],"answer":0,"hint":"What went wrong means the problem. What was bad in the story?","explain":"The problem is that the dog ran away. Finding it under the bench is how the problem got fixed."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Story: Zara picked {n} red apples. Question: How many apples did Zara pick?","choices":["{n}","{=n+1}","I like apples"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen for the number in the story, not a number you like.","explain":"The story said Zara picked {n} apples, so the answer is {n}. The answer comes from the words you heard.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Story: First Raj got wet in the rain. Next he dried off. Last he had hot soup. What happened next?","choices":["He dried off","He had hot soup","He got wet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the part that came after getting wet and before the soup.","explain":"Raj got wet first, then dried off, then had soup. Dried off is the middle part, so it came next."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Story: Kim had {n} toy cars. She gave one to her friend. Your friend has lots of cars. How many cars did Kim have at the start?","choices":["{n}","Lots","One"],"answer":0,"hint":"The question asks about Kim in the story, not about your friend.","explain":"Kim had {n} cars at the start. Your friend's cars are not in the story, and one is the car she gave away.","params":{"n":[3,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["When a grown-up reads to you, you do not have to read. You just listen. Your ears do the work.","Listen for three things. Who is in it? Where are they? What goes wrong? Mia's kite got stuck in a tree. That is what went wrong.","Answers come from the story. The kite in the story was red. Your kite may be blue, but the answer is still red.","Can you tell it back? Say what came first, next and last. Is a word new? Ask what it means. Then the rest makes sense."],"adult":["This topic is about understanding books that are read to a child, often books harder than the child could read alone. Listening comprehension runs ahead of reading for years, so read-alouds are where children meet richer stories, facts and words. Children show they understood by answering and asking questions about key details, retelling events, and asking when something is unclear.","The lab reads a short story one line at a time, with a picture for each line and an optional spoken voice. The child then answers who, where and what went wrong, taps the exact line that holds a detail, puts three events in order, and finds a new word in a fact text about owls, asks what it means, and uses the answer.","Common mistakes: answering from their own life instead of the text, such as saying blue because their own kite is blue; naming a true detail, like the colour of the kite, when asked for the problem; inventing a character who was never there; retelling events out of order; and nodding along past a word they did not know.","When you read together, pause once or twice to ask who, where or what went wrong. If your child answers from their own life, ask, what did the book say? Point to the page together. Invite them to stop you when a word is new, and praise them when they do."]},"familyTip":"After a bedtime story, ask your child to tell it back using first, next and last. Then ask one question whose answer is in the book, such as where did they go, and find the page together to check.","keyIdea":"When someone reads to you, listen for who is in it, where it happens and what goes wrong. Your answers come from the words you heard, not from your own life. You can tell the story back in order. If a word or part is not clear, ask the reader.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does the answer come from the story? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["From the story","Not from the story"],"items":[["Mia had a red kite",0],["Grandpa got a ladder",0],["The kite got stuck",0],["My kite is blue",1],["A pirate came",1],["I went to the beach",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagaloud","caption":"Flagship lab: hear a story line by line, answer who, where and what went wrong, find answers in the text, retell it in order and ask about a new word.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_dmFnJzxKwz":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Claim: Sam is excited about the trip. Which evidence is strongest?","choices":["“Sam bounced on her toes, grinning at the coach.”","“The coach was late.”","“Sam wore a green coat.”"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which quotation shows Sam herself, and fits excitement?","explain":"Bouncing and grinning show Sam herself and suggest excitement. The late coach is setting, and the coat is irrelevant."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which explanation analyses the quotation “he crept down the stairs”?","choices":["“Crept” suggests he does not want to be heard.","This shows that he crept down the stairs.","This shows that he is sneaky."],"answer":0,"hint":"One retells, one repeats a claim, one zooms in on a word. Which zooms in?","explain":"Analysis names the word and what it suggests. The others retell the quotation or repeat the claim."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The text says “Kai’s hands were shaking.” Is “Kai is afraid” stated or inferred?","choices":["Inferred: the shaking is a clue","Stated: the text says so","Neither: we can not use it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the text actually use the word afraid?","explain":"The text states the shaking hands. Fear is an inference from that clue, and it is a fair one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why is “Zara read every book in the library twice” strong evidence that she loves reading?","choices":["It shows a habit kept up over time","Because it is a long quotation","Because it mentions books"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does every book, twice, tell you about how often?","explain":"It shows a repeated choice over a long time, which is what loving reading looks like. Length and topic words do not make it strong."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ellie “gave away {n} of her own books”. Which sentence embeds this correctly?","choices":["Ellie “gave away {n} of her own books”, which shows her generosity.","Ellie is generous. “She gave away {n} of her own books.”","Ellie is generous “books”."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one reads as a single smooth sentence?","explain":"The first fits the quotation into its own sentence. The second is dropped in, and the third breaks the grammar.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Claim: Dev is hungry. Which quotation is best trimmed?","choices":["“his stomach growled”","the whole page about the kitchen","“a”"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one keeps the proof and drops the rest?","explain":"His stomach growled is short and proves the point. A whole page is too long, and a proves nothing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Mr Hale “sighed {n} times” in the meeting. Which explanation analyses this?","choices":["Repeated sighing suggests his patience is running out.","This shows he sighed {n} times.","This shows he is impatient."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one says what the action suggests, not just what happened?","explain":"Saying that repeated sighing suggests patience running out is analysis. The others retell or restate.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Claim: Nia is brave. Which evidence does NOT support it?","choices":["“Nia had red hair.”","“Nia stepped forward when no one else would.”","“Her voice shook, but she kept speaking.”"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which line has nothing to do with bravery?","explain":"Red hair is true but irrelevant. Stepping forward and speaking through fear both show courage."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Claim: Priya is exhausted. Which evidence is strongest?","choices":["“Her eyelids drooped as she slumped over the desk.”","“The classroom clock ticked loudly.”","“Priya had a blue pencil case.”"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which quotation is about Priya herself and can only mean tiredness?","explain":"Drooping eyelids and slumping show tiredness in Priya herself. The clock is setting, and the pencil case is irrelevant."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ravi “checked his phone {n} times” during the film. Is this good evidence that he is bored?","choices":["Yes: a repeated action that shows it","No: it only mentions a phone","No: numbers can not be evidence"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the action happen once or again and again, and what does that suggest?","explain":"Checking a phone {n} times is a repeated action by Ravi, so it is strong evidence of boredom or distraction.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which sentence embeds the quotation correctly?","choices":["The sea “roared against the rocks”, which makes it sound dangerous.","The sea is dangerous. “The sea roared against the rocks all night.”","The sea is dangerous “roared”."],"answer":0,"hint":"Read each one aloud. Which still sounds like one proper sentence?","explain":"The first one fits the quotation into its own grammar. The second drops a whole line in, and the third breaks the sentence."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Your quotation is {n} words long, but only 4 of them prove your point. What should you do?","choices":["Cut it down to the words that prove it","Keep all {n} words","Add more words around it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a longer quotation stronger, or just harder to analyse?","explain":"Cut the {n} words down to the ones that do the work. Length does not make evidence stronger.","params":{"n":[10,16]}}],"explain":{"kid":["When you make a point about a text, you need proof. The proof is evidence: the exact words the writer used. Quote them, and the reader can check your idea for themselves.","Not all evidence is equal. Say your claim is that Maya is nervous. The hall was packed is weak, because it is about the room, not Maya. She gripped her script so hard that the pages crumpled is strong. It shows Maya herself, and it can only mean tension.","Keep quotations short. Pick only the words that do the work, then fit them into your own sentence: Maya gripped her script so hard that the pages crumpled. Do not just drop a whole line in after a full stop.","Then explain. Do not retell the quote, and do not just repeat your claim. Zoom in on a word. The verb gripped suggests force she cannot control, so her body gives her nerves away."],"adult":["This topic asks learners to back up points with exact words from a text. That covers what the text says and what we can work out from it. They also judge which quote best supports a point, and they explain the link. For ages 11 to 14, the goal moves from giving any quote to giving the one that fits best.","The lab splits the skill into parts. Learners match each claim to the line that proves it. They rate quotes as strong, weak or off the point. They cut a sentence down to the shortest quote that still works. They fit quotes into their own sentences. Last, they pick the explanation that looks closely at a word.","Common mistakes: picking a quote about the setting or a different person, not the one in the claim; picking a quote that is true but beside the point; thinking a long quote is a strong one; dropping a quote in as its own sentence; retelling the quote or repeating the claim instead of explaining it; and treating a guess as if the text said it.","Ask your learner why one quote is better than another. Good answers say whether it is about the right person, whether it could mean something else, and which single word carries the meaning."]},"familyTip":"When your child says something about a book, film or song, ask \"Which exact words make you think that?\" and then \"Is there a line that proves it even better?\"","keyIdea":"Back up every point about a text with exact words from it. Strong evidence is about the thing you are claiming and fits only one reading. Keep quotations short, build them into your own sentence, then explain which words prove your point and how.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Claim: Ben is jealous of his brother. How well does each quotation support it? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Strong","Weak","Does not support"],"items":[["“Ben glared at his brother’s new bike.”",0],["“Mine is better anyway,” Ben muttered.",0],["“Ben went quiet at dinner.”",1],["“Everyone admired the new bike.”",1],["“The bike was bright red.”",2],["“Ben was twelve.”",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagevid","caption":"Flagship lab: find the line that proves a claim, rate quotations, trim a quotation, embed it in your own sentence and explain it.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_NJu_l5_Xhp":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which lens asks how empire and colonised people are shown?","choices":["Postcolonial","Marxist","Ecocritical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which lens looks at empire, colonies and who tells the story?","explain":"Postcolonial critics ask how empire and colonised people are shown, as Edward Said did with Mansfield Park."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A critic says Elizabeth Bennet's family may lose their home because only a male heir can inherit it. Which lens is that?","choices":["Feminist","Ecocritical","Postcolonial"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is this point about gender, nature or empire?","explain":"Inheritance passing only to men is a point about gender, so it is a feminist reading."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Your reading of a novel is bold, but you cannot find any words in it that back it up. What should you do?","choices":["Change or drop it: a reading needs textual evidence","Keep it: lenses let you say anything","Say the author secretly meant it"],"answer":0,"hint":"What makes a reading strong rather than a guess?","explain":"A lens opens new questions, but the answers have to come from the words. With no evidence it is only a guess."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"“Mind-forg’d manacles” in London are chains the mind makes for itself. Which lens fits this reading best?","choices":["Psychoanalytic","Marxist","Ecocritical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which lens looks for hidden fears and the inner mind?","explain":"Chains made by the mind point to fears people have taken inside, which is the psychoanalytic question."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A reading quotes {n} phrases from the poem, and each is really there. A classmate objects that Blake did not intend it. Is that a strong objection?","choices":["No: a lens reading stands on textual evidence, not intent","Yes: only intent counts","Yes, unless there are more than {n} phrases"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a lens reading a claim about the author's plan?","explain":"With {n} real phrases as evidence, the reading stands. Intent is not the test: the words are.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"An essay spends {n} paragraphs explaining feminist theory, but quotes the novel only once. What is its main weakness?","choices":["Too little close reading of the text","Too few lenses","It should explain theory for even longer"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do examiners reward: theory or close reading?","explain":"{n} paragraphs of theory with one quotation means too little close reading. Use the lens to read the words.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What lifts a critical essay into the top band?","choices":["Weighing your reading against another one","Using every lens in one paragraph","Retelling the plot in detail"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the last move in a strong paragraph.","explain":"Top essays develop one reading, then weigh it against an alternative. Piling up lenses or retelling the plot does not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In a strong critical paragraph, what comes straight after your claim?","choices":["A quotation that backs it","An alternative reading","A summary of the plot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Claim, then what proves it, then analysis.","explain":"After the claim comes the quotation that backs it, then analysis of the words, then an alternative reading."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"{n} students read London through {n} different lenses, and each backs their reading with quotations. How many readings can be valid?","choices":["All {n}, if each is backed by the text","Only one of them","None, because only Blake knows"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a poem support more than one strong reading at the same time?","explain":"All {n} can be valid. A text can hold many strong readings, as long as each one is backed by words on the page.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Your essay has {n} quotations but never makes a claim through a lens. What is missing?","choices":["A clear claim about what the lens reveals","Even more quotations","A longer plot summary"],"answer":0,"hint":"Quotations are evidence. Evidence for what?","explain":"{n} quotations prove nothing without a claim. Start with what the lens shows you, then use the quotations to back it.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which question does a Marxist lens ask?","choices":["Who has money and power, and who does the work?","How is nature shown?","What hidden fears drive people?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Marxist critics focus on class, money and work.","explain":"A Marxist lens asks about money, power, class and work. Nature is the ecocritical question, and hidden fears the psychoanalytic one."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A critic says Blake's “charter’d Thames” shows a river turned into property. Which lens is that?","choices":["Ecocritical","Feminist","Psychoanalytic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which lens asks how people treat nature?","explain":"Ecocritics ask how nature is shown and used. A river being owned is a classic ecocritical point."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A classmate says your feminist reading of London is wrong because Blake was not a feminist. Best reply?","choices":["A lens reading is judged on the text, not on the author's views","You are right, so I will drop it","Every reading is right anyway"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a lens a claim about what the author planned?","explain":"A lens is a question you bring to the text. It is judged on the words that back it, not on what Blake believed."}],"explain":{"kid":["A critical lens is a set of questions you bring to a text. Each lens makes you notice different things. A Marxist lens asks who has money and power, and who does the work. A feminist lens asks how women and gender roles are shown. A postcolonial lens asks how empire and colonised people are shown.","An ecocritical lens asks how nature is shown and how people treat it. A psychoanalytic lens looks for hidden fears and wishes. Take Blake's poem London from 1794. A Marxist sees the soldier bleeding on the palace walls. An ecocritic sees that even the river is owned.","One text can hold many strong readings. But not anything goes. A good reading points to the words that back it. If no words back it, it is a guess.","A lens is not a claim about what the writer planned. Blake was not reading Marx. You are asking new questions of old words. The best essays weigh one reading against another."],"adult":["This topic introduces the main critical lenses used at A level and beyond: Marxist, feminist, postcolonial, ecocritical and psychoanalytic. Each is best taught as a set of questions the reader brings, which lets students produce several readings of one text.","The lab uses public-domain texts. Learners match each lens to its question, hunt for the phrase in Blake's London that backs each reading, sort readings of the first line of Pride and Prejudice by whether the words support them, name the lens a critic is using, and build a paragraph in order.","A common mistake is to think a lens reading claims the author's intention, so that a feminist reading of Blake is wrong because he was not a feminist. Another is the belief that there is one right reading, or the opposite, that any reading goes. In fact readings are judged on textual evidence.","Exam boards reward students who consider different interpretations. At home, pick a film you have both seen and ask your teen who has the power in it, and what the story says about women. Then ask which scene proves it."]},"familyTip":"Next time you watch a film together, ask one lens question, such as who has the money and power here, and then ask your teen which scene backs their answer.","keyIdea":"A critical lens is a set of questions you bring to a text: Marxist critics ask about money, power and work; feminist critics about gender; postcolonial critics about empire; ecocritics about nature; psychoanalytic critics about hidden fears and wishes. One text can support many strong readings, but a reading is only as good as the words that back it, and it is not a claim about what the author intended. Top essays weigh one reading against another.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each reading of Blake's London backed by the words? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Backed by the words","Not backed"],"items":[["The “Palace walls” show the rulers stained by the soldier's death",0],["The “charter’d Thames” shows nature owned for profit",0],["The “youthful Harlots curse” shows women harmed by poverty",0],["London is a happy poem about a holiday",1],["Blake secretly loved the king",1],["The poem is set in Paris",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglens","caption":"Flagship lab: match lenses to their questions, find evidence in Blake's London, test readings of Austen and build a critical paragraph.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Hqz5y_tWz2":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is the best description of a climax?","choices":["The turning point, where tension is highest","The last page of the story","The most exciting description"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the top of the story mountain.","explain":"The climax is the turning point at peak tension. It usually comes near the end, but the resolution follows it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Maya rows alone and is beaten back by the waves. Next she asks the crew for help. Why does she ask?","choices":["Rowing alone has failed","It is spring","She always liked the crew"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the event just before her choice.","explain":"Her failure alone forces the choice. One event causes the next. That is what makes her change believable."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A student writes: 'Tom changes a lot.' What would make this a strong point?","choices":["Naming the event that changes him and quoting evidence","Saying it again in longer words","Describing the setting"],"answer":0,"hint":"A claim about change needs a cause and proof.","explain":"A strong point names the event that pushes Tom to change and quotes what he does before and after."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In a mystery, every clue makes the detective more worried and the danger closer. Which stage is this?","choices":["Rising action","Exposition","Falling action"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the tension going up or down?","explain":"The tension keeps climbing, so this is rising action. Falling action comes after the climax, when tension drops."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Maya's tension line reads 1, 3, 5, {n}, 4, 2. Which event is the turning point?","choices":["Event 4","Event 6","Event 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the highest point on the line.","explain":"Event 4 has tension {n}, the highest score, so it is the turning point. The events after it fall back to 4 and 2.","params":{"n":[7,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A story has {n} chapters. The climax is in chapter {=n-1}. Which chapter most likely holds the resolution?","choices":["Chapter {n}","Chapter 1","Chapter {=n-2}"],"answer":0,"hint":"The resolution comes after the climax, at the end.","explain":"The resolution is the new normal after the climax, so it is in chapter {n}, the last one. Chapter {=n-2} comes before the climax.","params":{"n":[6,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A villain is cruel on page 1 and cruel on the last page, with no change. What can you say?","choices":["The villain has no arc","The villain is the setting","The story has no plot"],"answer":0,"hint":"An arc is the change a character goes through. Compare the start and the end.","explain":"With no change, the villain has no arc. That can be a choice. Often only the main character changes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A story about a lost child is set in a busy market on a festival day. How might the setting shape the plot?","choices":["The crowds make the child harder to find","It has no effect at all","It tells us the ending"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does the place make easier or harder?","explain":"The crowd makes the search harder and raises the tension. The setting drives events instead of sitting in the background."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In a story, a boy finds a map in his grandad's attic. This starts the whole adventure. Which stage is this?","choices":["Inciting incident","Climax","Resolution"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which stage starts the problem or the quest?","explain":"Finding the map sets the story moving, so it is the inciting incident. The climax comes much later, at peak tension."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A novel's climax is in chapter {n}. The resolution is in the last chapter. Where is the falling action?","choices":["After chapter {n}, before the last chapter","Before chapter {n}","In chapter 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Falling action is what happens as a result of the climax.","explain":"Falling action follows the climax, so it starts in chapter {=n+1} and runs until the resolution. Before chapter {n} is rising action.","params":{"n":[8,14]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Ana is shy at the start. At the end she gives a speech to the whole town. What has the writer shown?","choices":["A character arc","A setting","An exposition"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do we call the way a character changes over a story?","explain":"Ana changes from shy to confident, so the writer has shown a character arc. To prove it, find the event that made her change."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A reader scores the tension of five events: 2, 4, {n}, 9, 3. Which event is the climax?","choices":["Event 4","Event 3","Event 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"The climax is where tension peaks.","explain":"Event 4 scores 9, the highest, so it is the climax. Event 3 scores {n}, which is less than 9, so it is still rising action.","params":{"n":[5,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A story is set on a ship in a fog. The fog hides the rocks, and the ship hits them. What role does the setting play?","choices":["It causes the problem","It is only decoration","It is the resolution"],"answer":0,"hint":"Would the crash happen on a clear day?","explain":"The fog hides the rocks, so the setting causes the crash. Without the fog, this plot falls apart."}],"explain":{"kid":["Most stories have a shape like a mountain. The start sets up who, where and when. Then something goes wrong. That is the inciting incident. Things get harder, and the tension climbs.","The top of the mountain is the climax. It is the turning point, where the tension is highest. It is not the last page. After it, the results play out, and the story settles into a new normal.","Events do not just happen one after another. Each one pushes the next. Ask why a character makes a choice. The answer is often the event just before it.","Good characters change. Maya starts out saying she needs no one. By the end she leads a crew. That change is her arc. Look for the moment she turns, and the proof in what she does. The setting matters too. Take away the storm and her whole story falls apart."],"adult":["This topic asks learners to explain how a plot unfolds through key events and how characters respond and change. They should also link character, setting and plot. At ages 11 to 14 the aim is analysis. Learners should not just retell the story. They should say why each event matters and point to evidence.","The lab uses one short original story. Learners deal event cards onto a story mountain. They shape a tension line so the climax is the peak. They plot the main character's arc from quoted evidence, and link each key event to its cause. Last, they swap the setting and see which events can no longer happen.","Common mistakes: putting the climax on the last page; calling every exciting moment the climax; retelling events as a list with no cause; saying a character changed with no event or evidence to show it; and treating the setting as decoration when it drives the problem.","When your child reads or watches a story, ask three questions. Where is the turning point? How is the main character different at the end, and what made them change? Would the story still work in a different place or time?"]},"familyTip":"After a film or a book, sketch a quick story mountain together on paper. Mark the turning point. Then ask: how did the main character change, and which event made it happen?","keyIdea":"A plot is a chain of events with a shape. Tension rises to a turning point, the climax, then falls to a new normal. Along the way the main character is pushed to change, and that change is the character arc. The setting is not just a backdrop. It can cause the problem and force the choices.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Before or after the climax? Drag each event, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Before the climax","After the climax"],"items":[["The storm warning comes",0],["Maya is beaten back by waves",0],["Maya rows alone and proud",0],["They tow the boat home",1],["Maya joins the crew",1],["The town cheers them in",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagplot","caption":"Flagship lab: build a story mountain, peak the tension, plot a character arc, link causes, swap the setting.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_-n7sDQHHYF":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In 1606, who did most people believe had chosen the king?","choices":["God","The people, by voting","The richest nobles"],"answer":0,"hint":"This belief is called the divine right of kings.","explain":"Under the divine right of kings, God chose the monarch. King James wrote that kings answer to God alone, so a murdered king was an attack on God's order."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The witches chant, “Fair is foul, and foul is fair.” What does it mean?","choices":["Things that look good may be evil, and evil may look good","The weather is bad today","Fair people are always foul"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fair can mean good or lovely. Foul means evil or ugly.","explain":"The witches warn that nothing is what it seems. That idea runs through the play: Macbeth looks loyal while planning murder."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In Act 4, the witches show Macbeth a line of eight kings who come from Banquo. Why might this please the first audience?","choices":["King James was said to descend from Banquo","Banquo was Shakespeare's father","Audiences liked long parades"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who was the king in 1606, and whose family was he said to come from?","explain":"King James was said to come from Banquo's family, so the scene flatters him. It also explains why Banquo is shown as loyal and good."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Lady Macbeth asks spirits to “unsex me here.” Which belief of the time is she going against?","choices":["Women should be gentle and obey their husbands","Women should rule the country","Women should fight in wars"],"answer":0,"hint":"She asks to lose something her time expected of women. What was it?","explain":"Women were expected to be gentle and obedient. She asks to lose that softness so she can plan a murder, which would have shocked the audience."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A student quotes {n} lines of a speech in a row and writes nothing about them. What is missing?","choices":["A paraphrase and a comment on what the words show","More lines of quotation","The date the play was written"],"answer":0,"hint":"Quotes are evidence. What must you do with evidence?","explain":"Long quotes on their own prove nothing. Quote a few words, say what they mean in your own words, then explain what they show about the character or theme.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The Porter jokes about an “equivocator” at the gate of hell. Why would that land in 1606?","choices":["A priest linked to the Gunpowder Plot had just defended equivocation at his trial","Equivocators were a type of soldier","It was a popular song"],"answer":0,"hint":"To equivocate is to twist words to avoid the truth. Who was in the news for it?","explain":"In 1606 a priest, Henry Garnet, was tried for his part in the Gunpowder Plot and defended equivocation. The joke ties Macbeth's lies to a real treason against the king."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Early on, Macbeth is called “brave Macbeth.” At the end, he is “this dead butcher.” What does this show?","choices":["He changes from a hero to a tyrant","He was always a butcher","Nothing about him changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the two names. What happened in between?","explain":"The names bookend his change: from a loyal, brave soldier to a murderous tyrant. Quoting the start and the end is how to prove a character changes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why did the witches frighten audiences in 1606 more than most people today?","choices":["Many, King James included, believed witches were real and served the devil","Witches were a new idea in 1606","The actors wore scary masks"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did King James write a book about in 1597?","explain":"King James wrote Daemonologie, a book about witches, and witch trials were real. To the first audience, the witches were a real danger, not fantasy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A messenger says, “I shall be with thee ere {n} days are past.” What does he promise?","choices":["I will be with you before {n} days have passed.","I will be with you after {n} days.","Where will you be in {n} days?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Swap thee for you and ere for before.","explain":"Thee is you and ere is before, so he will arrive within {n} days, not after them. Reading ere as after reverses the promise.","params":{"n":[2,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Juliet says, “Wherefore art thou Romeo?” What is she asking?","choices":["Why are you Romeo, a Montague?","Where are you, Romeo?","Who are you, Romeo?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Wherefore is an old word. Is it where, why or who?","explain":"Wherefore means why. Juliet knows where Romeo is not the point: she wishes he were not from the family her own family hates."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A king says, “Thou hast but {n} days to live.” What does this mean in modern English?","choices":["You have only {n} days to live.","Where are your {n} days to live?","They have {n} days to live."],"answer":0,"hint":"Swap thou for you and hast for have. What does but mean here?","explain":"Thou is you and hast is have. But here means only, so the line is: you have only {n} days to live.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"After Duncan's murder, his horses turn wild and eat each other. Why does Shakespeare include this?","choices":["Nature itself is thrown into disorder by killing a king","To add a scene about animals","To show the horses were hungry"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the Great Chain of Being. What happens when one link breaks?","explain":"A king was believed to be chosen by God. Killing him breaks the chain, so nature turns upside down: animals attack their betters and day becomes night."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Lady Macbeth says, “Ere {n} o’clock, the deed is done.” What does ere mean?","choices":["Before","After","Where"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ere is an old word for time. Try each choice in the sentence.","explain":"Ere means before. The line means: before {n} o’clock, the deed is done.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which sentence uses context best?","choices":["People in 1606 believed God chose kings, so Duncan's murder is a crime against heaven.","Shakespeare was born in 1564.","Macbeth is a play with five acts."],"answer":0,"hint":"Good context explains something in the play. Which one does?","explain":"Only the first links a belief of the time to an event in the play. A birth date or the number of acts explains nothing about what the play means."}],"explain":{"kid":["Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in about 1606. His English is early modern English. Most of the words are ours. A few small ones change the meaning, so learn them first. Thou means you. Art means are. Hath means has. Wherefore means why, not where.","To paraphrase, put a line into your own words. Keep the meaning, not the picture. When Macbeth says, “Stars, hide your fires,” he does not want the stars to die. He wants it dark, so no one sees the murder he is planning.","Now read it like his first audience. Many people then believed God ranked all things in a chain. God was at the top, then angels, then the king, then nobles, then everyone else, then animals. So killing a king was a crime against God. After Duncan dies, the play shows nature going wrong. Day stays dark, an owl kills a falcon, and horses eat each other.","The king who watched this play was James the First. He wrote a book about witches and believed they were real. He was also said to come from the family of Banquo. So the witches were truly scary to that audience, and Banquo is shown as good. Use context like this to explain a line, not as a fact stuck on the end."],"adult":["This topic asks learners to read a Shakespeare play in depth. They follow plot, character and theme through early modern English. They also link ideas to what people in the time of Elizabeth and James believed about kings, women, fate and order. The lab uses Macbeth, the most widely taught play at this stage.","In the lab, learners match old words to their meanings and fix paraphrases of a key speech. They rebuild the Great Chain of Being and find the signs of nature in disorder after the murder. Then they link details of the play to events of 1606, such as King James's book on witches and the trial of a priest tied to the Gunpowder Plot.","Common mistakes: reading wherefore as where; paraphrasing word by word, so the images are taken literally; judging the play only by modern values; and adding context that explains nothing, such as Shakespeare's birth date. Another slip is to say a character changes without quoting the start and the end.","Ask your teenager to paraphrase one famous speech aloud, line by line. Then ask how the first audience would have felt about it, and why. A strong answer links a belief of the time to particular words in the play."]},"familyTip":"Pick a famous line, such as “Fair is foul, and foul is fair.” Take turns putting it into everyday words, then ask: why would this have scared people who believed witches were real?","keyIdea":"Shakespeare's English is mostly our English, with a few old words to swap, such as thou for you and wherefore for why. Paraphrase a speech to find what the character means, not just the picture the words paint. Then read it the way a 1606 audience would: they believed God chose the king and that witches were real, so killing King Duncan breaks the whole order of nature.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does this context explain the play? Drag each fact, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Explains the play","Bolted on"],"items":[["People believed God chose kings",0],["King James feared witches",0],["James was said to descend from Banquo",0],["Shakespeare was born in 1564",1],["The Globe was round",1],["Shakespeare had three children",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbard","caption":"Flagship lab: decode old words, paraphrase Macbeth's speech, rebuild the Great Chain of Being, spot nature in disorder and link the play to 1606.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_x2sWtfTeYT":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"“Tom stared at the sea. He wondered if his dad would come. Behind him, Mum quietly hoped he would not.” Which perspective?","choices":["Omniscient","Third person limited","First person"],"answer":0,"hint":"Whose thoughts do we hear: Tom's only, or Mum's too?","explain":"We hear Tom's wondering and Mum's secret hope. Entering more than one mind is the mark of an omniscient narrator."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A story uses she on every one of its {n} pages. What can you say for sure?","choices":["It is third person, limited or omniscient","It is omniscient","The narrator is a girl"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the pronoun tell you how many minds we enter?","explain":"She tells us it is third person. To decide limited or omniscient, check whose thoughts we get: one mind or many.","params":{"n":[120,400]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The reader knows the bridge is down. The hero races toward it at night. What is this called?","choices":["Dramatic irony","A plot twist","An unreliable narrator"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the reader already know, or find out later?","explain":"We already know about the danger and the hero does not. That is dramatic irony. A twist would surprise the reader later."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A narrator says, “I am perfectly calm,” then screams at a ticking clock. Why does this matter?","choices":["His actions show we cannot trust his words","It proves he really is calm","It shows the author is angry"],"answer":0,"hint":"Test what he says against what he does.","explain":"His behaviour contradicts his claim, which marks him as unreliable. Poe uses this in The Tell-Tale Heart, where the narrator insists he is sane."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A first-person narrator is cruel to everyone she meets for {n} chapters. Is the author cruel?","choices":["No, the narrator is a voice the author made up","Yes, the author is speaking","Yes, if the book won prizes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the narrator the same person as the writer?","explain":"Authors invent narrators, including nasty or foolish ones, often so readers judge them. Treat the narrator as a character, not the writer.","params":{"n":[3,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The same fight is told once by Kai and once by Rosa. Why do the two versions differ?","choices":["Each knows and feels different things","One of them must be the author","Fights can only be told in third person"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can each character see and think?","explain":"Each teller only knows their own thoughts and what they saw, and each wants to look good. Comparing two tellings shows how perspective shapes the story."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A narrator in a comedy brags, “Nobody noticed my mistake,” while {n} people point and laugh. What effect does this create?","choices":["Humour, because we see what he cannot","Suspense about a mystery","Sadness, because he is honest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who knows the truth: the reader or the narrator?","explain":"The gap between his boast and what we see makes the scene funny. Unreliable narrators often create humour and irony this way.","params":{"n":[4,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which question best tells third limited from omniscient?","choices":["Whose thoughts does the narrator reveal?","Does the narrator say he or she?","Is the story set in the past?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both use he or she. What else differs?","explain":"Both kinds use he or she. Limited reveals one mind, while omniscient can reveal many, so ask whose thoughts we get."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A murder story is told by a narrator who leaves out one key night. Why should readers notice?","choices":["Gaps can be a clue that the narrator is hiding something","Gaps mean the author made an error","Gaps prove the narrator is omniscient"],"answer":0,"hint":"Unreliable narrators can mislead by what they skip.","explain":"Leaving things out is a classic sign of an unreliable narrator. Agatha Christie's The Murder of Roger Ackroyd hides its twist in a gap like this."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"“I opened the door and froze. Someone had been in my room.” Which perspective is this?","choices":["First person","Third person limited","Omniscient"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the narrator's pronoun. Is the teller inside the story saying I?","explain":"The narrator says I and takes part in the events, so this is first person. We only know what this one character sees and thinks."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A narrator follows only Ella. We hear her thoughts, but in chapter {n} we never learn what her rival is planning. Which perspective is this?","choices":["Third person limited","Omniscient","First person"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the minds we enter. One mind, or many?","explain":"The narrator uses she and stays with Ella's thoughts only. Because we are locked out of the rival's mind, this is third person limited, not omniscient.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"In a film, we see a monster hiding under the bed. The girl climbs in, smiling. Why do we feel tense?","choices":["We know something she does not: dramatic irony","The story has a surprise twist","The narrator is lying to us"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who knows about the monster: us, her, or both?","explain":"We know about the danger and she does not. That gap in knowledge is dramatic irony, and it creates tension now rather than a surprise later."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Jay narrates: “I never cheat.” Later, {n} classmates see answers written on his hand. What does this suggest?","choices":["Jay is an unreliable narrator","Jay is telling the truth","The author is a cheat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare what Jay says with what others see.","explain":"Jay's claim clashes with the evidence, so we should not trust his account. Readers spot unreliable narrators by testing words against actions and other characters' reactions.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why might an author choose a limited narrator for a mystery?","choices":["To hide what other characters know, building suspense","To tell the reader the answer early","Because limited narrators cannot use he or she"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a limited view keep from the reader?","explain":"A limited narrator only knows what one character knows. The reader is kept in the dark with them, so clues and suspects stay uncertain until the end."}],"explain":{"kid":["Every story has a narrator, the voice telling it. Ask two questions. Who says I? Whose thoughts do we get? If the narrator says I and takes part, it is first person. If the narrator says he or she, it is third person.","Third person comes in two kinds. Third limited follows one character. We hear only their thoughts, so we miss what they miss. Omniscient means all-knowing. The narrator can step into any mind and tell us things no character knows.","This choice shapes how we feel. A limited view keeps us guessing, which builds suspense. When we know more than a character, we get dramatic irony. We want to shout a warning, but they cannot hear us.","Some narrators cannot be trusted. They may lie, forget or not understand. This is an unreliable narrator. Look for clues: actions that clash with their words, other people reacting oddly, or details they skip. The narrator is not the author. It is a voice the author made."],"adult":["This topic covers point of view in fiction: first person, third person limited and third person omniscient. It also covers the unreliable narrator. Learners explain how each choice widens or narrows what the reader knows. They then link that to effects such as suspense, dramatic irony and humour.","The lab uses one small scene: a smashed vase, a boy called Sam, his gran and a cat. The same event is told by Sam, from Gran's view and by an all-knowing narrator. Learners watch which thought bubbles open. Then they name the view in three short passages, find the facts Gran does not know, and catch Sam's account out against the evidence.","The most common mistake is to treat third person as all-knowing. The test is not the pronoun but how many minds we enter. Pupils also mix up dramatic irony, where we already know, with a twist, which surprises us later. Many trust a first-person narrator because they say so. Some also treat the narrator as the author.","At home, read the opening of any novel together and ask: whose head are we in, and what can we not know yet? Classic unreliable narrators to try at this age include Christopher in The Curious Incident, a reliable but limited teller, and Poe's narrator in The Tell-Tale Heart, who insists he is sane."]},"familyTip":"Tell a small family event, such as who ate the last biscuit, twice: once as yourself saying I, and once as an all-knowing narrator. Ask what the second telling reveals that the first could not.","keyIdea":"Who tells a story decides what the reader can know. First person gives one mind from inside, third limited follows one mind from outside, and omniscient opens every mind. Narrators can also be wrong or lie, so test what they say against what happens.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which perspective is it? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Third limited","Omniscient"],"items":[["We hear only Ella's thoughts",0],["We miss what the rival plans",0],["We learn as the hero learns",0],["We hear Tom's and Mum's thoughts",1],["We know a secret no character knows",1],["We jump between minds in a scene",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnarr","caption":"Flagship lab: tell one event three ways, name the view, find what Gran doesn't know, catch Sam's lies and mix narrators.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ak_ZgoMKRQ":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What must be true of the words inside quotation marks?","choices":["They are exactly the writer's words","They mean the same as the writer's words","They are the most important words in the text"],"answer":0,"hint":"Quotation marks make a promise to the reader. What is it?","explain":"Quotation marks promise the writer's exact words. Words that only mean the same are a paraphrase, so they go outside the marks."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The text says: \"Ana hid her face and would not speak.\" Which quote best proves Ana was shy?","choices":["\"hid her face and would not speak\"","\"Ana\"","\"Ana was shy and quiet\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the copied words that show the feeling.","explain":"\"hid her face and would not speak\" is exact and proves it. One name proves nothing, and the text never says \"Ana was shy and quiet\"."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The text never says \"Leo was hungry\". Which quote could prove it?","choices":["\"Leo's tummy rumbled as he stared at the cake\"","\"Leo was hungry\"","\"Leo walked home\""],"answer":0,"hint":"You can quote a clue and infer the feeling from it.","explain":"A rumbling tummy and staring at cake are clues that let you infer hunger. You cannot quote words the text never uses."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The text says: \"She ran as fast as the wind.\" Kim writes: She \"ran as quick as the wind.\" What is wrong?","choices":["She changed \"fast\" to \"quick\"","Nothing, it means the same","She used too few words"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare Kim's words with the text one at a time.","explain":"\"quick\" is not the writer's word. Even when the meaning stays the same, a changed word makes it a misquote."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Where do the quotation marks go in: The poet says the sea was \"grey and cold\" that morning?","choices":["Round only the copied words \"grey and cold\"","Round the whole sentence","Round \"The poet says\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Which words were copied from the poem?","explain":"Only \"grey and cold\" came from the poem, so only those words go inside the marks. The rest is your own sentence."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What should usually come after a quotation in your answer?","choices":["A few words that say what it shows","Another quotation straight away","Nothing at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"How will the reader know why you chose those words?","explain":"Explaining what the quote shows links it to your point, for example \"which shows that Mia was nervous\"."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Max copies a {a}-word sentence but changes {b} of the words. How many words does he need to fix to quote it accurately?","answer":"b","hint":"An accurate quote has every word exactly as the writer wrote it.","explain":"All {b} changed words must go back to the writer's words. One changed word is enough to make a misquote.","params":{"a":[8,14],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"An article says: \"Owls can turn their heads a long way round.\" Which quote proves owls can see behind them?","choices":["\"Owls can turn their heads a long way round\"","\"Owls are birds\"","\"Owls can see behind their backs\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Choose words that are really in the article and that support the point.","explain":"Turning the head a long way round is the clue. The article never says \"see behind their backs\", and \"Owls are birds\" is not in it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Your answer quotes {a} separate phrases from the text. How many opening quotation marks (“) does it need?","answer":"a","hint":"Each quoted phrase needs its own pair of marks: one to open and one to close.","explain":"Each of the {a} phrases opens with its own “ mark, so you need {a} opening marks, and {a} closing marks too.","params":{"a":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The story says: \"Tom slammed the door and stomped up the stairs.\" Which quote best proves Tom was angry?","choices":["\"slammed the door and stomped\"","\"up the stairs\"","\"Tom was very cross\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Which words show how Tom acted? Are they the story's own words?","explain":"\"slammed the door and stomped\" is copied exactly and shows anger. \"up the stairs\" does not show a feeling, and the story never says \"Tom was very cross\"."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A quote you want to use has {a} words, but only the last {b} prove your point. How many words should you quote?","answer":"b","hint":"Quote only the words that do the proving.","explain":"Quote just the {b} words that prove the point. The other words only hide your proof.","params":{"a":[12,20],"b":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The text says: \"The river was wide and brown.\" Which is an accurate quote?","choices":["The text says the river \"was wide and brown.\"","The text says the river \"was big and brown.\"","The text says the river \"is wide and brown.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare each word with the text, one at a time.","explain":"Only \"was wide and brown\" matches exactly. \"big\" changes a word, and \"is\" changes the ending of the verb."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam's sentence has {a} words. Only {b} of them are copied from the text. How many words go inside the quotation marks?","answer":"b","hint":"Only copied words go inside the marks. Your own words stay outside.","explain":"The {b} copied words go inside the marks. The other words are Sam's own, so they stay outside.","params":{"a":[10,16],"b":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is a signal phrase that leads in to a quotation?","choices":["According to the text,","I think that","In my opinion,"],"answer":0,"hint":"A signal phrase points the reader to the text, not to you.","explain":"\"According to the text,\" tells the reader the next words come from the text. The other two lead in to your own opinion."}],"explain":{"kid":["When you write about a text, you can prove your point with its own words. That is a quotation. Copy the words exactly, then put quotation marks round them. Your own words stay outside the marks.","Quote just the words that prove your point. To show Mia was nervous, \"Her hands shook\" is enough. A whole paragraph hides your proof, and one word on its own may not make sense.","Check your copy word by word. \"Her hand was shaking\" is not what the story says, even if it means the same. Changing a word, an ending or a small word like \"her\" turns a quote into a misquote.","Texts do not always say things outright. The story never says Mia was proud, but \"Mia grinned all the way home\" is a clue. Quote the clue, then say what it shows. Start with a signal phrase, such as \"According to the text,\"."],"adult":["This reading skill (ages 10 to 11, Common Core RL.5.1 and RI.5.1) asks learners to quote accurately from literary and informational texts, both for what a text says explicitly and for inferences. In practice that means choosing relevant words, copying them exactly and punctuating them as a quotation.","Good quotations are short and relevant: a phrase or sentence that proves one point. They are introduced with a signal phrase (\"The author states\", \"According to the text\") and followed by an explanation that links the words to the claim. Copied words go inside quotation marks; the learner's own wording stays outside.","Common mistakes: copying a long passage instead of the proving words; paraphrasing but still using quotation marks; changing small words or endings (hand for hands, the for her); quoting accurate words that do not support the claim; and dropping a quotation in with no signal phrase or explanation.","In the lab, learners drag across a story to select proof for a claim, spot the three changes in a classmate's misquote, tap gaps to place opening and closing marks, quote a clue for an inference, and build a signal phrase, quotation and explanation from tiles. The challenge uses a new non-fiction text about honeybees."]},"familyTip":"When your child tells you a book character is mean or brave, ask, \"Which words in the book tell you that?\" Let them find the exact words and read them out, then say what those words show.","keyIdea":"A quotation is the writer's exact words, copied word for word and put inside quotation marks. Quote only the words that prove your point, whether the text says it outright or gives a clue you infer from. Lead in with a signal phrase such as \"According to the text,\" and then explain what the quotation shows.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"The story says: \"Her hands shook as she held her poem.\" Is each one an accurate quote? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Accurate quote","Not accurate"],"items":[["\"Her hands shook\"",0],["\"held her poem\"",0],["\"as she held her poem\"",0],["\"Her hand shook\"",1],["\"she held the poem\"",1],["\"Her hands were shaking\"",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagquote","caption":"Flagship lab: drag across a story to quote the proof, spot a misquote, place quotation marks, quote a clue and build a signal phrase, quote and explanation.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_aFvsj35QzC":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A page says: Bats sleep in the day. Bats fly at night. They eat bugs. What is it about?","choices":["Bats","Bugs","The day"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who is \"they\"? What does every sentence tell about?","explain":"They means bats, so every sentence tells about bats. Bugs are one detail."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which sentence sums up a page about how a chick hatches?","choices":["This page is about how a chick hatches.","Chicks are fluffy.","This page is about farms."],"answer":0,"hint":"A summary tells about the whole page, not one detail.","explain":"The first sentence covers the whole page. Fluffy is one detail, and farms is too wide."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A book about cars has {n} facts. One says \"Bikes have two wheels.\" Does it belong?","choices":["No, it is about bikes","Yes, bikes are like cars","Yes, it has wheels"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: is this fact about cars, or something else?","explain":"Bikes are close to cars, but the fact tells about bikes, so it does not belong with the {n} car facts.","params":{"n":[5,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Is \"My dog is the best\" a key fact for a page about how dogs smell things?","choices":["No, it is what someone thinks","Yes, it says dog","Yes, it is the topic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it teach how dogs smell things?","explain":"It says what someone thinks. It does not teach how dogs smell, so it is not a key fact."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You read a page with {n} facts about sharks. How many key facts might you retell?","choices":["Two or three","None","Just the title"],"answer":0,"hint":"A retell gives a few of the most useful facts.","explain":"Pick two or three key facts from the {n} to retell. You do not need all of them, but you need some.","params":{"n":[5,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The sun came out. The ice melted. Which word links them?","choices":["so","but","or"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did the sun make the ice melt?","explain":"The sun made the ice melt, so we use so: the sun came out, so the ice melted."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A page says: Owls hunt at night. Owls eat mice. Owls have big eyes. A child says it is about mice. What went wrong?","choices":["Mice is only one detail","Mice is the main topic","Nothing went wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many sentences tell about mice?","explain":"Mice is in one sentence. Every sentence tells about owls, so owls is the main topic."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A page says: Ants live in big groups. Ants dig tunnels. Ants carry food home. What is the main topic?","choices":["Ants","Tunnels","Food"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does every sentence tell about?","explain":"Every sentence tells about ants. Tunnels and food are details about the ants."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A fact book about the moon has {n} pages. Which fact belongs in it?","choices":["The moon goes round the Earth.","Cars have four wheels.","I like my red hat."],"answer":0,"hint":"A fact belongs when it tells about the topic of the book.","explain":"Only the first fact tells about the moon, so it belongs in all {n} pages of the moon book.","params":{"n":[8,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is a key fact about how fish breathe?","choices":["Fish use gills to take air from water.","My fish is called Bob.","I think fish are fun."],"answer":0,"hint":"A key fact teaches about the topic. It is not what someone likes or owns.","explain":"Gills taking air from water is the fact that teaches how fish breathe. The others are about someone."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"It snowed for {n} days. Which is the best way to link it to the next fact?","choices":["So the roads were white.","So it was very hot.","So I like jam."],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick what snow could make happen.","explain":"After {n} days of snow, the roads were white. Snow does not make it hot, and jam has no link.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A fact page tells you about one big thing. That is the main topic. Ask: what does every sentence tell about?","Owls hunt at night. Owls have big eyes. Owls eat mice. Mice are in it, but just once. Every sentence is about owls. So the main topic is owls.","Key facts teach you about the topic. \"Plants need water\" is a key fact. \"I like sunflowers best\" is not. It just says what someone likes.","Some facts are linked. It rained all day, so the river got high. The word so shows how one thing made the next thing happen."],"adult":["Informational texts, such as fact books about animals or space, are organised around a main topic. At five to seven, children learn to say what a short text is mostly about in one sentence, to list two or three key facts they learned, and to explain how two facts or events are connected, often by cause and effect.","The lab builds each skill with its own action. Children tap a word to see which sentences tell about it, sort facts into a shark book, pick the one sentence that sums up a page, choose key facts about plants while leaving out opinions, and link pairs of facts with so.","Common mistakes to watch for: naming a detail that is vivid or comes last, such as mice on an owl page, as the topic; accepting a fact because it is near the topic, such as dolphins in a shark book; giving one fact as a summary; and treating opinions or personal stories as key details. Pronouns can also hide the topic, so check what they or it points back to.","To help, after reading a page together, ask: \"What was every sentence about?\" then \"Tell me two things you learned.\" Use the title and pictures as clues, but check them against the sentences."]},"familyTip":"After reading a non-fiction page or a cereal box together, ask, \"What was it all about?\" and \"Tell me two things you learned.\" Then ask how two facts are linked: \"It was cold, so what happened?\"","keyIdea":"A fact book or page has a main topic: the one thing every sentence tells about. Facts that tell about the topic are key details; things someone likes are not. You can say the main topic in one sentence, retell a few key facts, and use words like so to show how two facts are linked.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"A page about frogs: is each line a key fact? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🐸 Key fact","💭 Not a key fact"],"items":[["Frogs lay eggs in water",0],["Tadpoles grow legs",0],["Frogs catch flies with their tongues",0],["Frogs can jump far",0],["I think frogs are cute",1],["My pal has a fish",1],["I had toast today",1],["Green is my best colour",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtopic","caption":"Flagship lab: find what every sentence is about, sort facts into a shark book, pick a one-sentence summary, choose key facts and link facts with so.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_XeMZdf2Y9W":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is on the front cover of a book?","choices":["The title and a big picture","The blurb","Only page numbers"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you see first when you pick up a book?","explain":"The front cover shows the title and a big picture. The blurb is on the back cover."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Who writes the words in a book?","choices":["The author","The illustrator","The spine"],"answer":0,"hint":"One name is for the words, one is for the pictures.","explain":"The author writes the words. The illustrator draws the pictures. The spine is a part of the book, not a person."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where can you find the title, the author and the illustrator together?","choices":["The title page","The back cover","The last page"],"answer":0,"hint":"Open the book. Which page near the front names the people who made it?","explain":"The title page, just inside the book, names the title, the author and the illustrator."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Point: Trees are important. Which is a reason?","choices":["Trees give us clean air","Some trees are tall","I climbed a tree"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it show trees matter? A true fact is not always a reason.","explain":"Clean air shows trees are important. Being tall is true, but it does not show trees matter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A page about frogs has {n} sentences. Which one backs up \"Frogs need water\"?","choices":["Their skin must stay wet","Frogs are green","I like frogs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which sentence tells you why frogs need water?","explain":"Of the {n} sentences, \"their skin must stay wet\" is the reason. Green is a fact that does not explain it, and liking frogs is an opinion.","params":{"n":[6,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"An author gives {n} reasons that bees help us. What do the reasons do?","choices":["Back up the point","Name the illustrator","Tell you the page number"],"answer":0,"hint":"Why does an author add reasons after a point?","explain":"All {n} reasons back up the point that bees help us. They show the point is right.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which sentence uses because to join a point and a reason?","choices":["We brush our teeth because it stops holes","We brush our teeth because I have a red brush","We brush our teeth and it is Monday"],"answer":0,"hint":"The part after because must show why the point is right.","explain":"Stopping holes is why we brush our teeth. A red brush and Monday do not explain the point."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where is the blurb on a book?","choices":["On the back cover","On the front cover","On the spine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Turn the book over. Where is the short bit that tells you what the book is about?","explain":"The blurb is on the back cover. It tells you what the book is about, so you can choose if you want to read it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A book has {n} pictures. Who drew them?","choices":["The illustrator","The author","The reader"],"answer":0,"hint":"One person writes the words. Another person can draw the pictures. Which name is for the pictures?","explain":"The illustrator drew all {n} pictures. The author wrote the words. You find both names on the title page.","params":{"n":[6,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Point: Dogs are good helpers. Which is a reason?","choices":["Dogs can guide people who cannot see","Dogs have four legs","I saw a dog in the park"],"answer":0,"hint":"A reason shows the point is right. Which one shows dogs help people?","explain":"Guiding people who cannot see shows dogs help. Four legs is true but does not show helping, and seeing a dog is not a reason."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"There are {n} books on a shelf. Which part of each book can you see?","choices":["The spine","The blurb","The title page"],"answer":0,"hint":"On a shelf, books stand side by side. Which part faces out?","explain":"On a shelf you see each spine, so all {n} spines show their titles. The blurb is on the back and the title page is inside.","params":{"n":[6,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at a book. The front cover is what you see first. It has the title and a big picture. The spine is the thin side. On a shelf, you see the spine.","Turn the book over. That is the back cover. The short bit of writing there is the blurb. It tells you what the book is about.","Open the book. The title page has the title again. It names the author, who wrote the words. It names the illustrator, who drew the pictures.","Books that teach facts make a point, like \"Bees are helpful.\" Then they give reasons. \"Bees make honey\" is a reason. \"Bees have six legs\" is true, but it does not show bees help us. So it is not a reason for that point."],"adult":["Five to eight is when children move from enjoying books to knowing how they work. This topic covers the parts of a book (front cover, back cover, spine, blurb and title page), the roles named on the title page (author and illustrator), and an early reading-for-meaning skill from information texts: finding the reasons an author gives to support a point.","In the US Common Core these are standards RI.K.5 and RI.K.6 (parts of a book, author and illustrator) and RI.1.8 and RI.2.8 (identify the reasons an author gives to support points). In England they sit in Year 1 and Year 2 reading: discussing the title and how items of information are related, and learning how non-fiction books are structured. A point is the main claim; a reason is a sentence that shows the claim is right.","Common mistakes to watch for: mixing up the author and the illustrator; looking for the blurb on the front cover; calling any true fact a reason even when it does not support the point, such as \"bees have six legs\" for \"bees are helpful\"; and offering an opinion such as \"I like frogs\" as a reason. Be careful to praise a true fact before explaining why it does not back up this point.","In the lab your child drags labels onto a book's front cover, spine, back cover and blurb, taps the title, author and illustrator on a title page, picks the three sentences that show bees are helpful, sorts dog facts under the point they support, and builds point because reason sentences."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime, before you read, ask your child to find the front cover, the spine and the blurb, then the author and illustrator on the title page. With an information book, read one page and ask, \"What is the author's point? Which sentence backs it up?\" Use the word because: \"Bees are helpful because they make honey.\"","keyIdea":"A book has a front cover with the title and a picture, a spine down the side, and a back cover with the blurb, which tells you what the book is about. Inside, the title page names the title, the author, who writes the words, and the illustrator, who draws the pictures. In an information book, the author makes a point and gives reasons that back it up. A reason shows the point is right; a true fact that does not show this is not a reason.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Point: bees are helpful. Is each line a reason for it? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Reason bees help","❌ Not a reason"],"items":[["Bees make honey",0],["Bees carry pollen",0],["Bees help fruit grow",0],["Bees help flowers make seeds",0],["Bees have six legs",1],["Bees are stripy",1],["I like yellow",1],["My cat sleeps a lot",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbook","caption":"Flagship lab: label a book's covers, spine and blurb, find the title, author and illustrator, then spot and sort an author's reasons.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_OltpfaX7l6":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word is a push word that shows persuading?","choices":["should","river","Monday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word tells someone what to do?","explain":"\"Should\" tells you what to do, so it is a push word. River and Monday just name things."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"\"A dragon ate the king's hat and burped out a rainbow.\" Why was it written?","choices":["To entertain","To inform","To persuade"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it true? Is anyone asked to do something?","explain":"It is a silly, made-up story with no facts and no push words, so it was written to entertain."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"\"The school wasted {n} pounds on new paint.\" How does the author feel?","choices":["Against the paint","For the paint","We cannot tell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the word wasted. Is it a happy or a cross word?","explain":"\"Wasted\" shows the author thinks the {n} pounds were spent badly. They are against it.","params":{"n":[200,900]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You think the author's purpose is to persuade. How do you prove it?","choices":["Point to the push words in the text","Say you liked it","Count the pictures"],"answer":0,"hint":"Proof comes from the words the author wrote.","explain":"Point to words like \"you must\" or \"best ever\". The text itself is your evidence."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A poster says the zoo has {n} animals and you should visit this summer. What is the main purpose?","choices":["To persuade","To inform","To entertain"],"answer":0,"hint":"The number is a fact, but what does the poster want you to do?","explain":"\"You should visit\" is a push. The {n} animals are a fact used to persuade you to go.","params":{"n":[50,400]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What is the author's point of view?","choices":["What the author thinks about the topic","The page the author wrote on","What every reader must think"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the author's opinion.","explain":"Point of view is what the author thinks. Readers can have a different view of their own."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"\"Penguins cannot fly, but they swim very fast.\" Why was it written?","choices":["To inform","To persuade","To entertain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it true? Does it ask you to do anything?","explain":"It gives true facts about penguins and asks nothing of you, so the author wants to inform."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"\"Get your tickets now! It is the best show in town!\" Why was it written?","choices":["To persuade","To inform","To entertain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it push you to do something? Look for now and best.","explain":"\"Get your tickets now\" tells you what to do and \"best\" praises the show, so the author wants to persuade you."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A fact book says a blue whale can be {n} metres long. Why was it written?","choices":["To inform","To persuade","To entertain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it teaching you a true fact, or asking you to do something?","explain":"A length of {n} metres is a true fact that teaches you about whales. The author wants to inform.","params":{"n":[24,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"An advert says: \"{n} out of 10 kids love Zoom Shoes. Buy yours today!\" What is the purpose?","choices":["To persuade, using a fact as help","To inform, because it has a number","To entertain, because it is fun"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the push words. Is the number there to teach you, or to make you buy?","explain":"\"Buy yours today\" pushes you. The fact that {n} out of 10 kids love them is there to make the push stronger.","params":{"n":[6,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"The author thinks dogs make the best pets. You like cats more. What is true?","choices":["Your view and the author's view can be different","You must agree with the author","The author is wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a reader have to think the same as the author?","explain":"The author's point of view is what the author thinks. You can disagree: the author thinks dogs are best, but I think cats are."}],"explain":{"kid":["Every text has a reason. Authors write to persuade you, to inform you, or to entertain you. That reason is called the author's purpose.","To persuade is to make you think or do something. Look for push words like should, must and best. An advert says: You must try it! Persuaders often add facts to make their case stronger.","To inform is to teach you true facts, like how owls hunt. To entertain is to give you a story or a joke to enjoy.","The author's point of view is what the author thinks. You do not have to agree. You can say: The author thinks school should start later, but I think it should not. Words show how the author feels: wasted money sounds cross, spent money sounds calm."],"adult":["Identifying the author's purpose is a core reading-comprehension standard: US Common Core RI.2.6 asks children to identify the main purpose of a text, and RI.3.6 asks them to distinguish their own point of view from that of the author. The English national curriculum for Years 3 to 6 adds distinguishing statements of fact from opinion and explaining how language contributes to meaning.","Teachers often use the shorthand PIE: persuade, inform, entertain. Informational texts usually inform, adverts and letters to the editor persuade, and stories and poems entertain, but form is only a clue. The best evidence is the language: imperatives, 'should' and 'must', and superlatives signal persuasion.","Common mistakes: deciding that any text with facts must be informing (persuaders use facts as evidence); thinking that a fun or colourful text must be entertaining when it is selling something; believing that disagreeing with the author means you read it wrong; and naming a purpose without quoting the words that show it.","Attitude is shown through word choice. 'The council wasted money' and 'The council invested money' report the same fact, but the loaded verb tells you which side the author is on. Ask your child to swap one word and say how the author now seems to feel."]},"familyTip":"Pick up any leaflet, cereal box or news headline at home. Ask: Why was this written? Which words tell you? Then ask what your child thinks, and let them disagree with the author.","keyIdea":"Every text has an author's purpose: to persuade you, to inform you, or to entertain you. Push words such as should, must and best show an author is persuading, even when the text also uses facts. The author's point of view is what the author thinks; your own view can be different. Loaded words show the author's attitude, and you prove a purpose by pointing to the words that show it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Why was each text written? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📢 Persuade","📘 Inform","🎭 Entertain"],"items":[["You must try our new pizza!",0],["Vote for a longer lunch break!",0],["Frogs lay eggs called spawn.",1],["The Moon goes round the Earth.",1],["The cat wore boots and sang.",2],["A giant sneezed and the town flew away.",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpurp","caption":"Flagship lab: sort texts by purpose, find push words, pin your view and the author's, swap loaded words, and prove each purpose.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_RjZNxkQb85":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the context of reception?","choices":["The time, place and values of whoever reads or watches the text","The time and place where the author wrote it","The plot events that come before a scene"],"answer":0,"hint":"Reception means receiving: who is receiving the text?","explain":"The context of reception is the time, place and values of the reader or audience, at any point. The author's world is the context of production."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"'Shakespeare was sexist because he did not support votes for women.' What is the slip?","choices":["Presentism: judging the past by a modern standard","It uses too much evidence","It confuses comedy with tragedy"],"answer":0,"hint":"When did campaigns for women's votes begin?","explain":"Votes for women were not a live issue in the 1590s, so this judges the past by a much later standard. That slip is called presentism."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why is 'Elizabethan audiences found the ending funny' a weak claim?","choices":["It treats a whole audience as having one reaction","Elizabethans never watched comedies","The ending is not in the play"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did every person in the theatre react the same way?","explain":"It assumes one audience mind. Hedge it: 'some original audiences may have laughed', especially as Fletcher's sequel shows others resisted."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Jean Rhys's Wide Sargasso Sea retells Jane Eyre from Bertha's point of view. It is best described as...","choices":["a later reinterpretation shaped by its own context","proof of what Charlotte Brontë intended","the context of production of Jane Eyre"],"answer":0,"hint":"It was written in 1966, over a century after Jane Eyre.","explain":"Written in 1966, it reads Jane Eyre from a postcolonial position. That makes it a reception, not evidence of Brontë's intent."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"In a {n}-mark essay, what should context do?","choices":["Sharpen an argument about the text","Replace close reading of quotations","Fill the first paragraph with background"],"answer":0,"hint":"Examiners reward context that is linked to the text.","explain":"In a {n}-mark essay, context earns credit when it changes how a line or choice is read. It supports close reading and never replaces it.","params":{"n":[25,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A director in {y} stages Katherine's speech as bitter irony. What does this show?","choices":["Reception can reshape how a text's meaning is presented","The words of the speech were rewritten","The play has no meaning of its own"],"answer":0,"hint":"The words are the same. What changed?","explain":"The {y} staging keeps the words but changes delivery and framing. That shows reception shaping meaning, while the text still limits what is plausible.","params":{"y":[1980,2024]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Reading 'Thy husband is thy lord' against the 1563 Homily on matrimony is which kind of reading?","choices":["Historicist","Performance-based","Purely personal response"],"answer":0,"hint":"It reads the line beside a document from the same era.","explain":"Setting the line beside a document from its own time is a historicist reading of the context of production."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is a context of production for The Taming of the Shrew?","choices":["Women's roles were played by boy actors","A modern audience's views on control in relationships","A film version made in 1929"],"answer":0,"hint":"Production is the world the play was first made and staged in.","explain":"Boy actors playing women belongs to how the play was first made and staged in the 1590s, so it is production. The other two are later audiences: reception."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A reader in {y} finds Katherine's final speech disturbing. Which context mainly explains this?","choices":["The context of reception","The context of production","The play's source material"],"answer":0,"hint":"Whose world is shaping the response: the writer's or the reader's?","explain":"A reaction in {y} comes from that reader's own values and time, which is the context of reception.","params":{"y":[1990,2026]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which sentence uses context to sharpen an argument?","choices":["Under coverture a wife's goods became her husband's, so 'my goods, my chattels' states the law, not just a boast","Shakespeare was born in Stratford in 1564","The Elizabethan era was a time of great change"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one changes how you read a specific line?","explain":"The coverture sentence ties a fact to a quotation and changes how we read it. The others are true or vague but change nothing about the play."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Fletcher answered the play in a sequel about {n} years after it was written. What does that show?","choices":["Early audiences did not all accept the taming","Fletcher misunderstood Shakespeare","The play was forgotten until the 20th century"],"answer":0,"hint":"If someone writes a reply, did everyone agree with the original?","explain":"A sequel in which Petruchio is tamed, written only about {n} years later, shows the taming was argued with in its own era. So a resistant reading is not purely modern.","params":{"n":[17,21]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A play is written once, but it is read again and again. The world the writer lived in is the context of production. The time and place of the person reading or watching is the context of reception.","Take Katherine's last speech in The Taming of the Shrew. She tells wives to obey their husbands. The words have not changed since the 1590s. But audiences have. Around 1611 John Fletcher wrote a sequel where Petruchio is tamed himself. In 1929 Mary Pickford winked at the camera as she spoke the lines. Many modern stagings play the speech as irony, or as a woman worn down.","So meaning is not only in the words. It is made when a reader meets them. That does not mean any reading goes. You still need evidence from the text.","Use context to change how a line reads. Coverture meant a wife's goods became her husband's. So when Petruchio calls Kate 'my goods, my chattels', he is stating the law. That fact sharpens your point. A date you just drop in does not."],"adult":["A-level English Literature tests context directly. AQA, Edexcel and OCR all use Assessment Objective 3 for it. Students must show how the contexts in which texts are written and received shape meaning. Strong answers split the two. The context of production is the writer's era, genre, theatre, law and beliefs. The context of reception is any later audience, including the student.","The Taming of the Shrew is a classic test case. Within about 20 years, Fletcher's The Woman's Prize, or The Tamer Tamed, answered it. Garrick's 1754 version kept the taming as farce. The 1929 Pickford film added a wink. Modern stagings often unsettle the speech. Reception theory, from Jauss's horizon of expectations to reader-response criticism, explains why the same words yield new readings.","Common mistakes: bolting on facts about the writer's life that change nothing about a line. Presentism, which judges a 1590s text by a modern standard. Saying 'Elizabethans thought' as if a whole audience had one mind. And letting context replace close reading. A careful answer hedges with 'may have' or 'some audiences', ties each fact to a quotation, and keeps the text at the centre.","In the lab your teenager sorts six contexts into production or reception. Next they place four real responses to Katherine's speech on a timeline. Then they judge whether context sentences sharpen a point or are bolted on. Finally they match one line to historicist, feminist and performance readings, and catch the classic slips."]},"familyTip":"Watch two short clips of the same scene from different productions or films together and ask: what did each director want the audience to feel, and what about their own time might explain the choice?","keyIdea":"Every text has two kinds of context. The context of production is the world it was made in: the writer's time, place, laws, beliefs and theatre. The context of reception is the time, place and values of each reader or audience. The words stay the same, but readers in different times and places bring different values, so they can read the same text in different ways. Good essays use context to sharpen an argument about the text, never to replace it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each one a context of production or of reception? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🖋️ Production","👓 Reception"],"items":[["Boy actors played women",0],["Coverture law",0],["The 1563 marriage homily",0],["Genre: Elizabethan comedy",0],["A 2020s audience",1],["Garrick's 1754 version",1],["Pickford's 1929 film",1],["Your own reading today",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagctx","caption":"Flagship lab: split production and reception, date four audiences, judge context use, match lenses, spot slips.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_V6456X6pJE":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which statement is a fact?","choices":["Bats are mammals.","Bats are creepy.","Bats should be kept as pets."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one could a scientist check?","explain":"\"Bats are mammals\" is a fact: bats feed their young on milk. Creepy is a feeling, and should is a view about what ought to happen."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"\"Our town has {n} parks, and they are all beautiful.\" What is this sentence?","choices":["A fact and an opinion","Just a fact","Just an opinion"],"answer":0,"hint":"Split it into two parts. Check each part on its own.","explain":"\"Our town has {n} parks\" can be counted, so it is a fact. \"They are all beautiful\" is a view. The sentence holds both.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"\"Everyone knows that cats are better than dogs.\" Fact or opinion?","choices":["Opinion","Fact, because everyone knows it","Fact, because it is about animals"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is \"better\" something you can measure?","explain":"It is an opinion. \"Everyone knows\" is a trick to make a view sound certain, and better is a judgement."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why is \"Water freezes at 0 °C\" a fact?","choices":["It can be checked with a thermometer","Most people believe it","It is written in a sure way"],"answer":0,"hint":"What makes any statement a fact?","explain":"It can be tested with ice and a thermometer. Belief and a confident tone do not make a fact; being checkable does."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word turns this into an opinion? \"The match lasted {n} minutes and was thrilling.\"","choices":["thrilling","lasted","minutes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word tells you how the writer felt?","explain":"Thrilling is the judging word. The {n} minutes can be checked with a clock, so that part is a fact.","params":{"n":[60,95]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"\"Venus is the hottest planet in our solar system.\" Fact or opinion?","choices":["Fact","Opinion, because hottest is a judging word","Opinion, because it is surprising"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hottest here is measured with instruments, not felt.","explain":"It is a fact. Space probes measured Venus at about 465 °C, hotter than Mercury. Hottest is a measurement here, not a taste."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A report says: \"Clearly, the new road is a terrible idea.\" What does \"clearly\" do?","choices":["Makes a view sound certain","Turns the view into a fact","Shows the writer checked it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did the writer give any evidence?","explain":"Clearly makes the view sound sure. Terrible is still a judgement, and nothing was checked."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"\"The school trip costs £{n}.\" Is this a fact or an opinion?","choices":["Fact","Opinion","Both"],"answer":0,"hint":"Could you check it? Look at the letter from school or ask the office.","explain":"It is a fact. You can check the price of £{n} on the letter. There is no judging word.","params":{"n":[5,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"\"The {n} best songs of the year are all by one band.\" Fact or opinion?","choices":["Opinion","Fact","Both"],"answer":0,"hint":"The number {n} can be counted. But is \"best\" something you can check?","explain":"It is an opinion. \"Best\" is a judgement, and the number {n} does not change that. Another listener would pick different songs.","params":{"n":[3,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which word makes this an opinion? \"The museum, which opened in 1990, is wonderful.\"","choices":["wonderful","opened","1990"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word tells you what the writer thinks?","explain":"Wonderful is the judging word. \"Opened in 1990\" can be checked, so that part is a fact."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"An advert says: \"It is a fact that Zing is the tastiest drink.\" What is it?","choices":["An opinion dressed as a fact","A fact, because it says so","A fact, because many people buy it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can anyone measure \"tastiest\"?","explain":"It is an opinion. Saying \"it is a fact that\" does not make tastiest checkable. Taste is a judgement."}],"explain":{"kid":["Ask one question: could I check it? \"A blue whale can grow to about 30 metres long\" can be checked in a book or with a tape. That is a fact. \"Whales are the most amazing animals\" cannot. Someone else may love tigers more. That is an opinion.","Look for judging words. Best, worst, brilliant, boring, should and delicious all tell you what someone thinks. A number does not make a sentence a fact. \"The top five goals were all spectacular\" still has the judging word spectacular.","A surprising fact is still a fact. Sharks were on Earth before trees. It sounds odd, but fossils let scientists check it. A fact can also turn out to be wrong when someone checks it. It is still the kind of statement you can test.","Writers mix the two. \"Our school has 400 pupils, so it is far too crowded\" has a fact, then a view. Adverts go further. They say \"it is a fact that\" or \"everyone knows\", or use sure words like clearly. None of that makes a view checkable."],"adult":["The English national curriculum for Years 5 and 6 asks pupils to tell statements of fact from opinion when they read. Here they label each statement in a non-fiction text. They explain how they can tell. They also spot tricks that make a view look like a fact.","The test is: can it be checked? A fact can be measured, counted, seen or looked up in a source you trust. An opinion is a judgement, a feeling or a belief, so fair people can disagree. Judging words (best, brilliant), words like should and must, and phrases like I think are the usual signs.","Common mistakes: thinking any sentence with a number is a fact; calling a strange or new fact an opinion; thinking a view held by lots of people becomes a fact; labelling a whole sentence when only one part judges; and trusting an advert that says \"it is a fact that\". Another slip is calling a false claim an opinion. A false claim can still be checked. It just fails the check.","In the lab the learner swipes statements into fact and opinion, taps the judging word in four sentences, and colours the fact part and the view part of mixed sentences. Then they tag three advert tricks and colour a whole news report."]},"keyIdea":"A fact is a statement that can be checked: measured, counted, observed or looked up in a reliable source. An opinion is what someone thinks, feels or believes, so it cannot be proved. Judging words such as best, brilliant, should and delicious mark an opinion, and one sentence can hold a fact and an opinion together. Writers, especially in adverts, often dress an opinion up as a fact.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Can each statement be checked? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🔎 Fact","💭 Opinion"],"items":[["The Moon orbits the Earth.",0],["Chess is more fun than football.",1],["A spider has eight legs.",0],["Spiders are the scariest animals.",1],["London is the capital of the UK.",0],["Everyone should learn to swim.",1],["The bus was 10 minutes late.",0],["The bus driver was rude.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfvo","caption":"Flagship lab: swipe statements, find the judging word, split mixed sentences, tag advert tricks and highlight a news report.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_gZIo5oiBMt":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A book says: Frogs lay eggs in water. Tadpoles hatch from the eggs. What type of text is it?","choices":["A fact book","A poem","A story"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are these true things about real animals, or is something made up happening to a character?","explain":"It tells true facts about real frogs, so it is a fact book. It has no characters and no rhyme."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam wrote {a} poems. He drew the pictures for {b} of them, and Ann drew the rest. How many poems did Ann illustrate?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Ann drew the pictures for the poems Sam did not draw. Take {b} away from {a}.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}. Ann is the illustrator of {=a-b} poems, and Sam is the author of all {a}.","params":{"a":[4,9],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A book starts with Once upon a time. What type of text is it most likely to be?","choices":["A story","A fact book","A recipe"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which type of text starts by telling you a tale is about to begin?","explain":"Once upon a time is a clue that a story is starting, with characters and something that happens."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The cover says: by Lin Wu, pictures by Raj Dev. Who is the author?","choices":["Lin Wu","Raj Dev","Both of them"],"answer":0,"hint":"The word by comes before the author's name. The pictures name is the illustrator.","explain":"Lin Wu wrote the words, so Lin Wu is the author. Raj Dev drew the pictures, so Raj Dev is the illustrator."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A fact book about bugs has {n} labels on each page. How many labels are on 3 pages?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Labels name the parts in a picture. Add {n} three times.","explain":"{n} + {n} + {n} = {=3*n} labels on 3 pages.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Where can you find the names of the author and the illustrator?","choices":["On the front cover","Only on the last page","Inside the pictures"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the outside of the book, near the title.","explain":"Their names are on the front cover, near the title, and often on the first page inside too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A book has a shark on the cover. How can you find out if it is a story?","choices":["Look at the words inside","Guess from the shark","Count the pictures"],"answer":0,"hint":"A shark could be in a story or in a fact book. What tells you which?","explain":"Look inside: characters and once upon a time mean a story; true facts and labels mean a fact book."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Does every poem rhyme?","choices":["No, some poems do not rhyme","Yes, every poem rhymes","Only poems about animals rhyme"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rhyme is a clue for a poem, but think about poems with short lines and no rhyming words.","explain":"Many poems rhyme, but some do not. Short lines and playing with sounds are clues as well."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is from a poem?","choices":["Twinkle, twinkle, little star, how I wonder what you are","Owls hunt at night and sleep in the day","Once upon a time, an owl lost her way home"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for short lines and words that rhyme at the end.","explain":"Star and are rhyme, and it is said in a sing-song way. The owl facts are from a fact book, and once upon a time starts a story."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A poem has {=2*n} lines. Each two lines rhyme as a pair. How many rhyming pairs are there?","answer":"n","hint":"Put the lines into twos: lines 1 and 2, lines 3 and 4, and so on.","explain":"{=2*n} lines in twos make {n} rhyming pairs.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Who draws the pictures in a book?","choices":["The illustrator","The author","The library"],"answer":0,"hint":"One person writes the words and one person draws the pictures. Which name goes with pictures?","explain":"The illustrator draws the pictures. The author writes the words. Sometimes one person does both."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A shelf has {a} books. {b} of them are stories and the rest are fact books. How many fact books are there?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the stories away from all the books on the shelf.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}, so there are {=a-b} fact books.","params":{"a":[6,10],"b":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["There are different types of text. A story tells about characters, and something happens to them. A poem has short lines. It often rhymes, like cat and hat. A fact book tells true things about the real world.","You cannot always tell from the cover. A book with a lion on it could be a story about a lion who sings. Or it could be a fact book about real lions. Look at the words inside for clues.","Clues for a story: Once upon a time, and The end. Clues for a poem: short lines and words that rhyme. Clues for a fact book: true facts, labels on photos, and a page that lists what is inside.","Two people can make a book. The author writes the words. The illustrator draws the pictures. Look for their names on the front cover. Sometimes one person does both jobs."],"adult":["This topic matches the US Common Core reading standards for Kindergarten and Grade 1. RL.K.5 asks children to recognise common types of texts, such as storybooks and poems. RL.K.6 and RI.K.6 ask them to name the author and illustrator and say what each one does. RL.1.5 asks them to explain the main differences between books that tell stories and books that give information. In England, Year 1 pupils meet key stories, rhymes, poems and non-fiction, and learn to talk about them.","Useful terms: fiction, non-fiction, story, poem, rhyme, title, front cover, author, illustrator, contents page, label and caption. A story has characters, a setting and events, often with a problem and an ending. A poem is set out in short lines and plays with sound, through rhyme, rhythm and repeated words. A fact book is organised to inform, with headings, labelled pictures and a contents page.","Common mistakes: judging the type from the cover picture; thinking any book with pictures is a story; thinking the author draws the pictures; thinking every poem must rhyme, when many do not; and saying fiction means lies. Fiction is made up on purpose to entertain, which is different from telling a lie. Be careful with picture books too, where one person may be both author and illustrator.","In the lab your child sorts six books onto Story, Poem and Facts shelves, where a lizard on a cover tempts a wrong guess. They tap the clue on three pages, drag a pencil and a paintbrush tag to the author and the illustrator on two covers, tap the rhyming lines in a short poem, and then write and draw their own little book of two different types."]},"familyTip":"At the library, pick three books together: a story, a book of poems and a fact book. Before you open each one, guess its type from the cover, then check the words inside and find the author and the illustrator.","keyIdea":"Stories, poems and fact books are different types of text. A story (fiction) has characters, and something happens to them. A poem has short lines and often rhymes. A fact book (non-fiction) gives true information about the real world. The cover can trick you, so look at the words inside for clues. The author writes the words and the illustrator draws the pictures; their names are on the front cover, and one person can do both jobs.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Story, poem or facts? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📖 Story","🎵 Poem","🔎 Facts"],"items":[["Once upon a time, a hen found some wheat.",0],["The little pig ran away, and the wolf ran after him.",0],["Jack and Jill went up the hill to fetch a pail of water.",1],["Wiggle, wiggle, little worm, twist and turn and squirm.",1],["Owls hunt at night and sleep in the day.",2],["The heart pumps blood all round the body.",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtexts","caption":"Flagship lab: sort six books onto shelves, tap the clue on each page, tag the author and the illustrator, find rhymes, then make your own book.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_tIi6L1n7kF":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"An actor stands stage left. Where is she, seen from the audience?","choices":["On the audience's right","On the audience's left","At the back"],"answer":0,"hint":"Stage directions use the actor's view as they face the audience.","explain":"Stage left is the actor's left as they face the audience, so it is on the audience's right."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A director wants a ghost scene to feel eerie. Which lighting fits best?","choices":["Cold, dim blue light","Warm, bright light on the whole stage","Normal house lights"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which light hides detail and feels unsettling?","explain":"Cold, dim blue light feels eerie and hides detail. Warm, bright light feels safe and open."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is blocking?","choices":["The planned movement and position of actors on stage","Stopping an actor from speaking","Building the set"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is about where actors stand and move.","explain":"Blocking is planned with the director: where each actor stands and moves, and when."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In one production of Macbeth, the audience sees a glowing dagger. In another, the air is empty. What does the empty air suggest?","choices":["The dagger is in his mind, a sign of guilt","The dagger is really there","The director forgot the prop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Macbeth himself asks if it is \"a dagger of the mind\".","explain":"If only Macbeth sees it, the production suggests his guilty mind creates it, which the words \"a dagger of the mind\" support."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Two productions of a play each make {c} choices about light, set and acting. If they make every choice differently, how many different choices are there altogether?","answer":"2*c","hint":"Each production has its own {c} choices. Add them.","explain":"{c} + {c} = {=2*c} different choices. The script is the same, but each set of choices gives a new interpretation.","params":{"c":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A director stages a play in a bare space with one chair. What kind of set is this?","choices":["Minimalist","Naturalistic","Expressionist"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it copy a real room, distort it, or use very little?","explain":"A minimalist set uses very little, which keeps the focus on the actors and allows quick scene changes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A production sets Romeo and Juliet in a modern city but keeps every word. What can you say about it?","choices":["It departs from the setting but keeps the script's words","It is not Romeo and Juliet any more","It is exactly the same as the original"],"answer":0,"hint":"Separate what changed (where and when) from what stayed (the text).","explain":"The setting departs from Shakespeare's Verona, but the words are faithful. You can then judge whether the new setting helps the play's meaning."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"The lighting plan has {a} cues in Act 1 and {b} cues in Act 2. How many lighting cues does the operator run in the whole show?","answer":"a+b","hint":"A cue is one planned change of light. Add the two acts.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} cues. Each cue is a choice about mood or focus, so lighting is planned as carefully as the acting.","params":{"a":[8,20],"b":[6,18]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"An actor is told to move downstage. Where does she go?","choices":["Towards the audience","Towards the back of the stage","Under the stage"],"answer":0,"hint":"Old stages sloped down towards the seats.","explain":"Downstage is the part of the stage nearest the audience. Upstage is the back."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A play has {s} scenes, each in a different place. A naturalistic production builds a full, detailed set for each one. How many set changes are needed between scenes?","answer":"s-1","hint":"The first set is already in place when the play starts. Count the changes after that.","explain":"The first scene starts on its set, so {s} scenes need {=s-1} changes. That is why many productions choose a minimalist set instead.","params":{"s":[4,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The line is \"I'm so happy for you.\" The actor says it through gritted teeth and walks away. What is the subtext?","choices":["She is not happy for them","She is very happy for them","There is no subtext"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do her voice and movement agree with her words?","explain":"Gritted teeth and walking away contradict the words, so the audience reads that she is not really happy."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A scene has {n} lines in the script. The director cuts {c} of them to speed it up. How many lines are still spoken?","answer":"n-c","hint":"Start with all the lines in the script and take away the cuts.","explain":"{n} - {c} = {=n-c} lines are spoken. Cutting lines is one way a production can depart from the script, so ask whether the cuts change the meaning.","params":{"n":[40,80],"c":[5,15]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A script gives the words. It does not tell you everything. Sarah says, \"I'm fine.\" If she smiles and hugs Tom, she means it. If she pauses, looks down and turns away, she is hurt. The words stay the same. The meaning changes.","That hidden meaning is called subtext. Actors show it with their voice, their pauses and their body. Directors and designers add more: light, sound, the set and where people stand.","Stage directions use the actor's view. Stage left is the actor's left as they face the audience, so from your seat it is on the right. Downstage is the front, nearest you. Upstage is the back.","Two productions can stage one scene in very different ways. In one Macbeth, we see a glowing dagger. In another, the air is empty and his hand shakes. Both use the same words, but they suggest different things."],"adult":["This topic is about plays as performance, not just text. The English national curriculum for Key Stage 3 asks pupils to see how a play is brought to life on stage. It also asks how different staging can give different readings of the same play. In the US Common Core, grades 6 to 8 compare a script with a staged or filmed version. They judge how far it stays true to the script (RL.6.7 to RL.8.7).","Key terms. Subtext is the meaning under the words. Stage directions are the playwright's written notes. Blocking is where actors stand and move. Proxemics is the space between characters: far apart can show conflict. A naturalistic set copies a real place. A minimalist set uses very little. An expressionist set bends shapes and colours to show a character's mind.","Common mistakes. Pupils often treat the script as the whole play and only analyse the words. Many mix up stage left and stage right. These are given from the actor's view, facing the audience. Some think downstage means under the stage, but it is the front. Be careful, too, with calling a production wrong just because it is different. Ask instead: do the words support this choice?","In the lab your child directs one line to show two meanings and lights three scenes. They follow stage directions on a grid seen from the seats, match sets to a director's aim, and sort four stagings of Macbeth's dagger."]},"familyTip":"Watch two different clips of the same scene from a play online (Shakespeare scenes are easy to find). Ask: what did each director or actor choose differently, and which version did you believe more? Why?","keyIdea":"A play script is only part of a play. The director, designers and actors make choices that give the words their meaning on stage: an actor's tone, pauses and body language show the subtext, lighting sets the mood and tells the audience where to look, blocking places actors on stage, and the set tells the audience what kind of world they are in. Because each production makes different choices, two productions of the same script can interpret it differently, and both can still be faithful to the text if the words support them.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Who makes this choice? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✍️ The playwright, in the script","🎬 The production, on stage"],"items":[["The words the characters say",0],["A cold blue light for the ghost scene",1],["Exit, pursued by a bear (a stage direction)",0],["The actor pauses before answering",1],["The order of the scenes",0],["Setting the play in a modern office",1],["A list of the characters",0],["The two lovers stand far apart",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagstage","caption":"Flagship lab: direct one line two ways, light three scenes, follow stage directions, choose a set and sort four stagings of Macbeth's dagger.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ujwtRoYJ34":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a group of lines in a poem called?","choices":["A stanza","A chapter","A scene"],"answer":0,"hint":"Chapters are for novels and scenes are for plays.","explain":"A group of lines in a poem, set off by a gap, is a stanza."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A page has character names with colons and words in brackets like (She opens the door.). What kind of text is it?","choices":["A play script","A poem","A novel"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which kind of text has stage directions?","explain":"Character names and stage directions in brackets show a play script, which is split into acts and scenes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A play has {a} acts, and each act has 3 scenes. How many scenes are in the play?","answer":"3*a","hint":"Each act holds 3 scenes. Multiply.","explain":"{a} acts with 3 scenes each make {=3*a} scenes.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Put these in order from biggest to smallest: scene, play, act.","choices":["Play, act, scene","Scene, act, play","Act, play, scene"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part holds all the others?","explain":"The play holds the acts, and each act holds scenes: play, act, scene."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Stanza 1 of a poem is about a calm sea. Stanza 2 is about a storm. How does stanza 2 build on stanza 1?","choices":["It changes the mood from calm to wild","It repeats stanza 1 word for word","It has nothing to do with stanza 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the feeling of the two stanzas.","explain":"The same sea goes from calm to stormy, so stanza 2 builds on stanza 1 by changing its mood."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A poem has {s} stanzas with 5 lines in each. How many stanzas does it have?","answer":"s","hint":"The question asks for stanzas, not lines.","explain":"It has {s} stanzas. The {=5*s} lines are the total number of lines, not stanzas.","params":{"s":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which word in a new chapter shows that it builds on an earlier one?","choices":["\"Still holding the key, Ama...\"","\"Once upon a time...\"","\"Chapter 1\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a word that points back to something that already happened.","explain":"\"Still\" points back: the key was found in an earlier chapter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In a play, the action moves from a kitchen to a garden the next day. What usually starts?","choices":["A new scene","A new stanza","A new chapter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part of a play often changes with the place or time?","explain":"A new place or time in a play usually means a new scene."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A play script says \"ACT 2, SCENE 1\". Which part is bigger?","choices":["Act 2","Scene 1","They are the same size"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part holds the other one inside it?","explain":"An act is a big part of a play that holds several scenes, so Act 2 is bigger."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A poem has {s} stanzas, and each stanza has 4 lines. How many lines does the poem have?","answer":"4*s","hint":"Each stanza is a group of 4 lines. Multiply by the number of stanzas.","explain":"{s} stanzas of 4 lines make {=4*s} lines. There are still only {s} stanzas.","params":{"s":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Chapter 4 begins: \"Still wet from the river, Sam ran home.\" What does this tell you?","choices":["An earlier chapter showed Sam in the river","This is the first chapter","The chapters are not linked"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word points back to something that already happened?","explain":"\"Still wet\" points back. An earlier chapter must show how Sam got wet, so Chapter 4 builds on it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A poem has {=3*k} lines split into stanzas of 3 lines each. How many stanzas does it have?","answer":"k","hint":"Each stanza is a group of 3 lines. How many groups of 3 fit in {=3*k}?","explain":"{=3*k} lines in groups of 3 make {k} stanzas.","params":{"k":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A novel is a long story. It is split into chapters. Each chapter has a number, and often a title, like Chapter 3: The Gate.","A play is split into acts, and each act is split into scenes. A new scene often means a new place or time. In the script you see the names of the characters and stage directions in brackets.","A poem is split into stanzas. A stanza is a group of lines with a gap after it. If a poem has 12 lines in 3 groups, it has 3 stanzas, not 12.","Each part builds on the parts before. In Chapter 3, Ama is still holding the key she found in Chapter 1. In a poem, the second stanza can change the mood of the first. Say which part you mean and how it links back."],"adult":["This matches Common Core RL.3.5: refer to parts of stories, dramas and poems using terms such as chapter, scene and stanza, and describe how each part builds on earlier sections. In England it sits in the Year 3 and 4 reading programme. Pupils learn to recognise some forms of poetry, to tell prose, play scripts and poetry apart, and to see how structure helps meaning.","Key terms: prose, novel, chapter and paragraph; play script, act, scene, cast list, dialogue and stage directions; poem, stanza, line and verse. A stanza is a group of lines set off by a blank line. Poets sometimes say verse for a stanza, but verse can also mean poetry in general. The parts nest: a play holds acts, an act holds scenes, and a scene holds lines of dialogue.","Common mistakes: counting lines when asked for stanzas; calling every part a chapter, even in a poem or play; thinking a scene is bigger than an act; and treating each chapter or stanza as a stand-alone piece. Ask your child to find the words that point back, such as still, again or once more.","In the lab, learners name a part from how the page looks, number the stanzas of two poems before they count them, and order the parts of a play, a novel and a poem by size. They put four mixed-up chapters in order using clues that point back, and say how each stanza of a short sea poem builds on the one before."]},"familyTip":"When you read a book or poem together, ask: which chapter or stanza is this, and what does it add to the one before? On a play or film night, spot when a new scene starts: a new place or a new time.","keyIdea":"Each kind of text has its own words for its parts. A novel is split into chapters, a play into acts and scenes, and a poem into stanzas, which are groups of lines. Big parts hold smaller parts: an act holds scenes, a chapter holds paragraphs. Each new part builds on the ones before, so when you talk about a text, name the part and say what it adds to what came earlier.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which kind of text has this part? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📖 Novel","🎭 Play","📝 Poem"],"items":[["Chapter",0],["Scene",1],["Stanza",2],["Act",1],["Paragraph",0],["Stage directions",1],["Groups of lines with gaps",2],["Numbered chapter titles",0],["Rhyming lines",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagparts","caption":"Flagship lab: name text parts, count stanzas, order parts by size, put chapters in order and see how stanzas build.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_OlhMP7ShFT":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Can a picture tell you something the words do not?","choices":["Yes, it can add more","No, it only copies the words","Only in fact books"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the rain in Max's picture.","explain":"Pictures often add details the words leave out, like the weather or what someone looks like."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Same words: \"Sam opened the gate.\" One picture is dark with shadows. One is bright and sunny. Which feels scary?","choices":["The dark one","The sunny one","They feel the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"How do dark and shadows make you feel?","explain":"The words are the same, but the dark picture with shadows sets a scary mood."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is a caption?","choices":["A sentence that tells what a picture shows","One word naming a part","The name of the book"],"answer":0,"hint":"It sits under a photo in a fact book.","explain":"A caption is a sentence about the picture. A label is one word naming a part, and the book's name is its title."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In Rosie's Walk, the words never say \"fox\". How do we know a fox is there?","choices":["From the pictures","From the title","We cannot know"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look where the words do not.","explain":"The fox is only in the pictures. That is why you need to read the pictures too."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A page in a fact book has {p} photos. Each photo has one caption under it. How many captions are on the page?","answer":"p","hint":"One caption goes with each photo.","explain":"{p} photos, one caption each, makes {p} captions. Each caption tells what its photo shows.","params":{"p":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The words say \"Lola was happy.\" Which picture matches?","choices":["Lola smiling","Lola crying","Lola looking cross"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a happy face look like?","explain":"A smile matches happy. A picture that does not match the words would confuse the reader."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A diagram of a plant has {a} labels on the flower and {b} on the roots. How many labels in all?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the labels on both parts.","explain":"{a} and {b} make {=a+b} labels. Each one names a part of the plant.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,2]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why should you look at the pictures as well as read the words?","choices":["They can add to the story","They are just for fun","They always say the same as the words"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the fox following Hen.","explain":"Pictures can add detail, set the mood or tell a second story, so reading both gives you the whole story."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The words say \"Ben went to the park.\" The picture shows Ben in a red coat, kicking leaves. What do you learn only from the picture?","choices":["Ben has a red coat","Ben went to the park","Ben's name"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which fact is not in the words at all?","explain":"The words tell you where Ben went. The red coat and the leaves come only from the picture."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A picture has {n} ducks. The words say \"Some ducks swam.\" How many ducks does the picture tell you about?","answer":"n","hint":"The words say \"some\". Count the ducks in the picture.","explain":"The words do not give a number, but the picture shows {n} ducks. The picture adds that detail.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The words say \"It was a cold, snowy night.\" Which picture matches?","choices":["Snow falling under a moon","A sunny beach","Children in a hot garden"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the words cold, snowy and night in the picture.","explain":"Snow and a moon match \"cold, snowy night\". The beach and the hot garden do not."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A bee diagram has {k} labels. Each label names one part. How many parts are named?","answer":"k","hint":"One label names one part.","explain":"{k} labels name {k} parts. A label is a word that names a part of the picture.","params":{"k":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Read the words. Then look at the picture. The words say, \"Max the dog wants to play.\" The picture shows rain. The words never said rain! The picture must still match the words: a hot sun, not a moon.","Pictures can change how a page feels. \"Mia walked into the woods.\" A dark, stormy picture feels scary. A sunny one feels happy. The words stay the same.","In some books, the pictures tell a secret. The words only talk about Hen. The pictures show a fox following her!","Fact books have labels and captions. A label names a part, like wing. A caption says what the picture shows."],"adult":["This is reading comprehension through the picture-text relationship. US Common Core RL.1.7 asks children to use illustrations and details to describe characters, setting or events, RI.1.6 to tell information from pictures apart from information in the words, and RL.3.7 to explain how illustrations create mood or emphasise a character or setting. RI.2.7 adds how images such as diagrams clarify a text. England's Key Stage 1 reading asks children to discuss what they read and check it makes sense, which includes the pictures.","Researchers on picturebooks describe three main relationships. The picture can match the words, extend them by adding detail the words leave out, or even counterpoint them by telling something different. Pat Hutchins' Rosie's Walk is a classic case: the words follow Rosie the hen round the farmyard and never mention the fox, which appears only in the illustrations.","Common mistakes: reading only the words and skipping the pictures, treating pictures as decoration, assuming the picture just repeats the words, and mixing up labels (a single word naming a part) with captions (a sentence about the whole picture). A common adult slip is covering the pictures to make a child 'really read'; for comprehension, ask \"What does the picture tell us that the words do not?\"","In the lab your child sorts facts by whether they came from the words, the picture or both, illustrates three sentences by choosing matching pictures, changes sky, weather and a face to make the same sentence feel scary and then happy, finds the fox the words never mention on three pages, and labels a bee before choosing its caption."]},"familyTip":"At story time, pause on a page and ask, \"What can you see in the picture that the words did not tell us?\" Try Rosie's Walk by Pat Hutchins together and let your child tell you the fox's side of the story.","keyIdea":"In a book, the words and the pictures work together. Sometimes the picture shows what the words say. Often it adds more: what a character looks like, the weather, the place, or how a moment feels. Some books tell a second story only in the pictures, so you need to read both. In fact books, a label names a part of a picture or diagram, and a caption is a sentence that tells you what the picture shows.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Label or caption? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🏷 Label","💬 Caption"],"items":[["wing",0],["A bee drinks nectar from a flower.",1],["root",0],["A frog can jump far on its long back legs.",1],["stem",0],["The castle was built about 900 years ago.",1],["tail",0],["Penguins huddle together to keep warm.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpic","caption":"Flagship lab: words or picture, be the illustrator, set the mood of a page, find what the words leave out, label a bee and pick its caption.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_oVwNnjYPUY":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which signal words point to compare and contrast?","choices":["However, both, unlike","First, next, finally","Because, so, as a result"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which words set two things side by side?","explain":"However, both and unlike show how things are alike or different. First and next show time order; because and so show cause and effect."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"'Our school threw away lots of food. To fix this, we started a compost heap.' Which structure is this?","choices":["Problem and solution","Cause and effect","Time order"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is something wrong, and does someone fix it?","explain":"Wasted food is the problem and the compost heap is the fix. 'To fix this' is a problem and solution signal."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A cause chain has {n} events, and an arrow joins each event to the one it causes. How many arrows are in the chain?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Draw it: one arrow between each pair of events next to each other.","explain":"{n} events in a row need {=n-1} arrows, one between each event and the one it leads to.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"'The town flooded because the river burst its banks.' What happened first?","choices":["The river burst its banks","The town flooded","Both at the same time"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Because' points to the cause. The cause comes first in time.","explain":"The flood is written first, but 'because' shows the river bursting caused it. The cause happened first."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"An author wants to explain how a caterpillar becomes a butterfly. Which structure fits best?","choices":["Time order","Compare and contrast","Problem and solution"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the reader learning steps, differences, or a fix?","explain":"A life cycle is a set of stages in order, so time order fits. A timeline would be the organiser."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A time-order text about growing a seed has {n} steps. You have read {k} of them. How many steps are left to read?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the steps you have read away from all the steps.","explain":"{n} steps take away {k} read leaves {=n-k} steps still to read, in order.","params":{"n":[5,9],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Two texts are both about bees. One tells a bee's life in order; the other compares honeybees and bumblebees. What does this show?","choices":["The author picks the structure, not the topic","Bee texts are always compare and contrast","One of the texts must be wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same topic, different plans. Who chose the plan?","explain":"One topic can be written in any structure. The author picks the one that fits what they want readers to understand."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which graphic organiser fits a compare and contrast text?","choices":["A Venn diagram","A timeline","A cause arrow"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one shows what is the same and what is different?","explain":"A Venn diagram has a part for each thing and a middle for what they share. A timeline is for time order."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Structure of information texts: 'Heavy rain fell for a week. As a result, the river burst its banks.' Which structure is this?","choices":["Cause and effect","Compare and contrast","Problem and solution"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the signal words 'As a result'. Do they give a reason, a difference, or a fix?","explain":"'As a result' links a cause (the rain) to its effect (the flood). Nothing is fixed and nothing is compared."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A time-order text has {n} steps. Step 1 starts with 'First' and the last step starts with 'Finally'. How many middle steps need other signal words, like next or then?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Take away the first step and the last step.","explain":"{n} steps take away the first and the last leaves {=n-2} middle steps, each with a signal word like next, then or after that.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"'First, frogs have smooth wet skin. However, toads have dry bumpy skin.' Which structure is this?","choices":["Compare and contrast","Time order","Cause and effect"],"answer":0,"hint":"'First' starts a list here. What does 'However' do?","explain":"'However' sets frogs against toads, so the text compares them. One word like first does not make a text time order."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A Venn diagram compares frogs and toads. It has {a} facts only about frogs, {b} only about toads, and {c} in the middle that are true of both. How many facts are true of frogs?","answer":"a+c","hint":"Frog facts are in the frog-only part and in the middle part.","explain":"{a} frog-only facts plus {c} shared facts makes {=a+c} facts true of frogs. The {b} toad-only facts do not count.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,5],"c":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Every information text has a plan, called its structure. There are four main ones. Time order tells steps or events in order. Compare and contrast shows how two things are alike and different. Cause and effect tells why something happens. Problem and solution says what is wrong and how to fix it.","Signal words are clues. First, next and finally point to time order. However, both and unlike point to compare. Because, so and as a result point to cause and effect. The problem and the solution point to problem and solution.","One word is not enough. A text that starts with first might still compare frogs and toads. Look at how the whole text is built. And watch out: writers often say the effect first. In 'Schools closed because the streets flooded', the flood came first.","The topic does not pick the structure. The author does. A text about bees could tell a bee's life in order, or compare honeybees and bumblebees. Ask: what does the author want me to understand?"],"adult":["This topic matches the US Common Core reading standard RI.4.5: describe the overall structure of events, ideas or information in a text, such as chronology, comparison, cause and effect, or problem and solution. In England, the Year 3 to 6 reading programme asks pupils to identify how structure and presentation add to meaning in non-fiction. At 9 to 10, pupils should name the structure, point to the words that show it, and say why it suits the author's purpose.","Key terms: text structure, chronological order (also called sequence), compare and contrast, cause and effect, problem and solution, signal words (also called transition words), graphic organiser and author's purpose. Some schemes add a fifth, description. Each structure has a matching organiser: a timeline, a Venn diagram, a cause arrow and a problem box.","Common mistakes: naming the structure from one signal word, such as calling a text time order because it starts with first. Thinking the topic decides the structure. Reading events in the order they are written, when 'because' puts the effect first. Mixing up problem and solution with cause and effect: both have a reason, but only one has a fix. Be careful with longer texts, which often mix structures; ask about the main one.","In the lab the learner taps the signal words in three paragraphs, sorts passages into the four structures, and fits four bee passages to their graphic organisers. Then they rebuild cause chains that are written effect first, and pick the structure that fits each author's goal."]},"familyTip":"Pick any non-fiction page, a recipe or a news story. Hunt for signal words together, then ask: is this telling us the steps, comparing, explaining why, or solving a problem? Then try saying the same facts in a different structure.","keyIdea":"Information texts are built in one of four main structures. Time order (chronology) tells events or steps in order. Compare and contrast shows how things are alike and different. Cause and effect explains why something happens and what it leads to. Problem and solution names what is wrong and how it was fixed. Signal words such as first, however, because and the solution give clues, but the whole text decides the structure, and the author picks the one that fits their goal.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which structure do these signal words point to? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Time order","Compare","Cause and effect","Problem and solution"],"items":[["first, next, finally",0],["after that, later",0],["however, both, unlike",1],["in contrast, similarly",1],["because, so, as a result",2],["this led to, therefore",2],["the problem is, to fix this",3],["one solution, this was solved by",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtstr","caption":"Flagship lab: tap signal words, sort passages into four structures, fit bee texts to organisers, rebuild cause chains and match structure to goal.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_v33BwiyRnd":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which one is a lesson, not something that happens?","choices":["Slow and steady wins the race","The hare had a nap","The tortoise won"],"answer":0,"hint":"A lesson is a rule you can use in your own life.","explain":"\"Slow and steady wins the race\" is a rule for life. The nap and the win are events in the story."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The Lion and the Mouse: a lion lets a mouse go, and later the mouse saves him. What is the moral?","choices":["Kind acts can come back to help you","Lions are bigger than mice","The mouse chewed a net"],"answer":0,"hint":"One choice is a fact, one is an event. Which is a lesson?","explain":"The lion was kind, and later his kindness helped him. Size is a fact, and chewing the net is an event."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which detail proves \"slow and steady wins the race\"?","choices":["The tortoise keeps walking and never stops","The race goes past a tree","The hare has long ears"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick a detail about what a character does.","explain":"The tortoise never stops, and it wins. That shows steady wins. The tree and the ears prove nothing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is the best way to say a moral?","choices":["Tell the truth, so people trust you","The boy should not shout \"Wolf!\"","The sheep were in a field"],"answer":0,"hint":"A moral should help anyone, not just one character.","explain":"\"Tell the truth, so people trust you\" fits anyone. The second only fits the boy, and the third is a detail."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Where do you look to find the lesson of a fable?","choices":["A choice and what happens after it","Only the title","The first line"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab you built: choice, then result, then lesson.","explain":"The grasshopper chose to play, and then he went hungry. A choice and its result show the lesson."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Anansi hid all the wisdom in a pot, but his small son had a better idea. What is the lesson?","choices":["Nobody knows everything","Spiders can climb trees","Anansi had a pot"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did Anansi find out from his son?","explain":"Even a small child knew something Anansi did not. So nobody knows everything. The other two are facts."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"The hare runs for {n} minutes, then naps for {s} minutes. How many minutes is that in all?","answer":"n+s","hint":"Add the running time and the nap time.","explain":"{n} + {s} = {=n+s} minutes. For {s} of them the hare did not move, so the steady tortoise caught up.","params":{"n":[2,6],"s":[5,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"The boy cries \"Wolf!\" as a trick {n} times. Then he cries it once when a wolf is really there. How many times does he cry \"Wolf!\"?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count the {n} tricks, then add the 1 real time.","explain":"{n} + 1 = {=n+1} times. After {n} tricks, no one believed the real one.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Sam says, \"The moral is: the tortoise beat the hare.\" What is wrong?","choices":["That is what happens, not what it teaches","Nothing, that is the moral","The hare won, not the tortoise"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is Sam telling an event or a rule for life?","explain":"Sam gave a summary of what happens. The moral is what it teaches: slow and steady wins the race."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is the moral of a fable?","choices":["The lesson the story teaches","The name of the main animal","Everything that happens, in order"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of \"slow and steady wins the race\". Is it a name, a list of events, or a lesson?","explain":"The moral is the lesson the story teaches. Telling everything in order is a recount, not the moral."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The tortoise takes {n} steps every minute and never stops. How many steps does it take in 3 minutes?","answer":"n*3","hint":"Add {n} steps for each of the 3 minutes.","explain":"{n} + {n} + {n} = {=n*3} steps. Small, steady steps add up. That is why the tortoise wins.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The Boy Who Cried Wolf lied, so no one came when a real wolf came. Which is the moral?","choices":["If you tell lies, people stop believing you","The wolf ate the sheep","The boy looked after sheep"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two choices say what happens. Which one is a rule for anyone?","explain":"The moral is a rule for everyone: lies make people stop believing you. The other two are events in the story."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"All summer, the ant stores {n} seeds a day for {d} days. How many seeds does the ant have for winter?","answer":"n*d","hint":"{d} days, with {n} seeds each day. Multiply.","explain":"{n} × {d} = {=n*d} seeds. The ant got ready for later, and that is the lesson.","params":{"n":[2,5],"d":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A fable is a short story that teaches a lesson. The lesson is called the moral. Many fables have animals that talk.","The moral is not what happens. \"The tortoise won the race\" is what happens. \"Slow and steady wins the race\" is the lesson. You can use it in your own life.","To find the moral, look at a choice. The hare chose to nap. The tortoise chose to keep going. What happened? The tortoise won. So the lesson is to keep going.","Prove the moral with key details. The hare naps. The tortoise never stops. The tortoise gets to the end first. Each of those shows the lesson."],"adult":["Working out the moral of a story is a reading comprehension skill. In the US Common Core, RL.2.2 asks children to recount fables and folktales from diverse cultures and determine their central message, lesson or moral. RL.3.2 adds explaining how that message is conveyed through key details in the text. In England, Year 2 pupils retell fairy stories and traditional tales, and Years 3 and 4 identify themes across a wide range of books.","Useful words for this age: fable (a short story, often with animals, that teaches a lesson), folktale (a traditional story passed on by telling), moral or central message (what the story teaches), key details (the events and choices that show the message), and recount (retell in order). Fables are a good place to start, because the message is clear and usually follows one choice and its result.","The most common mistake is giving a summary instead of a message: \"The tortoise beat the hare\" says what happens, not what it teaches. Other slips are naming the topic (\"it is about animals\"), stating the moral so narrowly that it only fits one character (\"the boy should not shout wolf\"), and choosing a detail that is true but does not prove anything, such as where the race took place.","In the lab your child sorts \"what happens\" from \"the lesson\", taps the key details that prove \"slow and steady wins the race\", and builds a choice, result, lesson chain for The Ant and the Grasshopper. They pick the moral of folktales from Greece, Ghana and India, and turn a rule about one boy into a lesson for everyone. Every drag has a button twin."]},"familyTip":"After a bedtime story, ask two questions: \"What happened?\" and then \"What does it teach us?\" If the answer is still about the story, ask \"What should we do because of it?\" Then ask which part of the story shows it.","keyIdea":"A fable or folktale teaches a lesson, called the moral or central message. The moral is not what happens in the story. It is a rule you can use in your own life, like \"slow and steady wins the race\". You find it by asking what a character chose and what happened because of it, and you prove it with key details from the story.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What happens in the story, or the lesson it teaches? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📖 What happens","💡 The lesson"],"items":[["The hare took a nap",0],["Slow and steady wins the race",1],["The mouse set the lion free",0],["Kind acts can come back to help you",1],["The grasshopper sang all summer",0],["Get ready now for what you need later",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmoral","caption":"Flagship lab: sort events from lessons, prove a moral with key details, build choice, result, lesson, and find morals from three lands.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_gR5_n99Ntt":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A poem tells how a girl lost her kite and found it again. What form is it?","choices":["Narrative poem","Haiku","Not a poem, because it tells a story"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it have a character, a problem and an ending?","explain":"It tells a story with a character, a problem and an ending, so it is a narrative poem."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How many syllables are in the word butterfly?","choices":["3","1","9"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clap it slowly: but-ter-fly. Count the claps, not the letters.","explain":"But-ter-fly has 3 claps, so 3 syllables. It has 9 letters, but we count beats, not letters."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How does your voice usually go at the end of \"Is anyone there?\"","choices":["Up","Down","It stays flat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it as a real question. Listen to the last word.","explain":"A question like this usually ends with the voice going up. That rise and fall is intonation."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which poem must have 3 lines of 5, 7 and 5 syllables?","choices":["A haiku","A narrative poem","Free verse"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which form is the tiny one with a syllable rule?","explain":"A haiku has 3 lines of 5, 7 and 5 syllables. Narrative poems and free verse have no such rule."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How should you perform \"BANG! The door flies open wide!\"?","choices":["Loud and quick","In a whisper","Slow, with long pauses"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at BANG and the exclamation marks.","explain":"BANG in capitals and the ! show something sudden, so read it loud and quick."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A haiku has 3 lines. How many syllables are in {n} haiku altogether?","answer":"17*n","hint":"One haiku is 5 + 7 + 5 syllables.","explain":"One haiku is 5 + 7 + 5 = 17 syllables, so {n} haiku have 17 × {n} = {=17*n} syllables.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A narrative poem has {n} stanzas of 4 lines each. How many lines is that?","answer":"4*n","hint":"Each stanza is a group of 4 lines.","explain":"{n} stanzas of 4 lines is 4 × {n} = {=4*n} lines.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which feature tells you a poem is free verse?","choices":["No regular rhyme or rhythm","Exactly 17 syllables","It must tell a story"],"answer":0,"hint":"Free verse is free from which rules?","explain":"Free verse has no regular rhyme or rhythm. The poet chooses line lengths freely."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A poem has no rhyme and no steady beat. What form could it be?","choices":["Free verse","Haiku","It is not a poem"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which form has no regular rhyme or rhythm?","explain":"Free verse has no regular rhyme or rhythm. It is still a poem: the poet chooses where each line breaks."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A haiku's first line has {a} syllables so far. It needs 5. How many more syllables does it need?","answer":"5-a","hint":"The first line of a haiku has 5 syllables. Take away what you have.","explain":"5 take away {a} is {=5-a}, so add {=5-a} more syllables. A haiku is 5, 7, then 5.","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How should you read the line \"Shh… the baby is asleep\" aloud?","choices":["Soft and slow","Loud and fast","Shout the last word"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does Shh ask you to do? What do the dots ask for?","explain":"Shh and asleep tell you to be quiet, and the dots ask for a pause. So read it soft and slow."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A haiku's middle line has {a} syllables. How many more does it need to make 7?","answer":"7-a","hint":"The middle line of a haiku has 7 syllables, not 5.","explain":"7 take away {a} is {=7-a}, so it needs {=7-a} more. The middle line is the long one.","params":{"a":[3,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A poem's form is the kind of poem it is. Each form has features, things you can spot. So to name the form, look for the features.","A haiku is tiny. It has 3 lines: 5 syllables, then 7, then 5. A syllable is a beat you can clap, so but-ter-fly has 3. Count beats, not words.","A narrative poem tells a story. Look for a character, a problem and an ending. Free verse has no regular rhyme or beat. It does not have to rhyme to be a poem.","When you perform a poem, your voice shows what it means. Read quiet lines soft and slow, and sudden lines loud. Pause at dots and commas. Let your voice go up at the end of a question."],"adult":["This matches Years 3 and 4 in the national curriculum for England. Pupils recognise some different forms of poetry, such as free verse and narrative poetry. They also prepare poems and play scripts to read aloud and perform, showing understanding through intonation, tone, volume and action. CCSS RL.4.5 asks pupils to refer to the structural elements of poems, such as verse, rhythm and meter. RF.3.4b asks them to read with expression.","Key terms: form (the type of poem), line and stanza, syllable (a beat in a word), rhyme, rhythm or meter (a regular pattern of beats), haiku, narrative poem and free verse. For performance: volume (loud or soft), pace (speed and pauses), tone (the feeling in the voice) and intonation (the rise and fall of the voice). The English haiku is usually 3 lines of 5, 7 and 5 syllables. Japanese haiku count sound units called on, and often name a season.","Common mistakes: thinking a poem must rhyme, so free verse is not a real poem. Counting words or letters instead of syllables in a haiku. Thinking a poem that tells a story is not a poem at all. In performance, the usual slip is reading every line in the same flat voice, or racing through the dots and dashes that ask for a pause. Famous narrative poems to share include The Highwayman by Alfred Noyes and The Pied Piper of Hamelin by Robert Browning.","In the lab your child sorts three short poems into haiku, narrative poem and free verse, and fills a feature chart. They clap the syllables of a haiku and fix its middle line to 7. They find the character, problem, ending and rhyme in a story poem. Then they choose a voice for each line of a spooky poem and perform it to a listener who reacts. Every tap has a button twin."]},"familyTip":"Pick a short poem together and read it twice: once in a flat robot voice, then again with soft parts, loud parts and pauses. Ask which reading made the meaning clearer, and why.","keyIdea":"Poems come in different forms, and each form has its own features. A haiku has 3 lines of 5, 7 and 5 syllables and shows one moment. A narrative poem tells a story, with a character, a problem and an ending, and often rhymes. Free verse has no regular rhyme or rhythm, but it is still a poem. To perform a poem, match your voice to the words: change the volume, pace and tone, pause where the punctuation tells you, and let your voice rise at the end of a question.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which feature belongs to which form? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Haiku","Narrative poem","Free verse"],"items":[["3 lines: 5, 7 and 5 syllables",0],["Has a character and a plot",1],["No regular rhyme or beat",2],["Shows one small moment",0],["Tells what happened, then how it ended",1],["Lines of any length",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpoem","caption":"Flagship lab: sort poems by form, clap a haiku to 5-7-5, find the story in a narrative poem, and perform a poem.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ZhUuT__i2H":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does the index tell you?","choices":["Which pages a word is on","What a word means","Who drew the pictures"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of “pond 6, 7” in the lab.","explain":"The index lists words in ABC order with the pages they are on. The glossary says what words mean."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The index says “tadpole 15, 16”. What does that mean?","choices":["Pages 15 and 16 tell you about tadpoles","There are 16 tadpoles","Tadpole is chapter 15"],"answer":0,"hint":"The numbers in an index are page numbers.","explain":"Pages 15 and 16 tell you about tadpoles. Index numbers are pages, not chapters or counts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which word comes first in an index?","choices":["ant","frog","worm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first letters: a, f, w. Which comes first in ABC order?","explain":"The index is in ABC order. a comes before f and w, so ant comes first."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A word in a fact book is in bold. Why?","choices":["It is a special word in the glossary","It is the name of the author","It is the last word"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where can you find out what a bold word means?","explain":"Bold words are special words. You can find what they mean in the glossary."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A page says: “First, an egg. Next, a tadpole. Last, a frog.” How is it built?","choices":["Time order","A list","Question and answer"],"answer":0,"hint":"First, next and last are time words.","explain":"The steps go in order with first, next and last, so it is time order."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What does a heading tell you?","choices":["What that part is about","What a word means","Which page is next"],"answer":0,"hint":"A heading sits at the top of a part.","explain":"A heading tells you what that part is about, so you can skip to the part you need."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Chapter 4 starts on page {a}. It is {k} pages long. On what page does chapter 5 start?","answer":"a+k","hint":"Count on {k} pages from page {a}.","explain":"Page {a} and {k} pages more is page {=a+k}, so chapter 5 starts on page {=a+k}.","params":{"a":[6,14],"k":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You want the chapter on what frogs eat. Which part helps you most?","choices":["The contents page","The glossary","The back cover"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part lists the chapters?","explain":"The contents page lists the chapters and the page each starts on, so you can turn straight there."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"The index says “eggs {a}”. You are on page {=a+b}. How many pages back do you turn?","answer":"b","hint":"Count back from page {=a+b} to page {a}.","explain":"From page {=a+b} back to page {a} is {=b} pages back.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where in a fact book is the contents page?","choices":["At the front","At the back","In the middle"],"answer":0,"hint":"You look at it before you start, to choose a chapter.","explain":"The contents page is at the front. The index and the glossary are usually at the back."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The contents page says chapter 2 starts on page {a} and chapter 3 starts on page {=a+k}. How many pages long is chapter 2?","answer":"k","hint":"Count on from page {a} to page {=a+k}.","explain":"From page {a} to page {=a+k} is {=k} pages, so chapter 2 is {=k} pages long.","params":{"a":[2,10],"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You want to know what “amphibian” means. Where do you look?","choices":["The glossary","The contents page","The cover"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part tells you what special words mean?","explain":"The glossary tells you what special words mean. The contents page only lists chapters."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You are on page {a}. The index says ponds are on page {=a+b}. How many pages forward do you turn?","answer":"b","hint":"Count on from page {a} to page {=a+b}.","explain":"From page {a} to page {=a+b} is {=b} pages forward.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A fact book tells you true things. You do not have to read it all. It has parts that help you find what you want.","The contents page is at the front. It lists the chapters and the page each one starts on. Want to read about what frogs eat? Find “What frogs eat … 10”. Then turn to page 10.","The index is at the back. It lists words in ABC order. Next to each word are the pages it is on. “pond 6, 7” means pages 6 and 7 tell you about ponds. The numbers are pages, not how many ponds.","Some special words are in bold. Bold means thick, dark letters. The glossary tells you what bold words mean. A heading at the top of a part tells you what it is about. Some pages are a list. Some go in time order: first, next, last. Some ask a question, then answer it."],"adult":["This lesson teaches children to find their way round an information book using its text features. It matches Common Core RI.1.5 and RI.2.5 (know and use text features such as headings, tables of contents, glossaries, bold print and indexes to locate key facts) and RI.3.5 (use text features and search tools efficiently). In England, Year 2 pupils are introduced to non-fiction books structured in different ways, and lower key stage 2 pupils retrieve and record information from non-fiction.","Key terms for ages 6 to 9: non-fiction, information text, text features, contents page (table of contents), chapter, page number, index, alphabetical (ABC) order, glossary, bold print, heading and subheading. Text structures at this age are list (facts in any order), chronological or time order (first, next, then, last) and question and answer.","Common mistakes: mixing up the contents page and the index, so a child hunts the contents for a single word. Reading the numbers in an index as chapters or as counts, rather than page numbers. Searching an index from the top instead of jumping to the right letter. And expecting the glossary to say where a word appears, or the index to say what it means.","In the lab your child uses one book, All About Frogs. They tap chapters on the contents page, look words up in the index and drop a new word into its ABC place, and drag bold words onto their glossary meanings. Then they sort questions to the right feature and name how three pages are built. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Pick any fact book at home or at the library. Before reading, ask your child to find one thing using the contents page and one using the index. Say the page number out loud together, then check if you were right.","keyIdea":"Fact books have special parts that help you find things fast. The contents page is at the front: it lists the chapters in page order. The index is at the back: it lists words in ABC order with their page numbers. The glossary says what the special bold words mean. Headings tell you what each part is about. A fact book can be built as a list, in time order, or as questions and answers.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which part of the book helps? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📑 Contents page","🔤 Index","📖 Glossary"],"items":[["Find the chapter on frog homes",0],["How many chapters are there?",0],["Which pages say “eggs”?",1],["Find the page that says “croak”",1],["What does “spawn” mean?",2],["What does “predator” mean?",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnfind","caption":"Flagship lab: use a frog book's contents page, index and glossary, sort questions to each, and name how pages are built.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_sMAcZW6vWM":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A paragraph says frogs lay eggs in ponds, the eggs hatch into tadpoles, and tadpoles grow legs. What is its main idea?","choices":["How a frog grows from an egg","Frogs","Tadpoles grow legs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the thing that every sentence tells about.","explain":"The main idea is how a frog grows from an egg. Frogs is the topic, and tadpoles grow legs is one detail."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Main idea: how bees make honey. Which sentence is NOT a key detail?","choices":["Bees can sting.","Bees collect nectar from flowers.","They store it in wax cells in the hive."],"answer":0,"hint":"A key detail must tell about making honey.","explain":"Bees can sting is true, but it is not about making honey, so it is not a key detail here."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which sentence should NOT go in a summary of a text about rivers?","choices":["I think rivers are the best place to play.","Rivers start high up and flow to the sea.","Rivers carry water, soil and stones."],"answer":0,"hint":"A summary only says what the text says.","explain":"I think rivers are the best place to play is an opinion, so it does not go in a summary."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What makes a good summary?","choices":["The main ideas, short, in your own words","Every fact, copied out","Just the first paragraph"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the summary you built in the lab.","explain":"A good summary is the main ideas, short, in your own words. It leaves out small details."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A text has {n} paragraphs. You write one short main-idea sentence for each. How many sentences is your summary?","answer":"n","hint":"One sentence for each paragraph.","explain":"One sentence for each of {n} paragraphs makes a summary of {=n} sentences.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You read {n} pages and write {k} key-word notes on each page. How many notes do you write?","answer":"n*k","hint":"{k} notes for each of {n} pages. Multiply.","explain":"{n} pages with {k} notes on each makes {=n*k} notes.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You want to compare what lions, zebras and elephants eat. How should you record it?","choices":["A table","Numbered steps","One long sentence"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which way puts things side by side?","explain":"A table is best, because it puts the three animals side by side so you can compare them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why are notes key words and not whole sentences?","choices":["They are quick to write and easy to find again","Whole sentences are wrong","So nobody else can read them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about why you make notes at all.","explain":"Key words are quick to write and easy to find again. Copying whole sentences takes longer and skips the thinking."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"You jot down facts about sharks under the headings Body, Food and Home. What is this?","choices":["A spider diagram","Numbered steps","A summary"],"answer":0,"hint":"Facts about one thing, sorted under headings around it.","explain":"It is a spider diagram: facts about one thing, sorted under headings."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Every sentence in a paragraph tells about how owls hunt at night. What is its main idea?","choices":["How owls hunt at night","Owls","Owls have big eyes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask what all the sentences tell about, not just one of them.","explain":"The main idea is how owls hunt at night. “Owls” is only the topic, and big eyes is one detail."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An article has {n} paragraphs. Your summary keeps one main idea from each paragraph. How many main ideas are in your summary?","answer":"n","hint":"One main idea for each paragraph, so count the paragraphs.","explain":"One main idea from each of {n} paragraphs gives {=n} main ideas in the summary.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is the best note about what bats eat?","choices":["moths, midges, insects","They hunt moths, midges and other insects at night.","yucky bugs"],"answer":0,"hint":"A note is key words from the text, not a whole sentence or what you think.","explain":"moths, midges, insects is the best note: just the key words. The long one is copied, and yucky is an opinion."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Your notes table compares {a} animals. Each animal has a row with {b} facts in it. How many facts are in the table?","answer":"a*b","hint":"There are {a} rows and {b} facts in each row. Multiply.","explain":"{a} rows with {b} facts each makes {=a*b} facts in the table.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You want to record how a seed grows into a plant. What is the best way?","choices":["Numbered steps","A table","Copy out the whole page"],"answer":0,"hint":"A seed grows in an order: first, next, last.","explain":"Numbered steps are best, because a seed grows in order. A table is for comparing things side by side."}],"explain":{"kid":["Every paragraph is about one big thing. That is its main idea. Ask: what do all the sentences tell about? In a paragraph about what hedgehogs eat, each sentence tells about food. So the main idea is what hedgehogs eat.","Key details are the small facts that back up the main idea. A fact can be true and still not fit. “Hedgehogs can swim” is true. But it does not fit in a paragraph about food.","A summary is short. Take the main idea of each paragraph and say it in your own words. Leave out small details. Leave out what you think, too. Only say what the text says.","Notes are key words, not whole sentences. Put them where you can find them again. A table is good to compare things. Numbered steps are good for things in order. A spider diagram is good for facts about one thing."],"adult":["This lesson covers two linked reading skills. In England, upper key stage 2 pupils identify main ideas drawn from more than one paragraph and summarise them, and lower key stage 2 pupils retrieve and record information from non-fiction. In the US the match is Common Core RI.3.2, RI.4.2 and RI.5.2 (determine the main idea, explain how key details support it, and summarise the text).","Key terms for ages 7 to 10: topic, main idea, key details, supporting details, paragraph, summarise, summary, retrieve, record, notes, key words, table, numbered steps (sequence) and spider diagram (mind map). The topic is the subject in one or two words; the main idea is what the text says about that topic.","Common mistakes: giving the topic (“hedgehogs”) or one striking detail (“they walk 2 km”) instead of the main idea. Treating every true fact as a key detail. Writing a summary that is as long as the text, retells one paragraph only, or adds an opinion. Copying whole sentences as notes, which skips the thinking that makes notes useful.","In the lab your child reads a short hedgehog article. They drag the main idea of each paragraph into a box, tap the sentence that does not fit, and sort sentences into a summary or out of it. Then they record notes about bats in a table, bin the poor ones, and choose a table, steps or a spider diagram for three tasks. Every drag has a button twin."]},"familyTip":"After reading any short article together, cover it and ask your child to say each paragraph's main idea in five words or fewer. Then write three key-word notes together on a sticky note, and check them against the text.","keyIdea":"The main idea of a paragraph is what every sentence in it tells about. Key details back up the main idea. A summary joins the main ideas of several paragraphs in a few short sentences, in your own words. Notes are key words from the text, recorded in a table, in numbered steps or in a spider diagram, so you can find them again.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Main idea or key detail? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["💡 Main idea","🔎 Key detail"],"items":[["What hedgehogs eat and how they find it",0],["How hedgehogs get through winter",0],["How spines keep a hedgehog safe",0],["They eat beetles and slugs",1],["They hibernate from November to March",1],["They roll into a ball when scared",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaggist","caption":"Flagship lab: find each paragraph's main idea, spot the sentence that does not fit, build a summary, file notes in a table and choose how to record.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_8-POYyg7GJ":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A book is called “The Hungry Bear”. What will it be about?","choices":["A bear looking for food","A fish who can sing","A trip to the shops for shoes"],"answer":0,"hint":"The title is a clue. Look at both words: hungry and bear.","explain":"Hungry and bear tell you it is about a bear who wants food."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You guessed the boy will win the race. Then he trips and falls. What should you do?","choices":["Change my guess","Keep it, because I said it first","Stop reading the book"],"answer":0,"hint":"Something new happened. Is it a new clue?","explain":"The fall is a new clue. Good readers change the guess and read on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which of these is a clue about what will happen?","choices":["A picture of a big dark cloud","The number at the bottom of the page","How heavy the book is"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clues are in the words and pictures of the story.","explain":"A dark cloud is a clue that rain may come. Page numbers and weight tell you nothing about the story."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Zak’s guess turned out wrong. What does that mean?","choices":["Nothing bad. He reads on and checks.","He is bad at reading.","He should stop guessing."],"answer":0,"hint":"Do good readers always guess right?","explain":"Good readers often guess wrong. A guess helps you think, and you change it as you read."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"The cat sees a bird. The cat gets low and still. What will happen next?","choices":["The cat jumps at the bird","The cat reads a book","The cat bakes a cake"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do cats do when they get low and still near a bird?","explain":"Getting low and still is a clue that the cat is about to pounce."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ana says, “The ice will melt.” What should she add to make it a good prediction?","choices":["Because the sun is out","A big smile","Nothing, it is done"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good prediction says why. Which part is a reason?","explain":"Because the sun is out gives the clue, so it is a reasoned guess."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A story has {n} pictures. You find 2 clues in each picture. How many clues do you find?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each picture gives 2 clues. Count in twos.","explain":"{n} pictures with 2 clues each makes {=2*n} clues.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You guessed it is a birthday. The next page shows a cake with candles. What do you do?","choices":["Keep my guess, the cake fits it","Change my guess","Guess something new for fun"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the new page fit your guess or go against it?","explain":"A cake with candles fits a birthday, so you keep the guess."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Lily makes {a} guesses in a story. She keeps {b} of them because the next page fits. She changes the rest. How many does she change?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take away the guesses she keeps. The rest are the ones she changes.","explain":"{a} guesses take away {b} kept leaves {=a-b} changed. Keeping and changing are both good reading.","params":{"a":[6,10],"b":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ella puts on her coat and picks up her umbrella. What will she do next?","choices":["Go out in the rain","Have a bath","Go to sleep"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the clues. When do you need a coat and an umbrella?","explain":"A coat and an umbrella are clues that she is going out in the rain."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A book has {n} pages. You stop at the end of every page, but not the last one, to guess what happens next. How many guesses do you make?","answer":"n-1","hint":"After the last page there is nothing left to guess. Take one away.","explain":"{n} pages, but no guess after the last one, so {=n-1} guesses.","params":{"n":[4,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which prediction says why?","choices":["I think the tower will fall because it wobbles.","I think the tower will fall.","Towers are fun."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the word because. The part after it is the clue.","explain":"Because it wobbles is the reason. A good prediction tells you the clue."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Max makes {a} guesses while he reads. {b} of them turn out right. He changes the rest. How many guesses does he change?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the right guesses away from all the guesses.","explain":"{a} guesses take away {b} right ones leaves {=a-b} changed. Changing a guess is what good readers do.","params":{"a":[5,9],"b":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Stop before you turn the page. Ask: what will happen next? Your guess is called a prediction.","Look for clues. The title is a clue. The pictures are clues. What has happened so far is a clue too. A book called The Big Snow will be about cold and snow.","Say why. \"I think he is going to the beach because he has a spade and a sun hat.\" The part after because is your clue.","Then turn the page and check. Was it right? If something new happens, like rain, change your guess. Good readers change their guess all the time. That is not getting it wrong."],"adult":["The English National Curriculum asks Year 1 pupils to predict what might happen on the basis of what has been read so far, and to discuss the significance of the title and events. In Years 3 and 4 this grows into predicting from details stated and implied. The Early Years goal for comprehension asks children to anticipate key events in stories.","Prediction is an active comprehension strategy. The child joins what the text has told them (title, illustrations, events) with what they already know about the world, then checks the guess as they read. Asking 'What makes you think that?' turns a wild guess into a reasoned one. Revising a prediction when new evidence appears is the skill, not a failure.","Common mistakes: guessing with no link to the story (the dog will fly to the moon), ignoring the title and pictures, giving a guess without a reason, holding on to a first guess after the story has changed, and feeling that a wrong guess means they are bad at reading.","In the lab your child sorts clues from non-clues on a book cover, picks the guess that fits three short picture stories, finds the clue that backs a guess, and then reads a new page and decides to keep or change the guess. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime, stop before the last page and ask: 'What do you think will happen? What makes you think that?' Make your own guess too, and when one of you is wrong, say: 'Ooh, I need to change my guess!'","keyIdea":"A prediction is a guess about what will happen next in a story. A good prediction uses clues: the title, the pictures, and what has happened so far. Say why you think it: \"I think the ice cream will melt because the sun is hot.\" Then read on and check. If the story gives a new clue, change your guess. A guess that turns out wrong is not a mistake.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"The book is called “The Lost Puppy”. Is each thing a clue about what will happen? Drag it to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Clue","Not a clue"],"items":[["The word Lost",0],["A puppy alone in the rain",0],["A girl calling a name",0],["The page number",1],["The book is thin",1],["The colour of the letters",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpgnext","caption":"Flagship lab: find the clues on a cover, guess what happens next, say why, then turn the page and keep or change your guess.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_E5KC4AnRLW":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"“Kim fed the ducks at the pond.” Where did Kim feed the ducks?","choices":["At the pond","The ducks","Kim"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where words name a place.","explain":"At the pond names a place. The answer is right there in the words."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"“Raj put on his pyjamas and brushed his teeth. He yawned.” What time of day is it?","choices":["Bedtime","Lunchtime","Raj is at school"],"answer":0,"hint":"When do you put on pyjamas and yawn?","explain":"Pyjamas, brushing teeth and a yawn are clues. They all fit bedtime."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"“The floor was covered in mud. Muddy paw prints went to the dog bed.” Who made the mess?","choices":["The dog","Mum","Nobody, the mud fell from the sky"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paw prints. Where do they go?","explain":"Paw prints going to the dog bed are the clue, so the dog made the mess."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Text: “Penguins cannot fly. They use their wings to swim fast.” Which line proves a penguin is a good swimmer?","choices":["They use their wings to swim fast.","Penguins cannot fly.","Penguins live in cold places."],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the line about swimming. Is every choice in the text?","explain":"They use their wings to swim fast proves it. Penguins cannot fly is true but is about flying, and the cold line is not in the text."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"“The ice cream ran down Joe’s hand. Drips fell on his shoes.” What can you work out?","choices":["It is hot, so the ice cream is melting","It is a freezing day","Joe does not like ice cream"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clue: drips. What do I know about ice cream in the heat?","explain":"The drips are the clue, and I know ice cream melts in the heat. A freezing day goes against the clue."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why do you go back to the text after you answer?","choices":["To find the words that prove it","To count the pages","You do not need to"],"answer":0,"hint":"How do you show someone your answer is right?","explain":"Good readers point to the words that prove their answer. That stops a wild guess."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A text has {a} lines. Only 1 line proves the answer. How many lines do not prove it?","answer":"a-1","hint":"Take away the 1 line that proves it.","explain":"{a} lines take away 1 proving line leaves {=a-1} lines that do not prove it.","params":{"a":[3,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Ali asks {a} right-there questions and {b} clue questions about a story. How many questions does he ask?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the two kinds of question together.","explain":"{a} right-there questions and {b} clue questions make {=a+b} questions.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"“Nia’s cheeks were red. She held her coat tight in the cold wind.” Which guess does NOT fit the clues?","choices":["Nia is on a warm beach","Nia is outside","Nia feels cold"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check each guess against every clue.","explain":"A warm beach goes against the cold wind clue. The other two guesses fit every clue."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"“Zara swam in the sea on Sunday.” When did Zara swim?","choices":["On Sunday","In the sea","Zara"],"answer":0,"hint":"When words tell a time. Find the time in the sentence.","explain":"On Sunday tells a time, so it answers when. In the sea is a place, so it answers where."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Each clue question needs 2 clues. Ella answers {a} clue questions. How many clues does she find?","answer":"2*a","hint":"Count 2 clues for every question she answers.","explain":"{a} questions with 2 clues each make {=2*a} clues.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"“Dad put on his sun hat. He rubbed on sun cream.” What is the weather like?","choices":["Sunny","Snowy","It does not say, so we cannot tell"],"answer":0,"hint":"The word sunny is not there. What do the sun hat and sun cream tell you?","explain":"A sun hat and sun cream are clues. You need them when it is sunny, so it is a sunny day."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A story has {a} pages. Sam finds {b} clues on each page. How many clues does he find?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Add the clues page by page. Every page has the same number.","explain":"{a} pages with {b} clues each make {=a*b} clues.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[2,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Read: Ben fed his dog Pip in the garden after school. Who fed Pip? Ben. Where? In the garden. When? After school. The answers are right there. Go back and find the words.","Now read: Mia dug in the sand. A wave got her feet wet. Where is Mia? It does not say! But sand and a wave are clues. You find them at the beach. So Mia is at the beach.","Clue plus what I know makes a good guess. The grass is wet and there are big puddles. I know rain makes puddles. So it rained. A guess must fit all the clues. It is not a hot, dry day, because the grass is wet.","Show how you know. Point to the words that prove it. Camels can go for days without a drink. That line proves a camel can make a long trip with little water."],"adult":["This is the start of reading comprehension in both national systems. The US standards ask Grade 3 readers to ask and answer questions to show understanding, referring explicitly to the text as the basis for answers (CCSS RL.3.1 and RI.3.1). The English National Curriculum asks Years 3 and 4 to draw inferences, such as characters' feelings, thoughts and motives, and to justify inferences with evidence from the text.","Teachers often split questions into two kinds. Literal or right-there questions have the answer stated in the words, usually who, what, where or when. Inference questions need the reader to join a text clue with background knowledge to reach something unstated. Many classrooms call this clue plus what I know equals my inference, and it works for stories and for information texts alike.","Common mistakes: guessing from the picture or from their own life instead of looking back at the words; giving a when phrase for a where question; making a guess no clue supports; keeping a guess that one clue contradicts; and quoting a line that is true but does not prove the answer. Ask your child which words told them, every time.","In the lab your child answers right-there questions by tapping words, hunts two clues to find an unstated place or time, builds clue plus what I know guesses, and taps the line that proves an answer in a short camel fact text. Every tap and drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime, stop on a page and ask one right-there question (Who? Where?) and one clue question (How do you think she feels? What time of day is it?). After each answer, ask: Which words told you? Let your child point.","keyIdea":"Some answers are right there in the words: who did it, where and when. Other answers are not said, so you look for clues. Sand and a wave tell you the beach, even if the word beach is not there. A good guess joins a clue with what you already know, and it fits every clue. Then you prove it: point to the line that shows you are right.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"“Ben fed his dog Pip in the garden. Pip wagged his tail.” Is each answer right there, or do you need clues?","opts":{"bins":["Right there in the words","Work it out from clues"],"items":[["Who fed Pip?",0],["Where was Pip fed?",0],["What did Ben feed?",0],["Was Pip happy?",1],["Is Pip still hungry?",1],["Does Ben look after Pip?",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagclue","caption":"Flagship lab: find right-there answers, hunt two clues for what is not said, join a clue with what you know, and prove an answer with the right line.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_DVSHx3YMkN":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"\"He met his Waterloo in the final.\" What does the allusion suggest?","choices":["A crushing, final defeat","A trip to Belgium","A great victory"],"answer":0,"hint":"Napoleon fought his last battle at Waterloo. Did he win?","explain":"Napoleon was finally defeated at Waterloo, so meeting your Waterloo means a decisive defeat."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A traffic warden gets a parking ticket. What kind of irony is this?","choices":["Situational irony","Verbal irony","Dramatic irony"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is someone saying the opposite of what they mean, or does something happen that reverses what you expect?","explain":"The person who gives out tickets getting one is the opposite of what you expect, so it is situational irony."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is the strongest analysis of \"The classroom was a zoo\"?","choices":["The metaphor suggests the class was wild, noisy and out of control.","This is a metaphor.","There were animals in the classroom."],"answer":0,"hint":"A strong answer says what the device suggests, not just its name.","explain":"It names the metaphor and explains what it suggests about the class. Just naming it is feature-spotting."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In a play the audience knows the letter is a fake, but the hero believes it. This is…","choices":["dramatic irony","situational irony","a simile"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who knows more here: the audience or the character?","explain":"When the audience knows something a character does not, it is dramatic irony. It builds tension as we wait for the truth."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"It rains on the day of the school trip. Is this irony?","choices":["No, it is just bad luck","Yes, it is situational irony","Yes, it is verbal irony"],"answer":0,"hint":"Was the rain the opposite of what you would expect, or just unlucky?","explain":"Rain on a trip day is unlucky but not a reversal of expectation, so it is not irony."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word gives the most menacing tone? \"The figure ___ at the window.\"","choices":["lurked","stood","waited"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word suggests someone hiding with bad intentions?","explain":"Lurked suggests hiding and danger, so it creates a menacing tone. Stood and waited are neutral."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A story uses {p} examples of personification and {a} allusions. How many devices is that in all?","answer":"p+a","hint":"Add the two kinds of device together.","explain":"{p} examples of personification and {a} allusions make {=p+a} devices in all.","params":{"p":[1,7],"a":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"In a set of {n} sentences, {v} use verbal irony and {d} use dramatic irony. The rest use no irony. How many use no irony?","answer":"n-v-d","hint":"Take both kinds of irony away from the total.","explain":"{n} take away {v} and {d} leaves {=n-v-d} sentences with no irony.","params":{"n":[10,14],"v":[1,4],"d":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word has the most positive connotation for someone who saves money?","choices":["thrifty","stingy","mean"],"answer":0,"hint":"All three describe someone who does not spend much. Which one sounds like praise?","explain":"Thrifty praises careful saving. Stingy and mean suggest someone who will not share, so they are negative."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"\"The old car coughed and wheezed up the hill.\" Which device is used and what is its effect?","choices":["Personification: the car seems old, ill and struggling","Allusion: it refers to a famous car","Verbal irony: the car is actually fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a car really cough? Whose actions are coughing and wheezing?","explain":"Coughing and wheezing are human actions, so this is personification. It makes the car seem like a sick old person, worn out and struggling."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A poem has {s} similes, {m} metaphors and 3 examples of personification. How many of these devices are comparisons (similes and metaphors)?","answer":"s+m","hint":"Similes and metaphors both compare one thing to another. Add those two counts only.","explain":"{s} similes and {m} metaphors make {=s+m} comparisons. The personification is counted separately.","params":{"s":[1,6],"m":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A student's paragraph names {n} devices but explains the effect of only {k}. How many devices are just spotted with no effect explained?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the devices with an effect explained away from all the devices named.","explain":"{n} named take away {k} explained leaves {=n-k} devices that were only spotted. Each needs a sentence on what it suggests.","params":{"n":[4,8],"k":[1,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Words have a meaning and a feeling. Slim, thin and scrawny all mean not fat. Slim sounds admiring. Scrawny sounds weak and a bit unkind. That feeling is called connotation, and it sets the tone of a piece.","Personification makes a thing act like a person. In 'the floorboards groaned', old boards cannot really groan, but the word makes the house sound tired and spooky. Look for a human action given to something that is not human.","An allusion points to a story most readers know, from a myth, the Bible or a famous book. 'Penalties were his Achilles' heel' borrows the Greek hero who had one weak spot. Two words bring a whole story with them.","Irony is a gap between what is said or expected and what is real. Saying 'lovely weather' in a storm is verbal irony. A fire station burning down is situational irony. When the audience knows something a character does not, that is dramatic irony. Plain bad luck, like rain on a picnic, is not irony."],"adult":["This topic meets the US Common Core reading standards RL.6.4 to RL.8.4 and RI.6.4 to RI.8.4. They ask students to work out figurative and connotative meanings and to analyse how word choice shapes meaning and tone. RL.8.4 adds allusions to other texts, and RL.8.6 names dramatic irony. The Key Stage 3 English programme for England asks pupils to study how writers use figurative language and vocabulary choice to create effects.","Denotation is a word's dictionary meaning; connotation is the set of feelings it carries. Tone is the writer's attitude, built largely from connotation. Personification gives human qualities to objects, animals or ideas. An allusion is a brief reference to a well-known story, person or text. Irony comes in three main kinds: verbal (saying the opposite of what is meant), situational (an outcome that reverses what was expected) and dramatic (the audience knows more than a character).","Common mistakes: treating synonyms as if they had the same effect; feature-spotting, where a student writes 'this is a metaphor' and stops; reading an allusion literally, as if Achilles' heel were about a sore foot; calling any bad luck ironic; and mixing up the kinds of irony. A useful frame is device, then what it suggests, then the effect on the reader or the tone.","In the lab students drag near-synonyms onto a positive, neutral and negative scale, tap the word that personifies an object, decode three allusions to Greek myth and the Bible, sort four cards into verbal, situational and dramatic irony or plain bad luck, and pick the analysis sentence that explains an effect rather than naming a device."]},"familyTip":"Pick a word from a news headline, such as 'slammed', and swap it for a calmer word. Ask which one the writer chose and why. When a film shows the audience something a character cannot see, pause it and ask: what do we know that they don't?","keyIdea":"Writers choose words for their connotations, the feelings they carry, as well as their meanings: slim and scrawny both mean thin but feel different. Literary devices such as personification, allusion and irony make readers see more than the literal words. Personification gives a thing human actions; an allusion borrows the meaning of a story readers know; irony is a gap between what is said or expected and what is true. A strong analysis names the device, says what it suggests and explains its effect on the reader and the tone.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which device is it? Drag each line, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Personification","Allusion","Irony"],"items":[["The kettle sang on the stove.",0],["He was a real Scrooge with his money.",1],["\"What a great day,\" she said, after losing her keys.",2],["The trees whispered in the breeze.",0],["It was opening Pandora's box.",1],["The lifeguard had to be rescued.",2],["Time crawled during the test.",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdevice","caption":"Flagship lab: place words on a connotation scale, find personification, decode allusions, sort kinds of irony and pick the best analysis of an effect.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_0MfpLj0Uhb":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Who should a book recommendation be built around?","choices":["The person you are recommending to","You, the recommender","The author"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who is going to read the book next?","explain":"A recommendation helps someone else choose, so it starts with what they enjoy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which line is a spoiler?","choices":["In the end, the dog was the hero all along","The story is set on a farm","It is told by a girl called Rosa"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which line gives away what happens at the end?","explain":"Saying who turns out to be the hero gives away the ending. Setting and narrator tell the reader what the book is like without spoiling it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which comparison is most useful?","choices":["Both books are about standing up to a bully","Both books have yellow covers","Both books are 150 pages"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for something the two stories share inside, like a theme.","explain":"Standing up to a bully is a shared theme, so a reader who liked one may like the other. Covers and page counts say nothing about the story."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which of these is a genre?","choices":["Mystery","Friendship","Chapter 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"A genre is a kind of story, like adventure or fantasy.","explain":"Mystery is a genre. Friendship is a theme, and Chapter 2 is just part of a book."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ben says, 'Read this, it's awesome.' How could he make it better?","choices":["Say what in the book makes it awesome","Say it louder","Tell the whole story"],"answer":0,"hint":"What would help his friend decide?","explain":"'Awesome' is praise. Naming a feature, like a funny narrator or a twist you never see coming, gives a real reason."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Mia loves scary stories. You loved a book about baking. What should you do?","choices":["Find her a scary story you read and liked","Push the baking book anyway","Tell her not to read scary stories"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who is the recommendation for?","explain":"Mia's taste comes first. Recommend a scary book you have read and can give reasons for."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You give {r} specific reasons and {v} vague ones like 'it's good'. How many reasons actually help your friend?","answer":"r","hint":"Only specific reasons say something about the book.","explain":"Only the {r} specific reasons help; the {v} vague ones say nothing about the book.","params":{"r":[1,4],"v":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which recommendation is strongest?","choices":["If you like mysteries, try this: the clues are hidden in plain sight, like in The Missing Key, and I won't tell you who did it","It's the best book ever, everyone loves it","The thief is the gardener, but it's still good"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for who it suits, a reason, a comparison and no spoilers.","explain":"The first one has all four parts. The others are vague or give the ending away."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"You wrote recommendations for {f} friends. {k} of them gave away the ending. How many recommendations kept the surprise?","answer":"f-k","hint":"Take the ones with spoilers away from the total.","explain":"{f} - {k} = {=f-k}. Those are the ones that kept the ending a surprise.","params":{"f":[4,8],"k":[1,3]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Omar loved a fantasy book with dragons. Which book should you recommend?","choices":["Another fantasy with magic and quests","A true book about football","Your favourite joke book"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start from what Omar already enjoyed, not from what you like.","explain":"Omar liked fantasy, so a fantasy with magic and quests is most likely to suit him. Your own favourite might not."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which is a specific reason?","choices":["The chapters end on cliffhangers, so you keep turning pages","It is really, really good","My friend said it was cool"],"answer":0,"hint":"A specific reason names something you can find in the book itself.","explain":"Cliffhangers are a real feature of how the book is built. 'Really good' and what a friend said do not tell the reader anything about the book."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Your recommendation has {a} lines. {b} of them give away the ending. How many lines are left once you cut the spoilers?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the spoiler lines away from the total number of lines.","explain":"{a} - {b} = {=a-b}. Cutting spoilers keeps the surprise for your friend.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You want to give your friend {t} specific reasons to read a book. You have written {n} so far. How many more reasons do you need?","answer":"t-n","hint":"Take the reasons you have written away from the number you want.","explain":"{t} - {n} = {=t-n}. Each reason should name something real in the book, like a narrator or a twist.","params":{"t":[3,5],"n":[1,2]}}],"explain":{"kid":["When you recommend a book, you are helping a friend choose what to read next. So start with them, not you. What did they enjoy last time? A friend who loved a detective story may want another puzzle. A friend who reads comics may want short chapters and pictures.","Next, give a real reason. 'It's good' or 'it's the best book ever' does not tell your friend anything. A specific reason names something in the book. You might say the clues are hidden so you can solve the case first, or that every chapter ends on a cliffhanger.","Comparing helps too. 'If you liked Storm Island, try Lost in the Snow, because both are about friends who must survive together.' That links the books by genre or theme, which is what your friend enjoyed. Page count and cover colour are not good links.","Last, keep the surprises. Do not say who the thief is or how it ends. You can hint, like 'You will never guess who did it!' A great recommendation says who the book suits, gives a real reason, compares it to another book, and has no spoilers."],"adult":["In the England National Curriculum, Year 5 and 6 pupils should be 'recommending books that they have read to their peers, giving reasons for their choices'. They should also identify and discuss themes and conventions across books and make comparisons within and across books. Recommending is where those reading skills become a real task with a real audience.","The most common mistake is recommending your own favourite to everyone, whatever they like. The next is praise without reasons ('it's amazing') or reasons about surface features such as length, cover or how fast you read it. Children also often retell the whole plot, ending included, because summary feels like explanation.","Good recommendations use the vocabulary of KS2 reading: genre (mystery, adventure, fantasy), theme (friendship, courage, loss), narrator and author's style, and plot structure such as cliffhangers. Ask your child to say why a feature matters to this reader, not just that it is there.","The lab uses made-up books and readers. Your child matches three friends to books that fit what they enjoy and sorts specific reasons from vague praise. They also spot spoilers, link pairs of books by genre or theme, and build a full four-part recommendation."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime or in the car, take turns recommending a book or film to someone else in the family. The rule: you must say who it suits, give one specific reason and keep the ending secret. Listeners can ask 'What makes you say that?'","keyIdea":"A good book recommendation is made for the reader, not for you. Start from what your friend already enjoys, then give a specific reason that names something real in the book (its genre, a theme, the author's style or how the plot is built). Compare it to a book they know, and never give away the ending.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Helpful in a recommendation, or not? Drag each line, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Helps my friend choose","Does not help"],"items":[["It is a mystery you can solve with the detective",0],["It's good",1],["If you liked The Missing Key, you'll like this",0],["The thief is the gardener",1],["The narrator makes jokes all the way through",0],["It has 300 pages",1],["It suits anyone who likes funny animal stories",0],["Everyone has read it",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrecbk","caption":"Flagship lab: match readers to books, sort real reasons from vague praise, spot spoilers, link books by theme and build a recommendation.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_sUVOS2jH3J":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which sentence is a real comparison?","choices":["Both heroes are scared of the dark, but only one faces it alone.","Book A is about a girl. Book B is about a boy.","I liked Book A more."],"answer":0,"hint":"A comparison talks about both books in one sentence and links them.","explain":"The first joins both books and shows how they are alike and different. The second is two separate facts, and the third is just an opinion."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Two books are both about friendship. What is the best thing to compare?","choices":["How each author shows friendship","Which book has more pages","Which cover is brighter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the writing, not the object.","explain":"A shared theme is a starting point. Comparing how each author shows it is where the interesting differences are."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"'I froze. My heart banged like a drum.' How is the narrator's fear shown?","choices":["Through her own thoughts and feelings, in first person","Through what other characters say about her","Through a description of the weather"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the word 'I' and what it tells us.","explain":"The narrator says 'I' and tells us what she feels inside. That is first person, and it lets us into her head."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Both heroes are brave by the end. Does that mean the books have the same message?","choices":["Not always: they may show courage in different ways","Yes: being brave is always the same","Only if they have the same author"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how each hero becomes brave.","explain":"One hero may learn to face fear alone, and another may learn that asking for help is brave. Same theme, different message."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A Venn diagram has {x} things only in Book A, {y} things only in Book B and {z} things in the middle. How many things are true of Book A?","answer":"x+z","hint":"Book A's circle includes its own side and the middle.","explain":"{x} + {z} = {=x+z}. The middle is part of Book A's circle too, because shared things are true of both books.","params":{"x":[2,5],"y":[2,5],"z":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Choose the word for the gap: 'Nell lives on an island, ___ Mo lives in a city.'","choices":["whereas","similarly","also"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are an island and a city alike or different?","explain":"An island and a city are different, so 'whereas' fits. 'Similarly' and 'also' would wrongly say they are alike."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"'The wind screamed round the house.' What is this setting line mainly doing?","choices":["Setting a scary mood","Telling us the hero's age","Ending the story"],"answer":0,"hint":"How does 'screamed' make you feel?","explain":"'Screamed' makes the wind sound alive and frightening, so the line builds mood."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"You compare two books on {p} points. {s} are similarities. How many are differences?","answer":"p-s","hint":"Every point is either a similarity or a difference.","explain":"{p} - {s} = {=p-s} differences. Each one could start with 'whereas' or 'however'.","params":{"p":[5,9],"s":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"What makes a comparison point convincing?","choices":["Evidence, such as a short quote, from each book","Saying it very strongly","Repeating it twice"],"answer":0,"hint":"How can a reader check that your point is true?","explain":"A quote from each book lets the reader check your point. Saying it loudly or twice does not prove anything."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In a Venn diagram of two books, where does something that both books share go?","choices":["In the middle, where the circles overlap","On the left side only","Outside both circles"],"answer":0,"hint":"The overlap is the part that belongs to both circles at once.","explain":"The overlap belongs to both circles, so shared features go in the middle. Each side is for one book only."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You list {a} features of Book A. {s} of them are also in Book B. How many features belong only to Book A?","answer":"a-s","hint":"Take the shared features (the middle of the Venn) away from Book A's total.","explain":"{a} - {s} = {=a-s}. Those go in Book A's own side. The {s} shared ones go in the middle.","params":{"a":[6,10],"s":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which word joins two things that are different?","choices":["Whereas","Similarly","Both"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word signals a contrast between the two books?","explain":"'Whereas' links two different points, as in 'Nell is alone, whereas Mo has help.' 'Similarly' and 'both' link things that are alike."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Your comparison has {s} similarities and {d} differences. Each one needs a quote from each book. How many quotes do you need?","answer":"2*(s+d)","hint":"Add the points, then remember each point needs two quotes: one from each book.","explain":"{s} + {d} = {=s+d} points, and each needs 2 quotes, so 2 × {=s+d} = {=2*(s+d)}.","params":{"s":[1,3],"d":[1,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Comparing books means looking at two books side by side. You ask what is the same and what is different. A Venn diagram helps. Things only one book has go on its side. Things both books share go in the middle.","Look past the plot. Ask about the theme, which is the big idea, like courage or friendship. Ask about the setting and the main character. Then ask how the story is told. Is it told by the hero, using 'I'? Or by a narrator, using 'he' or 'she'?","Two books can share a theme but show it in different ways. In one book the hero tells us her scared thoughts. In another we only see what the hero does and says. They also may not say the same thing. One hero is brave alone. Another learns that asking for help is brave too.","When you write it up, join both books in one sentence. Use 'similarly' or 'both' for things that are alike. Use 'whereas' or 'however' for things that are different. Then add evidence, like a quote, so the reader can check your point."],"adult":["The England National Curriculum asks Years 5 and 6 to make comparisons within and across books, and to identify and discuss themes and conventions across a wide range of writing. The US Common Core standard RL.5.9 asks pupils to compare and contrast stories in the same genre on their approaches to similar themes and topics.","The most common mistake is the 'two lists' answer: a paragraph about one book, then a paragraph about the other, with no link. A second mistake is thinking that a shared theme means a shared message. Two stories about courage can disagree about what courage is. Children also compare surface features such as length and covers instead of the writing.","Useful terms are theme, setting, narrator (first person and third person), character development and comparative connectives such as similarly, both, whereas, however and in contrast. Ask your child to back each point with a short quotation from each book.","The lab uses two made-up novels. Your child sorts facts into a Venn diagram and sorts extracts by how each author shows courage. They then track how each main character changes, sort what each setting does and choose 'similarly' or 'whereas' to join comparison sentences."]},"familyTip":"After you both watch a film or read a story that reminds you of another one, play 'Same and different'. Each person names one thing that is the same and one that is different, joined in one sentence with 'similarly' or 'whereas'.","keyIdea":"To compare two books, look at both together. Find what is the same and what is different: theme, characters, setting and how the story is told. Two books can share a theme but show it in different ways. A good comparison joins the books in one sentence with words like 'similarly', 'both' and 'whereas', and backs each point with evidence.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Two books about courage. Is each point a similarity or a difference? Drag each line, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Similarity","Difference"],"items":[["Both heroes are ten years old",0],["One is told with 'I', the other with 'he'",1],["Both stories are about being brave",0],["One is set on an island, the other in a city",1],["In both, water is the danger",0],["One hero acts alone, the other gets help",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbkcmp","caption":"Flagship lab: sort two books into a Venn diagram, compare how each shows courage, track the characters and settings, and join comparisons.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_vuHR_y2Z8c":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does hamartia mean?","choices":["An error or flaw in the hero that leads to the fall","The villain who destroys the hero","The audience's release of emotion"],"answer":0,"hint":"It belongs to the hero, not to another character or the audience.","explain":"Hamartia is the hero's own error or failing, such as Macbeth's ambition. A villain may exploit it, as Iago does, but it is not the villain."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Where does catharsis happen?","choices":["In the audience, as pity and fear are released or clarified","On stage, in the final death scene","In the hero's mind, at the moment of recognition"],"answer":0,"hint":"Aristotle describes what tragedy does to the people watching it.","explain":"Catharsis is the audience's response. Placing it in the plot confuses it with catastrophe or anagnorisis."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why does Aristotle reject a thoroughly wicked hero?","choices":["His fall feels deserved, so it stirs neither pity nor fear","Wicked characters cannot speak in verse","Audiences refuse to watch villains"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pity is for suffering that is more than the person deserves.","explain":"We feel pity for undeserved suffering and fear for someone like us. A villain's punishment gives satisfaction instead."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Arthur Miller's Death of a Salesman makes Willy Loman, an ordinary salesman, its tragic hero. Which classical idea does it challenge?","choices":["That a tragic hero must be of high status","That a tragedy needs a reversal","That plays are written in acts"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare Willy with Oedipus, Lear and Macbeth. What do those three have that he lacks?","explain":"In 'Tragedy and the Common Man' Miller argues the common man is as apt a subject for tragedy as kings, rejecting the high-status rule."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"In Othello, which moment is best read as the peripeteia?","choices":["Act 3, when Othello accepts Iago's lies and vows revenge","Act 1, when Othello and Desdemona marry","After the play, when the audience leaves"],"answer":0,"hint":"Peripeteia is the reversal, when fortune starts to swing the other way.","explain":"In the temptation scene of Act 3, Othello's fortune turns from love and success towards murder and ruin. That is the reversal."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A student argues that Othello has no hamartia because Iago causes everything. What is the strongest reply?","choices":["Iago exploits Othello's jealousy and credulity, but Othello still chooses to kill","Othello is the villain of the play","Iago is the real tragic hero"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a villain's plot and a hero's flaw both be true at once?","explain":"Tragedy can share responsibility. Iago lights the fuse, but Othello's readiness to believe and his choice to act make the fall his own."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A film shows a family losing their home in an earthquake after {n} minutes. Using Aristotle's terms, how is it best described?","choices":["Pathos: suffering from chance, with no hamartia","Tragedy, because the ending is unhappy","Catharsis, because it is upsetting"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did anyone's error cause the earthquake?","explain":"It may be deeply moving, but the suffering comes from chance, not from a hero's error, so it is pathos rather than tragedy.","params":{"n":[20,90]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A {n}-word essay calls Lear's banishing of Cordelia in Act 1 his anagnorisis. What is the slip?","choices":["Banishing her is the hamartia in action; the recognition comes later, when he sees she loved him","There is no slip: banishing her is the recognition","Lear has no recognition in the play"],"answer":0,"hint":"Recognition means coming to see the truth. Does Lear see clearly when he banishes her?","explain":"In Act 1 Lear's pride blinds him. His recognition comes in Act 4, waking to Cordelia, too late to save either of them.","params":{"n":[600,1500]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A character is killed in a random accident in the final scene. Why is this not tragic in Aristotle's sense?","choices":["No error or choice of the character's causes the fall","Accidents are too exciting for tragedy","Tragedies cannot end with a death"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask what drives the fall: chance, or the hero's own hamartia?","explain":"Chance suffering is pathetic, not tragic. In Aristotle's model the fall grows out of the hero's own error, so we see cause and effect."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"An essay says, \"{n} characters die by the end of Hamlet, so it is a tragedy.\" What is the weakness?","choices":["A body count is not a definition; it should show hamartia, reversal and recognition","It should count the deaths more carefully","Hamlet is a comedy, so the claim is wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Would a war film with {n} deaths automatically be a tragedy?","explain":"Deaths alone prove nothing. Argue from Hamlet's delay as a possible hamartia, the reversal after the play scene, and his late recognition.","params":{"n":[4,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Neoclassical critics read the Poetics as limiting a tragedy's action to one day (24 hours), the unity of time. A play's action spans {d} days. By how many hours does it break that limit?","answer":"24*d-24","hint":"Work out the total hours in {d} days, then take away the 24 allowed.","explain":"{d} days is {=24*d} hours, so it is {=24*d-24} hours over. Shakespeare ignores the unity: The Winter's Tale leaps sixteen years.","params":{"d":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Oedipus discovers that the man he killed was his own father. Which term fits this moment?","choices":["Anagnorisis","Catharsis","Hamartia"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which term means a change from ignorance to knowledge?","explain":"Anagnorisis is recognition. In Oedipus it arrives together with the reversal, which Aristotle thought the finest kind of plot."}],"explain":{"kid":["A sad story and a tragedy are not the same. If a hiker is hit by lightning, that is sad, but nothing he chose made it happen. In a tragedy, the hero's own choice starts the fall. Macbeth chooses to kill the king, and that choice ruins him.","Aristotle gave us the key words. Hamartia is the hero's error or flaw: Macbeth's ambition, Othello's jealousy, Lear's pride. It is not the same as being evil. The hero is mostly good, and often great, which is why the fall hurts to watch.","The plot turns twice. Peripeteia is the reversal, when fortune swings the other way. Anagnorisis is recognition, when the hero finally sees the truth, often too late. Then comes the catastrophe, the final ruin.","Catharsis happens in us, not on stage. Aristotle said tragedy should make us feel pity, because the hero suffers more than he deserves, and fear, because he is like us. Later writers changed the rules: Arthur Miller made a salesman his tragic hero."],"adult":["Tragedy is a named genre study in A-level English Literature (for example AQA's Aspects of tragedy). Students must use the terms from Aristotle's Poetics. They apply them to set texts such as Othello, King Lear or Death of a Salesman. Then they judge how far each play fits, bends or breaks the pattern.","The terms are precise. Hamartia means an error or failing, not just a vice. Peripeteia is a reversal of fortune. Anagnorisis is a change from not knowing to knowing. Aristotle thought the best plots join the two, as in Oedipus. Catharsis is still argued over: it may mean a purging, a cleansing or a clearing of pity and fear.","Common mistakes: calling any death or sad ending tragic; treating hamartia as the villain or as bad luck; putting catharsis in the plot, not in the audience; and using the old rules as a checklist. Strong essays test the model. Miller's essay Tragedy and the Common Man rejects the rule that heroes must be high born. Iago's plotting makes Othello's share of the blame a real question.","In the lab students swipe events into sad or tragic. They drag a fortune line into the tragic arc and test it, pin the right flaw on three of Shakespeare's heroes, and name the turning points in Othello. Last, they change a hero's traits until Aristotle's pity and fear meters both fill."]},"familyTip":"Watch or read any tragedy together (a film counts) and ask three questions at the end: what choice of the hero's started the fall, when did everything turn, and when did the hero finally understand? If the answers are 'nothing, never, never', you may have a sad story rather than a tragedy, which is a good argument to have.","keyIdea":"In the tradition that runs from Aristotle's Poetics through Shakespeare to Arthur Miller, tragedy is not just a sad story. It shows a hero of some greatness whose own error or flaw (hamartia) sets off a fall: fortune reverses (peripeteia), the hero comes to see the truth (anagnorisis), the play ends in catastrophe, and the audience is moved to pity and fear, which Aristotle said are released or clarified (catharsis). Writers have reshaped every part of this pattern, so the definition is a lens to argue with, not a checklist.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which part of the tragic pattern is it? Drag each moment, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Hamartia (error or flaw)","Peripeteia (reversal)","Anagnorisis (recognition)"],"items":[["Macbeth's ambition to be king",0],["Lear's need to hear his daughters flatter him",0],["Othello swallows Iago's lie and turns on Desdemona",1],["The messenger's good news ruins Oedipus instead",1],["Oedipus learns the man he killed was his father",2],["Lear sees, too late, that Cordelia loved him truly",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtragic","caption":"Flagship lab: judge sad or tragic, shape the tragic arc, pin each hero's hamartia, name Othello's turns and tune pity and fear.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_aClzPBiS9k":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many feet are in a line of iambic pentameter?","choices":["5","10","14"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Penta' means five. Is it counting feet or syllables?","explain":"Pentameter means five feet. Each iamb has 2 syllables, so the line has 10 syllables but only 5 feet."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which rhyme scheme belongs to a Shakespearean sonnet?","choices":["ABAB CDCD EFEF GG","ABBAABBA CDECDE","AABB CCDD EEFF GG"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of three alternating quatrains and a closing couplet.","explain":"Shakespeare uses three quatrains with alternate rhyme and a final couplet. ABBAABBA CDECDE is the Petrarchan pattern."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where does the volta usually come in a Petrarchan sonnet?","choices":["between the octave and the sestet","in the final couplet","after line 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"A Petrarchan sonnet is built from an 8-line part and a 6-line part.","explain":"The octave sets up a problem and the sestet answers it, so the turn comes after line 8. The final couplet is the Shakespearean turn."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A sentence carries on from the end of one line into the next with no punctuation. What is this?","choices":["enjambment","a caesura","an end-stopped line"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the line pause at the end, or run straight on?","explain":"A line that runs on into the next is enjambed. An end-stopped line pauses at the end, and a caesura is a pause inside a line."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which statement about free verse is accurate?","choices":["it has no regular metre or rhyme scheme","it has no line breaks","it must rhyme in couplets"],"answer":0,"hint":"Free verse is still set out in lines. What does it leave out?","explain":"Free verse drops regular metre and a set rhyme scheme, but it still uses line breaks, often for emphasis."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"'The fair breeze blew, the white foam flew.' Which two devices does Coleridge use?","choices":["alliteration and internal rhyme","only end rhyme","onomatopoeia and caesura only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sounds of 'fair', 'foam' and 'flew', and listen to 'blew' and 'flew'.","explain":"Fair, foam and flew repeat the f sound (alliteration), and blew and flew rhyme inside one line (internal rhyme)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which word pattern is an iamb?","choices":["be-LOW (soft then strong)","TI-ger (strong then soft)","DAY (one strong syllable)"],"answer":0,"hint":"An iamb goes da-DUM: a soft syllable first, then a strong one. Say each word aloud.","explain":"An iamb is an unstressed then a stressed syllable, like be-LOW. TI-ger is a trochee, strong then soft."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A sonnet has 14 lines. A student has drafted {a} lines. How many more lines are needed?","answer":"14-a","hint":"Take the drafted lines away from 14.","explain":"14 − {a} = {=14-a}, so {=14-a} more lines are needed to complete the sonnet.","params":{"a":[3,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A ballad stanza in common metre has lines of 8, 6, 8 and 6 syllables. How many syllables are in {a} such stanzas?","answer":"a*28","hint":"First add the syllables in one stanza: 8 + 6 + 8 + 6.","explain":"One stanza has 8 + 6 + 8 + 6 = 28 syllables, so {a} stanzas have {a} × 28 = {=a*28} syllables.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Why might a poet use a caesura?","choices":["to make the reader pause mid-line for emphasis","to make the line rhyme","to add a 15th line"],"answer":0,"hint":"A caesura is a pause. What does a pause do to how a line is read?","explain":"A pause in the middle of a line slows the reader and puts weight on the words around it. It has nothing to do with rhyme or line count."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A stanza ends with the words 'night', 'day', 'light', 'away'. What is its rhyme scheme?","choices":["ABAB","AABB","ABCD"],"answer":0,"hint":"Give 'night' the letter A, then check which later line ends with the same sound.","explain":"Night and light rhyme (A), and day and away rhyme (B), so the pattern alternates: ABAB."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A poem has {a} lines, all in iambic pentameter. Each line has 10 syllables. How many syllables are there in total?","answer":"a*10","hint":"Each line has 5 iambs, and each iamb has 2 syllables.","explain":"Each line has 10 syllables, so {a} lines have {a} × 10 = {=a*10} syllables.","params":{"a":[2,14]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A line of iambic tetrameter has 4 feet. A stanza has {a} such lines. How many stressed syllables does it have?","answer":"a*4","hint":"Each iamb has one stressed syllable, so count one stress per foot.","explain":"Each line has 4 feet with one stress each, so {a} lines have {a} × 4 = {=a*4} stressed syllables.","params":{"a":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"'I celebrate myself, and sing myself,' has a comma in the middle. What is that pause called?","choices":["a caesura","enjambment","a volta"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the pause inside the line, at the end, or a turn in the argument?","explain":"A pause inside a line is a caesura. Enjambment is a line running on, and a volta is the turn in a sonnet."}],"explain":{"kid":["A rhyme scheme uses letters to show which lines rhyme. The first end sound is A. A new sound gets the next letter. So 'cloud, hills, crowd, daffodils' is ABAB, because cloud and crowd rhyme and so do hills and daffodils.","Metre is the beat of a line. Each beat unit is a foot. An iamb is a soft syllable then a strong one: da-DUM. Five iambs in a row make iambic pentameter. That is ten syllables but only five feet, as in 'The curfew tolls the knell of parting day'.","A sonnet has 14 lines. A Shakespearean sonnet has three quatrains and a final couplet: ABAB CDCD EFEF GG. A Petrarchan sonnet has an octave of 8 lines and a sestet of 6. The volta is the turn, where the poem shifts its argument.","Free verse has no regular metre or rhyme scheme, so its line breaks matter. An end-stopped line ends at a pause. An enjambed line runs straight on. A caesura is a pause in the middle of a line. Alliteration repeats first sounds, as in 'fair', 'foam' and 'flew'."],"adult":["England's Key Stage 3 English programme asks pupils to read poetry critically by 'recognising a range of poetic conventions and understanding how these have been used'. It also asks them to study 'the effects of sound, for instance in poetry'. GCSE then expects pupils to analyse form and structure as well as language.","Key terms: rhyme scheme (letters for end sounds), metre (the pattern of stresses), foot (one unit, such as an iamb, da-DUM, or a trochee, DUM-da) and line length (tetrameter has four feet, pentameter five). A Shakespearean sonnet rhymes ABAB CDCD EFEF GG. A Petrarchan sonnet has an octave, ABBAABBA, and a sestet, often CDECDE. Ballads often use common metre, with lines of 8 and 6 syllables.","Common mistakes: counting pentameter as five syllables, not five feet; giving a repeated rhyme sound a new letter; mixing up enjambment and caesura; thinking free verse has no structure; and naming a device without its effect. Real lines also vary the metre, for example with a stressed first syllable, so scansion is a guide, not a rule.","In the lab students label the rhyme scheme of Wordsworth's 'I wandered lonely as a cloud' and mark stresses in two lines of Gray's 'Elegy'. They then build both sonnet forms and place each volta, break a sentence from Whitman's 'Song of Myself' into lines, and find alliteration and internal rhyme in Coleridge."]},"familyTip":"Pick a song lyric your teenager likes and work out its rhyme scheme together with letters. Then clap the beats of one line and count the stresses. Ask where the song changes direction, the way a sonnet turns at the volta.","keyIdea":"Poets shape meaning with conventions. A rhyme scheme labels end sounds with letters, metre is the pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables, and fixed forms such as the sonnet set the number of lines, the rhyme scheme and where the argument turns. Free verse drops regular metre and rhyme, so line breaks, enjambment and caesura carry the effect, and sound devices like alliteration and internal rhyme add pace and mood.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a feature of a fixed form or of free verse? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fixed form (such as a sonnet)","Free verse"],"items":[["exactly 14 lines",0],["a set rhyme scheme",0],["regular iambic pentameter",0],["a volta after line 8",0],["no regular metre",1],["no set rhyme scheme",1],["line lengths chosen freely",1],["breaks placed for effect, not a pattern",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmetre","caption":"Flagship lab: label a rhyme scheme, scan pentameter, build two sonnets and their volta, break free verse and find sound devices.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_r1zqQWyK30":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Tone should be…","choices":["matched to evidence in word choice and pacing","a random mood word with no proof","always comic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Back tone with the lines.","explain":"Tone claims need textual evidence, not a guessed label."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Open a timed unseen response with…","choices":["gist of what happens","a random list of poetic devices","rewriting the poem as prose only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Situation first. Use evidence from the lines to back the claim.","explain":"Gist first keeps later method comments anchored."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"You plan {a} minutes for gist+speaker and {b} for methods. Total planning minutes?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the gist-and-speaker block to the methods block for total planning time.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} minutes of structure before writing.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Overall interpretation should…","choices":["tie methods to a clear reading of the poem","ignore tone completely","invent a new plot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Methods serve one reading.","explain":"End by saying what the methods add up to — without inventing plot."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Treating speaker as always the poet is…","choices":["a common slip","required by every mark scheme","the same as naming the printer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Persona vs writer. Use evidence from the lines to back the claim.","explain":"Speaker and poet can differ; assume identity only with evidence."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"This lesson vs poetry performance forms is mainly about…","choices":["first-read analysis under time pressure","clapping haiku syllables for ages 7–10","staging a group performance only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Unseen analytical response.","explain":"Here the skill is timed interpretation of an unfamiliar poem."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You pick {a} images and write one clear effect for each. How many effects to write?","answer":"a","hint":"One clear effect per chosen image keeps the count honest.","explain":"Write {a} effects — one for each chosen image.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Sound or form can matter when…","choices":["you can say how they shape mood or meaning","you only name alliteration","you ignore the gist"],"answer":0,"hint":"Effect still required. Use evidence from the lines to back the claim.","explain":"Sound and form count when linked to meaning, not as bare labels."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Inventing a plot the lines omit…","choices":["weakens the reading","always gains AO2 marks","replaces the need for gist"],"answer":0,"hint":"Stay with what the poem gives.","explain":"Build from the text. Invented plot drifts from evidence."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Best first move on an unseen poem?","choices":["sum what happens in one or two sentences","list every metaphor before reading","ignore the last line on purpose"],"answer":0,"hint":"Get the situation clear before you hunt for techniques.","explain":"A clear gist anchors tone and method comments. Device lists first waste time."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Speaker means…","choices":["the voice in the poem (not always the poet)","always the exam board","only the printer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask who seems to be talking in the lines themselves.","explain":"Speaker is the poem’s voice. It may be a persona, not the writer."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"In a {a}-minute unseen task you spend {b} minutes on gist and speaker. Minutes left for methods and writing?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Subtract planning minutes from the total time allowed for the task.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} minutes remain for methods and the written response.","params":{"a":[20,45],"b":[5,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You explain {a} images and {b} sound/form choices, each with an effect. How many method-with-effect points is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the image points to the sound or form points for a total count.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} method-with-effect points.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is the mix-up?","choices":["list techniques with no link to meaning","gist then speaker then methods","tone backed by word choice"],"answer":0,"hint":"Spot the weak exam habit that skips meaning.","explain":"Feature-spotting without effect does not build an interpretation."}],"explain":{"kid":["An unseen poem is one you have not studied. Start with a short gist: what happens or what is the situation?","Name the speaker (the voice in the poem, not always the poet) and the situation. Then choose a tone word that fits the evidence, such as lonely or bitter.","Pick concrete methods — an image, a sound pattern, a line break — and say how each one shapes mood or meaning. A technique name with no effect is weak.","Finish with a short overall reading that ties those methods together. Do not invent a plot the lines do not support."],"adult":["Ages 14 to 16. First-read poetry response: gist, speaker/situation, tone, methods with effects, overall interpretation. Distinct from forms-and-performance for younger learners.","Words: gist, speaker, situation, tone, imagery, form, sound, interpretation. Common mistakes: feature-spotting with no effect; speaker = poet always; inventing plot; device lists before gist.","In the lab the learner builds gist, speaker, tone and a method-to-mood link, then spots weak habits and orders a timed response."]},"familyTip":"Read a short unfamiliar poem once, ask for a one-sentence gist, then ask how one image shapes the mood — before naming any technique.","keyIdea":"On a first-read poem under time pressure: sum the gist, name speaker and situation, pin tone with evidence, then link two language/form/sound choices to effects that support one clear overall interpretation.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Strong unseen move or weak habit? Drag each, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Strong move","Weak habit"],"items":[["sum gist in one or two sentences",0],["list metaphors with no effect",1],["link an image to mood",0],["treat speaker as always the poet",1],["tone backed by word choice",0],["invent a plot the lines omit",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagunseen","caption":"Flagship lab: gist a short unseen poem, mark speaker/situation, pick tone, link method to mood, catch feature-spotting, order the response, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_9k1qcpvVi_":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"“By 2350, the last forest grew under a glass dome on Mars.” Which genre is this?","choices":["science fiction","historical fiction","memoir"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the date and the place.","explain":"A future date and life on another planet signal science fiction."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which text was written first?","choices":["Romeo and Juliet","Frankenstein","Noughts & Crosses"],"answer":0,"hint":"Shakespeare wrote in the late 1500s.","explain":"Romeo and Juliet is from about 1595, Frankenstein from 1818 and Noughts & Crosses from 2001."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A book has about 3 tricky words in every 50 that you can work out from context. What is it for you?","choices":["a stretch read","a comfort read","a book you should give up on"],"answer":0,"hint":"Easy enough to follow, hard enough to grow?","explain":"A few tricky words you can work out make a stretch read: hard enough to grow, not too hard to follow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why is reading 30 books in one genre not the same as reading widely?","choices":["wide reading means many kinds of text, not just many books","30 books is too few","it is, as long as you finish them all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it about how many, or how many kinds?","explain":"Wide reading means covering genres, forms and periods. Thirty books of one kind is a lot of reading, but narrow."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You want to finish a {p}-page novel in {d} days. How many pages a day do you need?","answer":"p/d","hint":"Share the pages equally across the days.","explain":"{p} ÷ {d} = {=p/d} pages a day.","params":{"p":[140,280],"d":[7,14]},"where":"p%d==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why read a novel written 200 years ago?","choices":["it shows how people lived and thought then, and later books build on it","it has nothing to say to readers now","only because it is short"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what older books show, and who borrowed from them.","explain":"Older books show other times and ideas, and many newer books borrow their plots, characters and themes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which line is from a play script?","choices":["SAM: (quietly) Is anyone there?","Sam whispered, “Is anyone there?”","Is anyone there? I wondered, alone."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a speaker name and stage directions in brackets.","explain":"A play script puts the speaker's name first, with stage directions in brackets and no speech marks."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"You read {d} pages a day for {n} days. How many pages is that?","answer":"d*n","hint":"Multiply the pages per day by the number of days.","explain":"{d} × {n} = {=d*n} pages.","params":{"d":[10,25],"n":[5,14]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"“The study door was locked from the inside, and the key was still in his pocket.” Which genre does this opening suggest?","choices":["mystery","fantasy","memoir"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a puzzle or a crime to solve?","explain":"A locked room with the key inside is a puzzle to solve, so this opening signals a mystery."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Priya has read 20 fantasy novels this year and nothing else. Which choice makes her reading wider?","choices":["a poetry collection, a play and a memoir","five more fantasy novels","the same fantasy novels again"],"answer":0,"hint":"Wide means new kinds of text, not more of the same.","explain":"New forms and genres widen her reading. More fantasy adds books, but not breadth."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A novel has {p} pages. You read {d} pages a day. How many days will it take to finish?","answer":"p/d","hint":"Divide the number of pages by the pages you read each day.","explain":"{p} ÷ {d} = {=p/d} days to finish the novel.","params":{"p":[120,360],"d":[10,20]},"where":"p%d==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A page has 50 words and you know all but {k} of them. How many words on the page do you know?","answer":"50-k","hint":"Take the tricky words away from the 50 words on the page.","explain":"50 − {k} = {=50-k} words you know on the page.","params":{"k":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which of these is a memoir?","choices":["a writer's true account of their own childhood","a novel told by an invented narrator","a play about a king"],"answer":0,"hint":"A memoir is non-fiction about the writer's own life.","explain":"A memoir is a true account of the writer's own life, so it is non-fiction, even when it reads like a story."}],"explain":{"kid":["A genre is a kind of story or text. Mystery, science fiction, historical fiction and fantasy are genres. A form is the shape a text takes: a novel, a short story, a poem, a play or non-fiction. A memoir is non-fiction. It is the true story of the writer's own life.","Reading widely does not mean reading lots of the same thing. Thirty fantasy books is a lot of reading, but it is narrow. Wide reading means trying new genres and forms. It also means books from other times and other places.","Old books are worth it. Romeo and Juliet is from about 1595. Frankenstein came out in 1818. They show how people lived and thought, and many newer books borrow their ideas.","Choose some books that stretch you. A comfort read is easy and fun. A stretch read has a few tricky words that you can work out. If almost every line has a word you don't know, save that book for later. Whole books need stamina, so read a little every day."],"adult":["The Key Stage 3 English programme of study asks pupils to read a wide range of fiction and non-fiction. That includes whole books, short stories, poems and plays, with wide coverage of genres, periods, forms and authors. It names English literature from before 1914 and from today, two Shakespeare plays, and seminal world literature. It also asks pupils to choose and read books on their own for challenge, interest and enjoyment.","Useful terms: genre, form, convention, fiction and non-fiction, memoir, play script, period, independent reading, reading stamina. A rough guide to difficulty comes from vocabulary research: readers follow a text on their own when they know about 98 per cent of its words. At about 95 per cent they can follow it with some effort or help. Below that, meaning breaks down.","Common mistakes: counting volume as breadth, so a pile of books in one genre feels like wide reading; reading only comfort books, or giving up on a stretch book at the first hard word; thinking a memoir is fiction because it tells a story in the first person; and thinking older books, such as Frankenstein, began as films. Be careful not to treat the tricky-word count as a strict rule. Interest and background knowledge matter too.","In the lab you drag six opening lines onto their genres. Then you add books to a reading log that holds eight fantasy books until it covers five kinds. Next you check three pages for tricky words and sort them into comfort, stretch and not yet. After that you put four texts in their century and set pages per day to finish a 240-page novel in two weeks."]},"familyTip":"Write down the last ten things you read, then label each one with its genre or form and roughly when it was written. If most labels are the same, pick your next book from a gap: a poem collection, a play, a memoir, or a novel from before 1914.","keyIdea":"Reading widely means reading many kinds of text, not just many books: different genres (mystery, science fiction, historical fiction, fantasy), different forms (novels, short stories, poems, plays, non-fiction such as memoir), and books from different periods and cultures. Mix comfort reads with stretch reads that have a few words you must work at, and build the stamina to finish whole books with a little reading every day.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which genre does each clue belong to? Drag each card into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Science fiction","Historical fiction"],"items":[["a colony on Mars in 2350",0],["a robot that learns to lie",0],["a time machine",0],["London during the Great Fire of 1666",1],["a soldier's letters home in 1916",1],["a Victorian factory child",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagwide","caption":"Flagship lab: name genres from first lines, widen a reading log, sort comfort and stretch pages, date four texts and plan your reading stamina.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_jIcgbCmziD":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"“Yo! School disco Friday 🎉 Wear something sparkly!!” Who is the audience?","choices":["Pupils at the school","Parents and governors","Readers of a national newspaper"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the register: how chatty is it?","explain":"“Yo!”, the emoji and “!!” are informal and chatty, which suits pupils. A letter to parents would be formal."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"“Dear Parents and Carers, the Year 8 trip is on Friday. Please return the consent form by Monday.” Who is the audience?","choices":["Parents and carers","Year 8 pupils","Museum staff"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who does the letter speak to in its first words?","explain":"The letter is about Year 8, but “Dear Parents and Carers” names the readers. The topic and the audience are different things."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which feature is a convention of a science report rather than a newspaper?","choices":["Stating the sample size, such as n = 120","A headline in capitals","The word “shock”"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one helps an expert judge the evidence?","explain":"A sample size lets experts judge how reliable a result is. Capitals and shock words are headline conventions for a general reader."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A 1912 newspaper calls the Titanic “practically unsinkable”. What is the best way to read this?","choices":["In its context: it was written before the ship sank","As a lie, because we know it sank","As a joke"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did the writer and readers know on that date?","explain":"Before 15 April 1912 many people believed the ship was very safe. Reading in context means judging by what was known then."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A writer turns a science fact into a picture book for 6-year-olds. What should change?","choices":["The words and tone, not the fact","The fact, to make it simpler but untrue","Nothing at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a fact be explained simply and still be true?","explain":"Register and vocabulary change with the audience: “little bits of plastic” instead of “microplastic particles”. The fact stays true."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"“SHOCK AS TINY PLASTIC FOUND IN 94% OF SAMPLES”. Why the capitals and “SHOCK”?","choices":["To grab a general reader's attention","Because scientists need capitals","To give more exact data"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where would you see a line like this, and who is it trying to catch?","explain":"Capitals and shock words are headline conventions to catch a general reader's eye. The exact data is the 94%."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A text was published in {y}. How many years ago was that from 2026?","answer":"2026-y","hint":"Subtract the year it was written from 2026.","explain":"2026 − {y} = {=2026-y} years. Knowing how old a text is helps you think about its context: what people knew and believed then.","params":{"y":[1850,1990]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"“Please do not touch the exhibits. Flash photography is not permitted.” What is the primary purpose?","choices":["To instruct visitors","To entertain visitors","To persuade people to visit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the sign telling you something to do or not do?","explain":"Imperatives such as “do not touch” instruct the reader. The formal register suits a public sign."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A leaflet has {f} sentences of facts and 1 sentence asking you to donate. How many sentences does it have in total?","answer":"f+1","hint":"Add the fact sentences and the one call to action.","explain":"{f} + 1 = {=f+1} sentences. Even with only 1 call to action among {=f+1} sentences, persuading is the primary purpose: the facts lead up to it.","params":{"f":[3,8]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"“Isotopic evidence indicates an asteroid impact 66 million years ago.” Who is the most likely audience?","choices":["Scientists or experts","Young children, because it is about dinosaurs","Teen readers of a gaming site"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the words, not the topic. Would a child know “isotopic”?","explain":"“Isotopic evidence” and “indicates” are technical, careful words for experts. The topic alone does not decide the audience."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A report tested {n} samples and found microplastics in all but {k} of them. How many samples contained microplastics?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the samples without microplastics away from the total.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} samples. A report gives exact numbers like this; a headline might only say “almost every sample”.","params":{"n":[100,140],"k":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A leaflet lists facts about plastic, then ends: “Give £5 today.” What is its primary purpose?","choices":["To persuade readers to donate","To inform, because it is mostly facts","To entertain"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does the writer want the reader to do at the end?","explain":"The facts build up to a call to action. Informing is a secondary purpose; persuading is the primary one."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A newspaper wrote about the Titanic on {d} April 1912. The ship sank on 15 April. How many days before the sinking was it written?","answer":"15-d","hint":"Take the date of writing away from 15.","explain":"15 − {d} = {=15-d} days. The writer could not know what would happen, which is part of the text's context.","params":{"d":[1,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why might a scientist write “the results suggest” instead of “the results prove”?","choices":["To say exactly how far the evidence goes","Because they do not understand the topic","To make it more exciting"],"answer":0,"hint":"Scientists are careful not to claim more than their data shows.","explain":"This is hedging, a convention of scientific writing. It signals care about evidence, not ignorance."}],"explain":{"kid":["Every text is written for someone, for a reason, at a time. Purpose is what the writer wants you to think or do. Audience is who it is for. Context is when, where and why it was made.","One fact can be written in many ways. A report says microplastics were found in 94% of samples. A paper shouts PLASTIC PERIL! A book for small children says little bits of plastic get into the sea.","To find the audience, look at the words, not the topic. Reports use exact numbers and careful words like suggest. Headlines use capitals and shock words. Books for small children use short, easy sentences.","A text can have two purposes. A charity leaflet gives facts, but its main aim is to get you to give money. Context counts too. In 1912, a paper called the Titanic unsinkable. It was written before the ship sank."],"adult":["Ages 11 to 14. National Curriculum in England, Key Stage 3 English: pupils read critically, knowing how language, vocabulary choice, grammar and text structure present meaning, and they study texts from different times. In the US Common Core, RI.6.6 to RI.8.6 ask students to find an author's purpose and point of view and how it shapes the text.","Purpose, audience and context work together. The audience sets the register (formal or informal), the vocabulary (technical or everyday) and the conventions of the form. The purpose explains what the text asks the reader to do. The context, such as the date, place and publisher, explains what the writer and first readers knew and believed.","Common mistakes: judging the audience by the topic instead of the language (a text about dinosaurs is not always for children); thinking a text full of facts must only inform, when the facts may serve a call to action; reading hedging words such as suggest as weakness rather than scientific care; and judging an old text by what we know now instead of its context.","In the lab your teenager matches three versions of one microplastics fact to their readers, taps five conventions of a science report, rewrites the fact for a picture book and a journal, finds the line that reveals a charity leaflet's primary purpose, places a 1912 Titanic clipping on a timeline, and names the purpose, audience and context of a school letter."]},"familyTip":"Pick one news story and find it in two places, such as a tabloid front page and a science website or a children's news site. Ask: who is each one for, and which words show it? What does each writer want you to feel or do?","keyIdea":"Before you judge a text, ask three questions. Purpose: what does the writer want the reader to think or do? Audience: who is it written for? Context: when, where and why was it written? The answers explain the writer's choices of register, tone, vocabulary and structure. The same fact is written differently for scientists, newspaper readers and young children.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Who is each sentence written for? Drag each card to its most likely reader, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Scientists","General public","Young children"],"items":[["Samples were analysed using infrared spectroscopy.",0],["The data suggest, but do not prove, a causal link.",0],["SCANDAL! Beach left buried in plastic",1],["Experts warn that our seas are in trouble.",1],["Can you spot the turtle? It is hiding!",2],["Plastic is not food for fish.",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpac","caption":"Flagship lab: match one fact to three readers, tap report conventions, rewrite it for two readers, find a primary purpose and read a 1912 text in context.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_gMSFymQlrW":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Claim: the park needs more bins. Which evidence is relevant?","choices":["A litter count found 200 items in one week","The park has a pond","The mayor likes ducks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is about litter?","explain":"The litter count is about the problem bins would fix. The pond and the mayor are beside the point."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"“My gran drank coffee every day and lived to 100, so coffee is good for you.” How strong is this?","choices":["Weak: it is one case","Strong: it really happened","Strong: 100 is very old"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many people does the evidence cover?","explain":"One person is a single case. It cannot outweigh studies of thousands of people."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"“Either we cancel sports day or the exams will be ruined.” What is wrong with this?","choices":["It gives only two options","It uses a fact","It is too polite"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are there really only two choices?","explain":"It is a false choice. There are other options, like moving sports day to a different week."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which makes an argument more convincing?","choices":["Relevant evidence that covers many cases","Lots of exclamation marks","A famous person agreeing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the scale in the lab weighed.","explain":"Relevant, sufficient evidence makes a claim convincing. Tone and famous fans add no evidence."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A survey asked {a} pupils and {b} of them wanted longer lunch. How many did not?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the yes answers away from the total asked.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} pupils did not. A fair writer reports both numbers, not just the ones that help.","params":{"a":[40,60],"b":[20,35]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"“Only selfish people disagree with the new rules.” Which trick is this?","choices":["Attacking the person","Slippery slope","False choice"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it about the rules or about the people?","explain":"It insults the people who disagree instead of answering their reasons. That is attacking the person."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Side A has evidence weighing {a}. Side B has evidence weighing {b} and uses 3 tricks. By how much does A's evidence outweigh B's?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Subtract B's weight from A's. Tricks add no weight.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}. The tricks do not count as evidence, so A is more convincing.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Two letters argue about a new skate park. How should you decide which is more convincing?","choices":["Compare the relevance and amount of evidence and the reasoning","Pick the angrier letter","Pick the one more people signed"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did the lab weigh on the scales?","explain":"Weigh each side's relevant evidence and check for tricks. Anger and numbers of signatures are not evidence."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A writer argues that the town needs a new library. Which sentence is the main claim?","choices":["The town should build a new library.","The old library opened in 1960.","Do you love reading?"],"answer":0,"hint":"The claim is the point the whole text wants you to accept.","explain":"The claim is that the town should build a library. The date is background and the question is a hook."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Evidence weights: strong = 3, some = 2, weak = 1. An argument has {s} strong pieces and {w} weak ones. What is its total weight?","answer":"3*s+w","hint":"Multiply the strong pieces by 3, then add the weak ones.","explain":"3 × {s} + {w} = {=3*s+w}. Strong, measured evidence adds far more than weak single stories.","params":{"s":[1,4],"w":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"An article gives {n} pieces of evidence. {k} of them are true but not about the claim. How many are relevant?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take away the pieces that are beside the point.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} relevant pieces. True but off-topic facts add nothing to the argument.","params":{"n":[6,10],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"“If we let pupils use phones at lunch, soon they will use them in every lesson.” Which trick is this?","choices":["Slippery slope","Bandwagon","Attacking the person"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it claim one step will lead to an extreme?","explain":"It claims one small step leads to an extreme with no evidence. That is a slippery slope."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A report saying fizzy drinks are healthy was paid for by a drinks company. What should you do?","choices":["Check its evidence closely","Believe it, they are experts","Ignore it, it must be false"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bias is a reason to check, not a verdict.","explain":"The company gains if you believe it, so look hard at its evidence. Bias does not prove it wrong."}],"explain":{"kid":["An argument tries to make you accept a claim. The claim is the main point, like: close Mill Road to cars at school times. Everything else in the text should support it.","First check the evidence is relevant. A fact can be true but beside the point. 'The road was paved in 1952' tells you nothing about safety now.","Then check there is enough. One story about a near miss is weak, because it is a single case. A trial at 12 schools is strong, because it covers many cases and was measured. Good arguments put several pieces together.","Watch for tricks that sound like reasons. Attacking the person, giving only two choices, saying everyone agrees, and claiming one step leads to disaster all skip the evidence. Then ask who wrote it. Bias does not make a writer wrong, but it tells you to check their facts more closely."],"adult":["Ages 11 to 14. The KS3 English programme of study asks pupils to read critically, to see how writers make and support claims, and to tell fact from opinion. US standards (RI.6.8 to RI.8.8) add judging whether reasoning is sound and evidence is relevant and sufficient.","A useful order is claim, evidence, reasoning, source. Relevance asks whether the evidence is about the claim. Sufficiency asks whether there is enough of it, based on how many cases it covers and how carefully it was measured. Common fallacies at this age are ad hominem (attacking the person), false dilemma (only two options), bandwagon (popularity as proof) and slippery slope (one step leads to an extreme).","Common mistakes: judging an argument by how confident or emotional it sounds; treating any true fact as support; letting one vivid anecdote outweigh a large study; dismissing a biased source outright rather than checking it; and counting how many people agree rather than weighing reasons.","In the lab a sample editorial and reply letter, written for the lab, are tested in six steps. The learner finds the claim and sorts five evidence cards onto a scale or into a bin. They rate the relevant ones strong, some or weak until the scale passes enough. Then they name four tricks in the letter, sort a biased writer's lines into fact and opinion, and judge which side is more convincing and why."]},"familyTip":"Pick a letter page, an advert or a speech clip. Ask three questions together: what do they want us to believe, what is their evidence, and is there a trick in there? Take turns finding the weakest sentence. Then argue the other side and see whose evidence weighs more.","keyIdea":"To evaluate an argument, find the main claim, then test the support. Is the evidence relevant to the claim, and is there enough of it? Is the reasoning sound, or does it lean on tricks like attacking the person? Check who is writing, then decide which side is more convincing and say why.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Claim: our school should start at 9:30. Is each line evidence, a reasoning trick, or not relevant? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Relevant evidence","Reasoning trick","Not relevant"],"items":[["Pupils at two schools that started later slept 34 minutes more on average",0],["Anyone who wants an 8:30 start just hates teenagers",1],["The school was built in 1974",2],["Everyone in my class agrees, so it must be right",1],["Attendance rose when one trial school moved its start later",0],["The head teacher drives a blue car",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagweigh","caption":"Flagship lab: find the claim, drag evidence onto a scale or into the bin, weigh it, name four tricks, read a biased source and judge two sides.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Pns7r9fFS6":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is pathos?","choices":["An appeal to the audience's emotions","An appeal to facts and logic","An appeal to the speaker's credibility"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the word sympathy, which shares the same Greek root.","explain":"Pathos appeals to feeling: pity, anger, hope or fear. Ethos is credibility and logos is reasoning."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"\"Seven in ten of our dogs arrive underweight.\" Which appeal is this mainly using?","choices":["Logos","Pathos","Ethos"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it giving a fact to reason with, a feeling, or a reason to trust the speaker?","explain":"It is a statistic used as evidence, so the main appeal is logos, even though the fact is sad."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which claim builds the strongest ethos for a dog shelter?","choices":["\"As the shelter vet for 15 years, I have treated over 2,000 dogs.\"","\"Trust me, I know what I'm talking about.\"","\"Everyone in town agrees with us.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one gives a checkable, relevant reason to trust the speaker?","explain":"Relevant, specific expertise earns trust. 'Trust me' asserts it without a reason, and 'everyone agrees' is an appeal to popularity."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"\"You can change that today.\" Which technique is this?","choices":["Direct address","Statistic","Anecdote"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who is the sentence speaking to?","explain":"It speaks straight to the reader as 'you', which makes them feel responsible for acting."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Who is a short story about one shy dog finding a home most likely to persuade?","choices":["A primary school assembly","A council budget committee","An auditor checking the accounts"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which audience responds most to one concrete, emotional story?","explain":"Young children connect with one dog's story. The committee and the auditor need figures and evidence."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which sentence analyses the effect of a technique best?","choices":["The anecdote about Bella makes the problem personal, so parents picture one real dog, not a number","The writer uses an anecdote","The writer uses lots of persuasive techniques"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good analysis names the technique, the effect and the audience.","explain":"It names the technique, explains the effect and links it to a specific audience. The others only spot or list."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A cake sale raised £{=5*w}. Food costs £5 per dog per week. How many dog-weeks of food is that?","answer":"w","hint":"Divide the money raised by £5.","explain":"£{=5*w} ÷ £5 = {w}. A local, costed figure is stronger logos than a vague claim like 'millions of dogs'.","params":{"w":[20,60]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"\"If you do not give, every dog's suffering is on you.\" Why might this backfire with parents?","choices":["Guilt-tripping can make the audience resent the writer","It uses too many statistics","It is an example of ethos"],"answer":0,"hint":"How do people feel when they are blamed?","explain":"Heavy guilt is pathos pushed too far. Readers who feel blamed may stop trusting the writer, which damages ethos too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"\"Who among us has not loved a dog?\" What is the effect of this rhetorical question?","choices":["It makes listeners agree in their heads before the argument starts","It asks the audience to reply out loud","It gives evidence for the claim"],"answer":0,"hint":"A rhetorical question is not asked to get an answer.","explain":"The expected answer is 'no one', so listeners silently agree and feel on the speaker's side."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"The shelter tests two leaflets on {=10*m} parents. {k} in 10 say the story about Bella moved them most. How many parents is that?","answer":"k*m","hint":"Find one tenth of {=10*m}, then multiply by {k}.","explain":"{=10*m} ÷ 10 = {m}, and {m} × {k} = {=k*m} parents. Testing on the real audience is how you judge which appeal works best.","params":{"k":[4,8],"m":[3,12]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"\"As a firefighter for 18 years, I can tell you smoke alarms save lives.\" Which appeal is this mainly using?","choices":["Ethos","Pathos","Logos"],"answer":0,"hint":"What reason does the writer give you to believe them?","explain":"The 18 years as a firefighter is relevant expertise. It makes you trust the speaker, so it is ethos."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An appeal says £5 feeds one dog for a week. How many dog-weeks of food does a £{=5*w} donation buy?","answer":"w","hint":"Divide the donation by £5, the cost of one dog-week.","explain":"£{=5*w} ÷ £5 = {w} dog-weeks. Turning money into a concrete result is a logos move.","params":{"w":[3,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"\"We feed them, we warm them, we find them homes.\" Which technique is this?","choices":["Triad","Anecdote","Rhetorical question"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the parallel phrases.","explain":"Three parallel phrases make a triad. The pattern sounds complete and is easy to remember."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A speech to a council budget committee opens with a sad story and gives no figures. What is the main problem?","choices":["The appeal does not suit an audience that must justify spending","Stories never persuade anyone","Speeches should never use emotion"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what this audience has to do after the speech.","explain":"Pathos can persuade, but a committee must defend its spending, so it needs logos: costs and evidence."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A leaflet says {k} in 10 rescued dogs arrive underweight. Out of {=10*m} dogs, how many is that?","answer":"k*m","hint":"Find one tenth of {=10*m}, then multiply by {k}.","explain":"{=10*m} ÷ 10 = {m}, and {m} × {k} = {=k*m} dogs. A clear statistic gives the reader something to reason with.","params":{"k":[3,7],"m":[4,20]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Persuasive texts work in three ways. Ethos makes you trust the speaker. Pathos makes you feel something. Logos gives you reasons and facts. Aristotle named these over two thousand years ago, and adverts still use them.","Ethos must be earned. A vet who has run a shelter for 15 years has real expertise. Saying trust me gives no reason at all. A famous fan has fame, not knowledge.","Writers use techniques to make each appeal work. A rhetorical question makes you answer in your head. An anecdote is a short true story that makes you care. A statistic adds weight. A triad groups ideas in threes so they stick. Direct address says you, so the reader feels involved.","The best appeal depends on the audience. A council deciding a budget wants costed figures. Young children respond to a story about one dog. Dog owners trust people like them. When you analyse, say who the text is aimed at and why the appeal works on them."],"adult":["Ages 14 to 16. GCSE English Language asks students to analyse how writers use language and structure to influence readers. It also asks them to judge texts with evidence. Spoken language work adds rhetoric in speeches. Key terms: rhetoric, ethos, pathos, logos, appeal, audience, purpose, rhetorical question, anecdote, statistic, triad (rule of three), direct address.","Common mistakes: calling any sad sentence pathos, even when it is a statistic used as evidence; treating any number as logos, even when it shows experience (15 years as a vet is ethos); spotting a technique but not explaining its effect; and writing about 'the reader' instead of a specific audience. Strong answers also judge which appeal works best for that audience, and why.","Appeals often overlap. A charity figure can move you and inform you at once. Credit students who argue for the main appeal with a reason, rather than looking for one right label.","In the lab students sort six sentences from a fictional dog-shelter appeal. They spot five techniques in a short speech and match three openers to a council committee, a primary assembly and local dog owners. They test four credibility claims on a meter and build a paragraph for parents, choosing the line in each pair that keeps the audience's trust."]},"familyTip":"Next time a charity advert or a political leaflet comes through the door, read it together. Highlight trust in one colour, feelings in another and facts in a third. Then ask: who is this aimed at, and which colour would work best on them?","keyIdea":"Persuasive writers and speakers use three appeals. Ethos makes you trust the speaker, through relevant expertise or a shared identity. Pathos makes you feel something, often through an anecdote or a vivid image. Logos gives you reasons and evidence, such as statistics and costed facts. Techniques like rhetorical questions, triads and direct address carry these appeals. To analyse a text, name the appeal, quote the technique, explain its effect on a specific audience, and judge whether it fits that audience.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"A road-safety campaign: which appeal does each line use most? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Ethos: trust the speaker","Pathos: feel something","Logos: reason with evidence"],"items":[["As a paramedic for 20 years, I have seen what speeding does",0],["Our road safety charity has worked with schools since 1998",0],["Picture a mother waiting by the phone for news",1],["One second of distraction. One child. One life changed",1],["A London study found 20 mph zones cut casualties by about 40%",2],["At 30 mph a car needs about 23 metres to stop",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrhet","caption":"Flagship lab: sort appeals, spot five techniques, match openers to audiences, test credibility and build the strongest paragraph.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
